  "output": "{\n  \"content\": \"Advanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\nAvailable online 18 April 2024\\n1474-0346/© 2024 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.Full length article \\nCyber-Physical Internet (CPI)-enabled logistics infrastructure integration \\nframework in the greater bay area \\nHang Wua, Linhao Huangb, Ming Lia,c,*, George Q. Huanga,c \\naDepartment of Industrial and Systems Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China \\nbSchool of Management, Jinan University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China \\ncResearch Institute for Advanced Manufacturing, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China   \\nARTICLE INFO  \\nKeywords: \\nCyber-physical internet \\nInfrastructure integration \\nRouting \\nTransportation ABSTRACT  \\nThe emerging digital economy has facilitated the digital transformation of the logistics industry toward a \\nstandard and collaborative state. The logistics infrastructure has accordingly attracted much attention as the \\nbasis for supporting digital economy implementation. Moreover, the initiatives of regional collaborative \\ndevelopment, such as in the Greater Bay Area (GBA) of China, have accelerated the integration of logistics \\ninfrastructure and operation-level collaboration. Fortunately, the Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) has created new \\nopportunities for promoting infrastructure integration through the cyber, physical and internet dimensions. \\nHowever, regional infrastructure integration in the GBA faces various challenges associated with CPI network \\noperations. Thus, a bottom-up approach is proposed to facilitate logistics infrastructure integration in the CPI \\nenvironment based on the conceptual fusion of computer networks. Considering the fundamental difference in \\nnetwork fusion and operation mechanisms between CPI and computer networks, a CPI-LI2 (logistics infra-\\nstructure integration) framework is designed as an integrated solution. Finally, a simulation study is conducted \\nusing real-life medical logistics data from the GBA to verify the effectiveness of the CPI-LI2 framework. Exper -\\niments show that the framework is well adapted to large-scale logistics networks with dynamically changing \\nlinks.   \\n1.Introduction \\nA wave of digitalization is sweeping through all industries and the \\nera of digital economy is coming with the well-digitalized infrastructure. \\nThe logistics industry has embraced this wave to construct smart logis-\\ntics infrastructure. In particular, the emerging Cyber-Physical Internet \\n(CPI) provides a logistics paradigm with an integrated logistics infra-\\nstructure that enables synergistic regional development. As a global \\neconomic centre, the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area \\nhas experienced changes in the manufacturing industry with the rapid \\ndevelopment of e-commerce, especially cross-border e-commerce, \\nwhich has greatly contributed to the growth of logistics demand. \\nAlthough the Greater Bay Area has a well-established logistics infra-\\nstructure, it is still unable to meet the fast-growing logistics demand. In \\naddition, the insufficient supply of logistics land restricts the construc -\\ntion of new logistics infrastructure. The National 14th 5-Year Plan and \\nthe Outline Development Plan for the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao \\nGreater Bay Area noted that logistics infrastructure integration is one of the most important processes of the integrative construction of the GBA. \\nThis suggested that logistics infrastructure integration will be a new \\ntrend after the saturation of logistics infrastructure. CPI can facilitate \\ninfrastructure resource sharing and load balancing through information \\nflows configured at the cyber level [1], thus building infrastructure- \\nintegrated logistics networks. \\nAs the foundation of CPI, Physical Internet (PI) establishes an open \\ninterconnected logistics network based on shareable physical infra-\\nstructure (e.g. PI containers, PI facilities, etc.), consensual protocols and \\ndigital interoperability [2]. The exploitation of cyberspace symbolized \\nthe evolution of PI into a new generation of CPI. CPI strengthens the \\nsynthetic use of cyber capacity to improve the operation and execution \\nof PI. The locations and states of PI containers in the physical world are \\ncollected, communicated, and synchronized in real time, and the flows \\nof PI containers are configured, supervised, and manipulated through \\nthe flows of information in cyberspace. \\nThe theoretical studies on CPI can be divided into PI-level research \\nand cyber-physical-level (CP-level) research. Most of the existing CPI \\n*Corresponding author at: Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China. \\nE-mail address: ming.li@polyu.edu.hk (M. Li).  \\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics \\nu{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2kot!\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.aei.2024.102551 \\nReceived 28 June 2023; Received in revised form 19 March 2024; Accepted 10 April 2024   \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n2research has been at the PI level and has mainly been conducted in \\nEurope. Preliminary results have been achieved in research on PI, with a \\nfocus on the PI backbone network, such as the standardization of PI \\nnodes [3,4], the mechanisms and optimizations of the operations and \\nservices of logistics networks [5,6], and the expansion and maintenance \\nof PI logistics networks [7]. Over the past few years, many scholars have \\ncombined and enhanced information and communication technologies \\nwith applications in CP-level research (e.g., cloud computing and digital \\ntwins), such as in cutting-edge applications of CPS in the logistics in-\\ndustry [8,9], the cyber layer optimization of CPI [10], and the data \\nsharing or interoperability services of CPI [11]. However, the integra -\\ntion of CPI infrastructures has some unresolved operational issues, \\nsummarized below.  \\n(1) The existing research has been generally conducted in a top-down \\nmanner, from conceptualization to proof-of-concept to program \\nimplementation, for the overall control and management of CPI \\nnetworks. These studies typically depend on the introduction of \\nnew infrastructure rather than enhancing the use of existing \\ninfrastructure. This paper adopts a bottom-up perspective to fuse \\ninfrastructure and establish an integrated logistics infrastructure \\nnetwork based on the existing logistics infrastructure in the Great \\nBay Area.  \\n(2) Most studies have designed the operational mechanisms of large- \\nscale networks from the perspective of operation research using \\ncombinational optimization methods. However, these methods \\ncannot provide solutions in a limited time for large-scale network \\nintegration problems for the reason of the large number of nodes \\nand connections in the network. It faces more uncertainties and \\ndisturbances during problem abstraction and mathematical \\nmodeling than do small networks. The huge size of the solution \\nspace makes it more difficult to perform global optimization in \\nlarge-scale networks. Computer networks can handle massive sets \\nof real-time data with a simple routing process. Introducing the \\nconcept of computer networks may enhance the real-time per-\\nformance of integrated infrastructure networks. \\nSince computer networks have faced similar integration problems, \\nthis paper explores a feasible migration approach for general conceptual \\nmethodologies to CPI by addressing the two research questions:  \\n(1) How can the general features of computer network fusion be \\nmigrated to CPI? There is a fundamental difference between a \\ncompletely virtual computer environment and a CPI-based vir-\\ntual-real environment. Some factors hinder infrastructure inte-\\ngration in CPI, such as the connectivity among infrastructures. \\nThus, the infrastructure integration method for computer net-\\nworks cannot be applied directly to CPI infrastructure networks. \\n(2)How can a novel routing mechanism be designed for the inte-\\ngrated CPI with the motivation of data package routing in com-\\nputer networks? Path evaluation is an initial step in routing. The \\ntypes and characteristics of paths are quite different between CPI \\nand computer networks. Therefore, an innovative path evalua -\\ntion mechanism for CPI should be investigated, and specific \\nrouting mechanisms should be designed to enable flexible PI \\nrouting in CPI. \\nTo address the above problems, a CPI-LI2 (logistics infrastructure \\nintegration) framework with a two-layer structure is proposed for \\nestablishing the integrated infrastructure network. The framework in-\\ncludes a link layer and a transport layer. For the infrastructure fusion at \\nthe CPI link layer, a logistics elemental area fusion (LEAF) approach is \\nproposed in accordance with the openness and synergy of the infra-\\nstructure to establish connections between different areas by electing \\nCPI core autonomous areas and regional link centers. For the link fusion \\nat the CPI transport layer, Distance Vector Master Paths (DVMP) and Link State Branch Paths (LSBP) are proposed with the CPI routing \\nmechanism. Finally, a case study is conducted for the medical logistics \\nnetwork in the Greater Bay Area, where the effectiveness of the proposed \\nframework and the advantages of the proposed routing mechanism are \\nexperimentally verified. \\nThe rest of this study is organized as follows. The current situation of \\nlogistics infrastructure and the preliminary studies on CPI are investi -\\ngated in section 2. In section 3, the CPI-LI2 framework and involved \\nservices are introduced. In sections 4 and 5, the fusion mechanisms for \\nthe link layer and transport layer are presented, respectively. Section 6 \\nincludes a case study to apply the CPI-LI2 framework to the medical \\nlogistics network in the GBA. Section 7 concludes the works with the \\nmajor contributions of this paper and provides future research topics. \\n2.Literature review \\na) PI theory: The term ‘Physical Internet’ first appeared in The \\nEconomist in 2006 [12] as a novel concept for building large-scale \\ncomplex logistics and transportation networks in the physical world. \\nSubsequently, Ballot et al. explored the potential of Physical Internet \\nand presented the topology of the PI logistics network [13]. Montreuil \\net al. perceived PI as a multilevel network collaboration model based on \\nglobal supply chains and used it to cope with sustainable demand in \\nsupply chains [14]. Then, they developed the foundation of PI [2]. They \\ndefined PI as an open global logistics system based on the encapsulation \\nand digital and operational interconnection of physical, interfaces and \\nprotocols, creating an evolving system driven by technology, infra-\\nstructure, and business innovation. For more than a decade, PI has been \\nstudied more and more comprehensively and evolves toward CPI. Zhong \\net al. noted that PI has become a new logistics paradigm with the inte-\\ngration of cloud computing and big data analysis [15]. Leung et al. \\ndeveloped the application of digital twins to PI to enhance the visibility \\nand traceability of logistics processes [16]. \\nb) Logistics infrastructure in PI: The design of logistics infrastruc -\\nture is a popular and significant topic in PI studies, which can be divided \\ninto three streams: PI containers, PI nodes, and PI platforms. PI con-\\ntainers are the standard, modular, smart, and reusable containers that \\nare coordinated, handled and shipped in the PI network, as the most \\nimportant component of the PI. It was developed in the smartBOX \\nproject for its tracking and tracing capability [17], and was developed \\nfor its activeness in an information context [5]. Charpentier et al. \\ndeveloped the digital twin of PI containers which can be used to check its \\ncompleteness and find its arrangement and position in the loading and \\nunloading phases [18]. Gumzeji et al. designed a safe and secure intel-\\nligent PI container for air cargo of pharmaceuticals [19]. Montreuil et al. \\nintroduced various types of PI nodes [20] and subsequently designed \\nthree main types of PI nodes: the road-based cross-docking hub, the \\nroad-rail hub, and the road-based transit center [21,22,23]. They func-\\ntionally designed the facilities within the logistics infrastructures, \\nsimplifying container loading operations and reducing operating time, \\nenabling flexible transfer of containers. Following their design, subse -\\nquent studies focused on consolidation strategies in the PI node, such as \\ntruck scheduling [24,25] and container grouping and loading \\n[26,27,28], and inbound container forecasting [29]. Although many \\nstudies have made contributions to the construction of data models and \\nparticular technology to share data, the development of PI platforms is \\nrelatively limited and preliminary due to the difficulty of building an \\nopen and federated platform [30]. However, Windolph et al. suggested \\nbuilding a PI platform using a common architecture for intelligent ser-\\nvice platforms as a cornerstone for future development [31]. \\nc) Routing in PI: Routing problem is always given great attention in \\nlogistics-related studies. In this field, the most common way to solve \\nrouting problems is to construct a VRP model. In the PI network, the \\nproblem is various. Pan et al. solve the intercity routing problem \\nconsidering the fragmentation of supplier-customer connections [32]. \\nKantasa-ard et al. looked for routes to reduce empty vehicle trips and H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n3logistics costs in a dynamic logistics network with multiple depots [33]. \\nAlso, previous studies have drawn some findings. Yoon et al. indicated \\nthat increasing the on-demand mobility of PI containers would reduce \\nthe uncertainty of dynamic routing [34]. Sarraj et al. also showed that \\nasynchronous shipment and creation of containers and merging \\ncontainer flows could improve the efficiency of multiple aspects [35]. \\nGontara et al. suggested the exclusive routing of PI containers following \\nthe PI-BGP protocol, which can guarantee a high speed of PI container \\nflows in intercity areas with a reduction of delay and storing [36]. \\nd) CPI framework: The layered framework including cyber and \\nphysical layers has also been proposed in previous studies, mainly \\ndescribing the interaction between different layers and different func-\\ntions. Lee et al. built a 5C framework for cyber-physical systems (CPS) \\nand clarified the key role of digital interoperability in establishing an \\ninterconnection between the cyber layer and the physical layer [37]. \\nBased on this framework, some research developed the application of \\nCPS in the logistics industry. Zhang et al. built a smart logistics path \\ndecision system that is composed of the physical layer, the cloud \\nnetwork layer, and the application layer with intelligent algorithms for \\npath optimization. The interconnection of nodes is attributed to the \\nphysical layer. The data transmission and resource sharing based on \\ninterconnection and interoperability of equipment is enabled by the \\ncloud network layer. [38]. Lian et al. proposed a CPS-based smart lo-\\ngistics system for intralogistics to control multiple automatic guided \\nvehicles (AGV). It also includes three layers terms of physical perception \\nlayer, the network transmission layer, and the control layer. In the \\nscenarios of this study, the physical layer contains all the entities of the \\nlogistics warehouse. The network transmission layer connects the cyber \\nand physical world to reliably and securely transmit data while the \\ncontrol layer is responsible for controlling and making decisions for \\nvarious tasks [39]. \\nWhen it comes to PI, Montreuil et al. initially proposed an open \\ninterconnection model for PI based on the OSI model in computer net-\\nworks [40]. On this basis, a new model that is closer to the OSI model \\nwas presented by Colin et al. [41]. Based on the prior studies on the PI \\nframework, Kaup et al. subsequently made transformations for protocols \\nadopted in computer networks and applied them to PI [42]. However, \\nthe capabilities of cyberspace are not fully indicated in their framework, \\nwhile some subsequent studies explore the capabilities enabled by cy-\\nberspace and develop specific PI frameworks. Fahim et al. proposed a \\nframework based on DSR theory for vehicle routing in PI ports, which \\nachieved the interactions and connections between the cyber layer and \\nvarious physical modules in the PI hub [43]. Pal et al. proposed a PI \\nframework for fresh food logistics to reinforce collaboration and infor-\\nmation sharing among different PI infrastructures [44]. Cerabona et al. \\nfocused on improving PI intelligence in decision-making in an unstable \\nenvironment. They established a PI atomic framework to enhance the \\nresilience and robustness of the airline supply chain [45]. Moreover, \\nMededjel et al. constructed a cloud framework to enhance the network \\nedge capacity, which displayed good performance for predicting dis-\\nruptions in logistics processes [46]. \\ne) Conclusion: In the context of synergistic development of cities in \\nthe GBA, logistics, as one of the industrial pillars of the GBA, is also a key \\nlink. The synergistic effect generated through the integration of logistics \\nnetworks is one of the motivations for the development of logistics in the \\nGBA. Although research on PI has yielded some results in theory and \\npractice, there are still some issues that deserve further exploration. \\nFirstly, although smart logistics infrastructures have developed \\ntremendously, the degree of interconnectivity among logistics networks \\nbelonging to different organizations is limited. This is due to the lack of \\ninformation flow among logistics companies and little interoperability \\namong logistics networks. To address this challenge, this study exploits \\nthe natural interconnectivity at the cyber level to configure logistics \\nnetworks. The real-time data collected from the physical logistics \\nnetwork is used to construct the corresponding cyber logistics network, \\nand then the cyber and physical logistics networks are synergized to coordinate the actual logistics process using the information flow in \\ncyberspace. \\nSecondly, for large-scale logistics networks with high real-time re-\\nquirements, the conventional solution of using heuristic algorithms to \\nsolve NP-hard problems is not feasible. The vastness of the solution \\nspace makes global optimization in large-scale networks challenging. \\nThe computer network is also a large-scale network covering the globe, \\nwhere data packets are transmitted efficiently, showing that cyberspace \\nhas sufficient capacity to handle a large-scale network. Previous \\nresearch explored the application of a single routing protocol in PI \\nnetworks, but different routing protocols should be developed for \\ndifferent hierarchical levels of logistics networks since different logistics \\nnetworks have different characteristics and should be allowed to follow \\ntheir own rules within the logistics network. Therefore, this study em-\\nploys a combinatorial routing mechanism, which is used together with \\nrouting tables to efficiently perform routing in the logistics network. \\n3.General framework \\nIn this section, a CPI-LI2 framework with a two-layer structure is \\nproposed to enable logistics infrastructure integration in the GBA, as \\npresented in Fig. 1. This framework can also be extended to serve a \\nlarger-scale logistics network. The services are included in two layers, \\nthe CPI link layer and the CPI transport layer. The CPI link layer is \\nresponsible for dividing autonomous areas based on the distribution of \\nlogistics demand in reality and electing key autonomous areas and \\nregional link centers taking into account the level of logistics develop -\\nment in the areas. This division and election are to enable independent \\nmanagement and routing control. The CPI transport layer is responsible \\nfor making decisions about routing based on massive amounts of real- \\ntime data to respond instantly to link state changes, ensuring efficient, \\nreliable and sustainable shipping activities. In this framework, the CPI \\nlink layer completes the integration of the infrastructure at the physical \\nlevel and generates the links that act in cyberspace. The CPI transport \\nlayer is based on these links to determine the direction and mode of the \\nPI container and information flows in the logistics network. Both layers \\nwork together to achieve the integration and operation of the logistics \\nnetwork. \\n3.1. CPI link layer \\nThe services in the CPI link layer have two main roles, one is to \\ngenerate the links and the other is to maintain them. The data source \\nservice, the clustering service and the election service are used to sup-\\nport the generation of links, while the CPI generation service, the CPI \\nvisualization service, the CPI maintenance service, and the data ware-\\nhouse service are used to maintain the links so that they can operate \\nstably and recover quickly even in the event of disturbances. \\nThe data source service is responsible for extracting the required data \\nfrom a wide variety of data sources to initialize the links. The sources of \\ndata are mainly information systems and official websites. The service \\nprovides basic data and database middleware. Basic data is what sat-\\nisfies the needs of various generation tasks and constitutes the founda -\\ntion of the link layer. Database middleware is used to update data in real \\ntime and achieve efficient off-site data synchronization. In general, the \\ndata source service is achieved by web crawlers and downloading public \\nstatistics from official websites. \\nThe clustering service is responsible for dividing a large region into \\nseveral autonomous areas according to the distribution of logistics de-\\nmand, as shown in the second stage at the link layer of Fig. 1. The di-\\nvision of autonomous areas is intended to balance the pressure on \\nlogistics infrastructures and to improve the sustainability and expand -\\nability of logistics. Independent management and operation can avoid \\nthe failure of a small number of infrastructures leading to the paralysis of \\nthe logistics network in the entire region. \\nThe election service is responsible for electing a core autonomous H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n4area and a supporting autonomous area from all autonomous areas, \\nusually determined based on the level of local transport and logistics \\ndevelopment and other political and economic influences. On the one \\nhand, from the digital perspective, the core autonomous area is \\nresponsible for collecting and broadcasting link status information for \\nthe entire region, so that other autonomous areas can learn about \\nchanges in the entire network through it, without the need for each \\nautonomous area to exchange information with each other. On the other \\nhand, if the destination involves multiple autonomous areas in the re-\\ngion, goods from the external network could be transported through the \\ncore autonomous area to the other autonomous areas. The supporting \\nautonomous area is responsible for taking over the tasks of the core \\nautonomous area when the core autonomous area fails and provides \\ndisaster recovery support for it. Moreover, the election service will elect \\na regional link center in each autonomous area. In this way, considerable \\nduplicate information in the network can be filtered and removed, the \\ndatabase size reduced, and the loads on other infrastructure components \\nin the autonomous areas reduced as well. The election service plays an \\nimportant role in shortening the demand response cycle and improving \\nthe service quality of the CPI logistics network. \\nWhile the above CPI generation service drives the generation of \\nlinks, long-term stable links also depend on the following services. The \\nCPI visualization service provides visualization of the running status of \\nthe link layer and early warning for abnormalities. The adjacency rela-\\ntionship and link status are presented visually, which allows real-time \\nmonitoring, efficient problem diagnosis, and informed decision- \\nmaking at the CPI link layer (e.g., link quality and link abnormalities \\nand failures). \\nThe CPI maintenance service involves the maintenance of link layer \\ndata, link status maintenance, and the handling of anomaly problems. If \\na core autonomous area fails or is overloaded, the CPI maintenance service will update the supporting autonomous areas to share resources \\nand keep the link loads balanced. When the situation improves, the CPI \\nmaintenance service will re-establish the original scheme for the core \\nautonomous area. When the handover between the supporting autono -\\nmous area and the core autonomous area is completed, they will return \\nto their initial state. \\nThe CPI data warehouse service provides data extraction, validation, \\nstorage, and retrieval functions. The extraction function in the CPI data \\nwarehouse service is responsible for pulling the basic data and database \\nmiddleware from the data source service so that the link layer can \\nmanipulate data (e.g., link state data, autonomous area management \\nand maintenance data). The above data will be processed and stored in a \\nstructured form in the data warehouse to effectively support protocol \\noperations at the link layer. \\n3.2. CPI transport layer \\nThe CPI transport layer is responsible for making decisions about the \\ntransport process based on the links generated by the CPI link layer. The \\nservices at the CPI transport layer include the routing table generation \\nservice, the link state evaluation service, the routing process traceability \\nservice, and the routing process visualization services. \\nThe routing table generation service is responsible for the estab -\\nlishment of neighbor relationships between PI nodes and nodes directly \\nconnected to them, thus allowing information to be exchanged between \\nneighboring nodes, and ultimately generating an initial routing table for \\nthe entire network. An established neighbor relationship means that \\ngoods can be transported between PI nodes, and even if the two nodes \\nare not directly connected, they can be connected by a combination of \\nmultiple paths. \\nHowever, establishing neighbor relationships is only the first step. \\nFig. 1.CPI-LI2 infrastructure integration framework.  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n5Then, the link state evaluation service will process the link information. \\nFor paths with more than one link, the guiding price of the path is \\ncalculated based on the price of the link with the lowest gradient price. If \\nthat link runs out of capacity, the guiding price will subsequently be \\ndirected to the next link in the gradient price list. By cumulating the \\nguiding price of paths included in a route, the overhead of the route is \\nmeasured. Routes with the same destination will be compared, and the \\nroute with the lowest overhead will be shown in the routing table. In \\naddition, the link state evaluation service also checks the status of a \\npath, for example, if a path is inaccessible, the guiding price of the path \\nwill be set as infinity, so that the path will be not selected as the next \\nhop. \\nThe routing process traceability service performs real-time tracking \\nin the PI container transportation process through CPI infrastructure. \\nThe service is used to satisfy all kinds of management requirements for \\nthe CPI transportation process and build a whole process information \\nchain with human –machine-related information coupling. Specifically, \\nhumans involved are order initiators, receivers, and handlers. Machines \\ninvolve PI containers, robotic hands, identification readers, etc. Notably, \\nthe object to be traced for the transport process is the PI container. The \\ntype, quantity and quality of PI containers can be tracked in real time by \\nemploying sensing identification technologies. \\nThe routing process visualization service provides link-state visual -\\nization, PI node visualization, vehicle visualization and PI container \\nvisualization, etc. PI container visualization provides information about \\ncontainers (e.g., origin, destination, quantity, type, temperature, hu-\\nmidity) and some basic information about goods. Vehicle visualization \\nmainly reflects the real-time location of the vehicle that carrying con-\\ntainers. It also provides some information about vehicles, such as the \\nmaximum capacity and current utilization. Link-state visualization in-\\ndicates the current path use of the network. As shown in Fig. 1, blue lines \\nrepresent the paths that are selected as the next-hop path to transport \\ncontainer B. PI node visualization distinguishes different types of PI \\nnodes as shown in the transport layer of Fig. 1, with all the circles \\nrepresenting PI nodes, the circles marked with the letter E are edge \\nnodes, while the yellow circles are core nodes or auxiliary core nodes. E \\nNodes are PI nodes that provide inter-autonomous area paths, as \\nopposed to internal nodes which only provide intra-autonomous area \\npaths. Core nodes and auxiliary core nodes are the backbone nodes of \\nthe CPI logistics networks. They are responsible for link information \\nexchange across autonomous areas via edge nodes, offering high-speed \\nand efficient routing capabilities to the CPI logistics network. Such \\nnodes located in the core autonomous area are called core nodes and \\nthose located in other autonomous areas are auxiliary core nodes. Other \\nPI nodes can obtain the link information by accessing the backbone \\nnodes to support the routing process. \\n4.CPI link layer fusion mechanism \\nThe fusion of the CPI link layer follows three steps. First, pre- \\nprocessing data obtained from private information systems and official \\nwebsites through the data source service to form a usable data point set. \\nThen, clustering on these two-dimensional spatial data sets to divide the \\nregion into several autonomous areas. Finally, electing the core auton -\\nomous area based on the local logistics infrastructure development and \\nthe level of logistics services, and regional link centers can be identified \\nthrough a distance-weighted approach. \\nThe notations are defined in Table 1. \\n4.1. CPI autonomous area division \\nIn a real logistics network, the division of the region should consider \\nthe effects of many realistic factors, such as logistics demand density, \\nroad network density, infrastructure loading capacity and other factors. \\nThis requires access to data and information on various influencing \\nfactors through the data source services. Many clustering algorithms can support the generation of autono -\\nmous areas. The most appropriate clustering method is selected by \\nconsidering multiple assessment indicators and practical needs. The \\ncommonly used assessment metrics include the SSE (Sum of Squared \\nErrors) [47], silhouette coefficient [48] and CH (Calinski-Harabasz) \\nindex [49]. SSE obtained by the elbow method indicates the intra-cluster \\nvariability. Within a reasonable number of clusters, the SSE decreases as \\nthe number of clusters increases. The number of clusters corresponding \\nto the inflection point (elbow), i.e., the location where the rate of SSE \\ndecline slows down significantly, is taken as the optimal number of \\nclusters, since further increasing the number of clusters may not \\nsignificantly improve the quality of the clusters. The silhouette coeffi -\\ncient is used to measure the tightness and separation of the clustering \\nresults. It takes values between \\u00001 and 1, with closer to 1 indicating \\ngreater differences between clusters and better clustering. In addition, \\nthe CH index is an internal evaluation metric. It could assess the tight-\\nness and separation of the clusters by calculating the ratio of the vari-\\nance between clusters to the variance within clusters. A higher CH index \\nimplies the clustering results have better separation and tightness. \\nK refers to the appropriate number of clusters, i.e. the number of \\nautonomous areas to be divided in this study. The larger the K value is, \\nthe larger the number of autonomous areas and the more difficult the \\ninfrastructure integration and management. Conversely, the smaller the \\nK value is, the larger the database size and infrastructure load, the higher \\nthe performance requirements of the infrastructure and the lower the \\nsecurity of the network. Therefore, the determination of the number of \\nautonomous areas and the division of the region is important for an \\nintegrated infrastructure network. \\n4.2. Clustering and CPI region link center generation \\nThe operation process of the K-means clustering algorithm is shown \\nin Fig. 2. \\nAccording to the degree of customer demand for logistics, customers \\ncan be divided into three categories. The first category is demand- \\nintensive customers, such as some large e-commerce enterprises. \\nThese customers maintain a high level of logistics demand over a long \\nperiod, and this demand is diverse and variable. The second category of \\ncustomers has relatively stable logistics demand, such as some \\nmanufacturing enterprises, called demand-stable customers. The third \\ncategory is customers with more scattered demand, such as retail stores. \\nThe logistics needs of these customers are usually related to the volume \\nof their merchandise sales, are lower than those of the first two cate-\\ngories, and are less predictable. \\nIn practice, demand-intensive customers have a higher priority for \\nlogistics use, followed by demand-stable customers, and finally by \\ndemand-scattered customers. Because demand-intensive customers \\nhave the highest demand for responsiveness in the network. Therefore, a \\nhigher response rate should be maintained for demand-intensive cus-\\ntomers in the CPI infrastructure-integrated logistics network. However, \\nhigh responsiveness and long-distance transport lead to high costs, so \\nthe regional link centers should be set up close to the demand-intensive \\ncustomers to improve the responsiveness of the logistics network. In this \\nstudy, the appropriate location of the CPI regional link centers is Table 1 \\nNotations.  \\nSymbol Description \\nK The optimal number of autonomous areas \\nk The number of autonomous area \\nCi Cluster i obtained by partitioning the demand nodes, referring to the \\nautonomous area i \\nxl Logistics node l \\nxd Demand node d \\nωdl The weight of the distance ldl \\nAD d The average demand of demand node d  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n6\\nFig. 2.K-means autonomous areas division flowchart.  \\nFig. 3.Example of an abstract network topology.  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n7determined by setting weights to the distance between the CPI regional \\nlink centers and various customers, which is expressed as Equation (1): \\nargmin\\nxl∃Cî\\nxd∃Ci‡xd\\u0000xl‡2≡ωdli1C2C⋯Ck (1)  \\n5.CPI transport layer fusion mechanism \\n5.1. Transport layer routing process \\nIn computer networks, routers are divided into border routers and \\ninterior routers according to their different routing functions. Border \\nrouters connect different autonomous systems and handle routing in-\\nformation exchange across autonomous systems, while interior routers \\nhandle routing and forwarding within the internal network of the \\nautonomous system. Routers that exchange routing information across \\nautonomous systems by establishing neighbor relationships with border \\nrouters of other autonomous systems are called backbone routers. They \\ndetermine optimal routing paths and forwarding strategies, enabling \\ndata packets to pass effectively among autonomous systems. In this way, \\nthe interconnectivity of autonomous systems and connectivity of the \\nglobal network is achieved. \\nIn CPI logistics networks, the PI nodes are divided into edge nodes \\nand internal nodes. According to the links provided by carriers, edge \\nnodes (E nodes in Fig. 3) are PI nodes with inter-autonomous area links, \\nwhile internal nodes only have intra-autonomous area links. Core nodes \\n(C nodes in Fig. 3) and auxiliary core nodes are the backbone nodes of \\nthe CPI logistics network. Because they have the highest performance \\nand reliability within the autonomous area to cope with huge network \\ntraffic and complex routing requests. \\nThe routing process for PI containers from one autonomous area to \\nanother will be illustrated based on the network topology presented in \\nFig. 3. Two autonomous areas are included in the figure. C Node 1 \\nrepresents core node of autonomous area 216.3.x.x, PI Node 2 and PI \\nNode3 are ordinary PI node in the autonomous area 216.3.x.x. E Node \\n4, E Node 5 are edge nodes of autonomous area 216.3.x.x, E Node 6 is \\nan edge node of autonomous area 91.198.x.x. Suppose a PI container \\nneed to be sent to E Node 6 from PI Node 2. PI Node 2 will first query \\nits own real-time routing table for the link with destination address \\n91.198.174.1. Since PI Node 2 is an internal node, its routing table \\ncontains only links to other nodes inside the autonomous area 216.3.x. \\nx. Links to other autonomous areas are all recorded in the routing ta-\\nbles of core nodes and auxiliary core node. Therefore, PI Node 2 will \\naccess to Core Node 1′s routing table to find the link to autonomous \\narea 91.198.x.x. When PI Node 2 knows that the PI container should be \\nforwarded via E Node 5 to the autonomous area 91.198.x.x, it will \\nselect path ③ as the next-hop path and send the PI container to C Node \\n1. After C Node 1 receives the PI container, C Node 1 will repeat the \\nrouting process described above to determine the next-hop path for the \\nPI container by querying its own routing table. As a result, path ④ will \\nbe selected as the next-hop path, and the PI container will be sent to E \\nNode 5. The routing table is updated in real time based on the changes \\nin link information throughout the network, and after several queries \\nand forwards, the PI container will finally be delivered to E Node 6. \\nNotably, the links recorded in the routing table are always the optimal \\nselection. On one hand, in Fig. 3, there are two links from PI Node 2 to \\nE Node 5: PI Node 2-PI Node 3-E Node 5 and PI Node 2-C Node 1-E \\nNode 5. Since the metric of the first link is 15 while the second is 9, \\nthe second link with a lower metric will be selected as the optimal link \\nand recorded in the routing tables. On the other hand, there may be \\nmany carriers providing transport services on the same link. Based on \\ntheir quote for the link, the cheapest one will be selected to provide the \\nservice, and the overhead of links will be recorded in the routing table \\nas a metric. If no carrier is willing to provide transport services for a \\npath, the link does not exist. 5.2. Transport layer routing mechanism \\nThe guiding price of a path is calculated based on the carrier quote. \\nAccording to different characteristics, paths are classified into distance \\nvector master path and link-state branch paths, corresponding to inter- \\nautonomous area links and intra-autonomous area links, respectively. \\nIn this study, we developed the CPI routing mechanism based on the \\nfollowing assumptions: (1) The PI containers flowing in the CPI logistics \\nnetwork are all standard boxes with the same weight, volume, and type, \\n(2) Distance vector links can provide sufficient capacity, while link-state \\npaths have limited capacity, (3) Each link is associated with a carrier \\nquote. Links covering the distance vector master path are charged based \\non transport distance, while links covering the link state branch path are \\ncharged based on the number of PI containers to be transported. (4) The \\ncarrier is only allowed to quote once in a short period and the quote \\nremains unchanged for the duration of the logistics process. \\nThe CPI routing mechanism adopted in the CPI transport layer is \\npresented in Fig. 4. At the beginning, the demand quantity and desti-\\nnation of the PI container should be clarified. Then, the status of each \\npath that connects the current node is checked according to the routing \\ntable. If the path status is healthy, it can be quoted by carriers to \\ngenerate links. Correspondingly, road repairs, road closure and the \\nexhaustion of link capacity would cause the path to be identified as \\nunhealthy, and the overhead of unhealthy paths will be set to an infinite \\nvalue to avoid being selected for routing. The routing table is updated in \\nreal time, showing the optimal next-hop path that reaches the destina -\\ntion with the least overhead for the current node at that time. If the next \\nhop is the destination, the routes, routing distance and total overhead \\ncan be output. Otherwise, the current node will be replaced by the next \\nhop, and the process will be re-executed until the PI container reaches its \\ndestination. \\nThe charging methods are various according to different types of \\npaths. Since the market for master paths is saturated, multiple carriers \\ncan provide transport service for a path and the capacity of the distance \\nvector path is sufficient. Neither carriers nor other logistics participants \\ncan affect the market price of links for distance vector paths by \\nmonopolizing the capacity. The market price only depends on the supply \\nand demand. The overhead of a distance vector path mainly depends on \\nthe transport distance. Carriers use unit distance quotes for distance \\nvector paths and compete to provide transport service. Therefore, ac-\\ncording to the DVMP routing mechanism, the unit distance quotes from \\nall participating carriers should first be obtained. Then, the quotes are \\nmultiplied by the distance and the number of PI containers to get the \\ngradient price for the path. The link with the smallest gradient price will \\nbe selected as the optimal link, and the gradient price for the corre -\\nsponding link as the path overhead will be added to the total overhead. \\nThe capacity provided by carriers is generally limited due to the \\nmode of transport in branch paths. They usually give a ladder price \\nbased on the quantity of PI containers. For the reason of limited re-\\nsources, the unit price gets higher as the number of PI containers in-\\ncreases. Therefore, according to the LSBP routing mechanism, the ladder \\nprice quotes from all participating carriers should first be obtained. \\nThen, the guiding prices corresponding to the quantity of PI containers \\nare calculated based on the gradient price. The carrier that provides the \\nlink with the smallest gradient price will be first selected to provide \\ntransport service. However, if too many PI containers are shipped on the \\nsame path, they may be shared by more than one carrier. For instance, a \\ncarrier provides a link with a capacity of 30 units for a unit price of 25, \\nbut from the 31st unit, which is the second link provided by the carrier, \\nthe unit price becomes 30. Another carrier ’s first link provides 20 units \\nof capacity at a unit price of 28, but the unit price for its second link \\nbecomes 32. If a total of 40 units of PI containers are to be shipped to the \\nsame destination, the two carriers will be selected to transport together, \\nand the path overhead should be the sum of the guiding prices of the two \\nlinks. H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n86.A case study \\n6.1. Background \\nTo validate the CPI-LI2 framework, this paper conducted a case study \\nof the medical logistics industry in the context of GBA. On one hand, the GBA has world-class logistics infrastructures and intensive logistics ac-\\ntivity, in which the cities are facing the development requirement of \\nintegration. The multifaceted and in-depth cooperative relationships \\namong the cities provide the conditions for the fusion of the link layer. \\nOn the other hand, containers for medical logistics have reached a \\nrelatively high level of standardization, since some medicines must be \\nFig. 4.Flowchart of the routing mechanism.  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n9transported in a specific storage environment. The circulation and use of \\nstandardized medical logistics containers are not restricted by region or \\norganization, which meets the requirements for use in the CPI-LI2 \\nframework. \\nIn this case study, the medical institutions and logistics hubs in the \\nGBA are the nodes in the logistics network, as PI demand nodes and PI \\nlogistics nodes, respectively. Medical institutions report a need for a \\nparticular type of medicine, and then the medicines will be shipped from \\na PI logistics node to the medical institutions. Notably, the unit of \\ntransport in the CPI logistics system is the container rather than the \\nmedicines or vehicles. The medicines are encapsulated in the PI con-\\ntainers and one vehicle may load various types of containers. Carriers \\nare responsible for transport tasks and quote independently to the links \\nthey provide. The quotes may vary from different paths or different \\ncarriers or different quantities of containers to be shipped, influenced by \\nmany factors such as path status, quality of service, and the disposable \\namount of logistics resources. For the same route, the transport tasks will \\nbe first assigned to the links lower charged by the carrier. \\nAccording to these settings, the CPI-LI2 framework is used to perform \\nthe process of logistics infrastructure integration, link generation and \\nintegration, routing table generation and routing, sequentially, to finally \\nsatisfy the demands of all the PI demand nodes. Moreover, to indicate \\nthe advantages of the CPI routing mechanism, we adopted the common \\nevolutionary algorithms such as GA and PSO for routing solving to make \\ncomparisons with the CPI routing mechanism. \\n6.2. Experimental design \\nIn this section, we intend to verify the performance of the CPI-LI2 \\nframework. The experimental data is provided by our cooperating \\ncompany, a medical logistics service provider. The data mainly includes \\ntwo parts. One is basic information about medical institutions and lo-\\ngistics hubs, including the name, address, and demands of medical in-\\nstitutions. Another is the link information, including the quote of links, \\nthe capacity of the links, and path status. We randomly selected demand \\nand carrier’s quotes over time from the data. The data exemplifications \\nare given in Table 2 and Table 3. \\nThe experiment follows three steps: first, using location information \\nof medical institutions and logistics infrastructure in the GBA, construct \\na logistics infrastructure integration-based CPI logistics network ac-\\ncording to the link layer fusion mechanism. Then, according to the \\ntransport layer fusion mechanism, simulate carrier quotes for link-state \\npaths and distance vector paths to establish the routing table and guide \\nrouting. Finally, for the routing process, two common heuristic algo-\\nrithms, Genetic Algorithm (GA) and Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), \\nare introduced for comparison experiments with the CPI routing \\nmechanism proposed in this paper. Since the size of the network may \\nhave an impact on the performance of algorithms, comparison experi -\\nments with networks of different sizes are also set up. The experiments were running on PyCharm CE (v2022.3.2) under \\nthe environment of macOS Sonoma 14.2.1 on a MacBook Air with an \\nApple M2 chip and 16 GB of RAM. \\n6.3. Link layer fusion of medical logistics network \\nBased on the coordinate information of medical institutions in GBA, \\nwe use the K-means clustering method for autonomous area division, \\nand three assessment methods, in terms of the elbow method, silhouette \\ncoefficient and CH index, to measure clustering performance. K-means is \\na classical but effective clustering algorithm [50]. It is easy to implement \\nand is scaled to handle large-scale data due to its low computational \\ncomplexity. For the dataset in this study, it outperformed other clus-\\ntering methods, such as K-medoids and DBSCAN. Since the K-medoids \\nclustering algorithm uses the actual data points as the centres of the \\nclusters rather than the mean, it is more accurate in capturing and \\nassigning outliers in the dataset. DBSCAN is a density-based clustering \\nalgorithm that can be better adapted to different shapes of clustering \\nstructures than K-means. However, according to the silhouette co-\\nefficients (Table 4) and the visualization effect (Fig. 5) obtained through \\neach clustering method, K-means is more applicable to partitioning in \\nthe GBA. \\nAfter determining the clustering method, the results of the three \\nassessment methods to get the appropriate K value as the number of \\nautonomous areas. The output of the program is shown in Fig. 6: \\nThe result of the elbow method reveals that the SSE tends to decrease \\nwith an increasing value of K, and when K is equal to 6, there is an \\nobvious turning point on the plot where the rate of decrease in the SSE \\nsignificantly slows down, indicating the optimal number of clusters. The \\nsilhouette coefficient and CH index also show that clustering is relatively \\nbetter when the number of clusters is 6. There is a significant decrease in \\nthe clustering effect whether the number of clusters is increased or \\ndecreased. Although clustering seems to work better when the K value is \\ngreater than 8; however, due to the consideration of cost and manage -\\nment difficulty, we usually do not choose to divide a larger number of \\nautonomous areas. Therefore, finally GBA is divided into six autono -\\nmous areas, which is presented in Fig. 7. \\nAfter the autonomous area division, autonomous areas support each \\nother in maintaining the logistics network of the GBA. Through the \\nelection service, the core autonomous area with the greatest logistics \\ncapabilities is identified to interconnect with the other regions outside \\nGBA. The weighted distance function (Equation (1)) is used to determine \\nthe regional link centers which are PI logistics nodes with the most \\nabundant logistics resources and infrastructures to respond efficiently to \\nthe PI demand nodes in the region. In the case study, the weights are \\nobtained using the Equation (2), related to the customer demand. Fig. 8 \\npresents the GBA medical logistics network obtained after CPI link layer \\nfusion. \\nTable 2 \\nThe data exemplification of PI nodes.  \\nID Name Type Address Longitude Latitude Cluster Demand \\n1 Guangdong Province People’s Hospital 1 Zhongshan 2nd Road, Guangzhou, China 113.2628592 23.12385815 4 8 \\n… … … … … … … … \\n1000 Guangzhou Airport 0 Baiyun District, Guangzhou, China 113.307605 23.389929 4 /  \\nTable 3 \\nThe data exemplification of quotes.  \\nID Link Effective time Expiration time Carrier Offered capacity Link type Charge(unit) \\nFrom To \\n1 1000 1 00:00 24:00 A 20 LSBP 10.73 \\n… … … … …  …  … \\n50 25 66 8:00 20:00 G 5 LSBP 13.19  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n10ωdlAD d⋃\\nxd∃CiAD di1C2C⋯Ck (2)  \\n6.4. Transport layer fusion of medical logistics network \\nBased on the autonomous areas generated by the link layer fusion, \\nthe paths ’ type is determined. According to the quotations of carriers, \\nthe gradient price of paths can be obtained and used to calculate the \\noverhead of routes, and thus decide the optimal route. The algorithm of \\nthis transformation is shown in Algorithm 1.  \\nAlgorithm 1: Generation of routing table \\nInput: quotation \\nOutput: routing_table \\ncreate path_table [] \\nfor i 0 to length(quotation) \\u00001 do \\nfor j 0 to length(quotation[i]) \\u00001 do \\nlinks_ijk quotation[i][j] \\nInitialize g_price infinity \\nInitialize g_capacity 0 \\nfor each link_ijk in links_ijk do \\nif link_ijk.capacity 0 then \\nlink_ijk.charge infinity \\nelse \\nif link_ijk.charge Dg_price then \\ng_price link_ijk.charge \\ng_capacity link_ijk.capacity \\nend if \\nend if \\nend for \\npath_ij.gradient_price g_price \\npath_ij.gradient_capacity g_capacity \\nappend path_table, path_ij \\nend for \\nend for \\ncreate routing_table {} \\nfor i 0 to num_nodes \\u00001 do \\nrouting_table[i] {} \\nfor j 0 to num_nodes \\u00001 do \\ninitialize min_route null \\ninitialize min_metric infinity \\nfor k 0 to num_routes[i][j] \\u00001 do \\npaths routes[i][j][k] \\ninitialize metric 0 \\nfor each path in paths do \\nif path.type “DVMP ” then \\npath.guiding_price f1(path) \\nelse \\npath.guiding_price f2(path) \\nend if \\nmetric metric path.guiding_price \\nend for \\nif metric Dmin_metric then \\nmin_metric metric \\nmin_route paths \\nend if \\nend for \\nrouting_table[i][j] { \\n“route ”: min_route, \\n“metric ”: min_metric \\n} \\nend for \\nend for \\nReturn routing_table  \\nFor the transport layer fusion, we use three methods to solve the routing \\nproblems: the proposed routing mechanism, GA and PSO. GA and PSO are popular optimization algorithms in path planning problems. Both \\nalgorithms randomly generate an initial solution and then find the \\noptimal solution through iteration, so the solution is heavily influenced \\nby the size of the network. Whereas the proposed routing mechanism is a \\nstrategy-based method and does not require iteration. The routing \\nmechanism stated that the next-hop path for the current node is deter -\\nmined by instant access to the routing table. If the routing table indicates \\nthat more than one demand node can be traveled from the current node, \\nthe demand node with the least overhead is preferred. To test their \\nperformance and stability, we design four network sizes: 2 logistics \\nnodes with 20 demand nodes, 2 logistics nodes with 50 demand nodes, 4 \\nlogistics nodes with 50 demand nodes, and 4 logistics nodes with 100 \\ndemand nodes. Four indicators are used to measure the logistics net-\\nworks: the total overhead of routing, the delay rate of demand nodes, the \\nnumber of overload paths, and the load balance of logistics nodes. \\nSince the objective is to fulfill the demands in the logistics network \\non time with minimal overheads and to avoid causing congestion on the \\nroad, the fitness of the algorithm is set to include delayed punishment, \\nthe overhead of the routes chosen to fulfill the demands in the logistics \\nnetwork, and the overload punishment. The iteration number of GA and \\nPSO is set to 20 to balance performance and solution time. In GA, the \\ngene represents the allocation of demand nodes to logistics nodes and \\nthe number of logistics nodes allocated to each demand node. The \\npopulation size is 50, and the probabilities of crossover, mutation, and \\nselection are 0.1, 0.05 and 0.5, respectively. The inverse of the fitness \\nwas used as a weight in selection. In PSO, the population size is 20, with \\nthe cognitive parameter and social parameter both set to 0.2 to control \\nthe candidate solution moving toward the optimal solution. \\nFor each size of the logistics network, the average of the results of ten \\nrandomized experiments is taken as the result of the performance of the \\nalgorithms as shown in Table 5. The routes solved by the three algo-\\nrithms under the four sizes of logistics networks are shown in Fig. 9. For \\neach scenario, the variance of the results of ten repeat experiments is \\ntaken as the result of the stability of the algorithms as shown in Table 6. \\nAs presented in Table 5, by comparing the performance of routing \\nsolved by the three methods in different network sizes, we have some \\nfindings. Firstly, the solution time and the routing overhead obtained by \\nusing the CPI routing mechanism are always smaller than that obtained \\nby using GA and PSO algorithms, and these advantages become more \\nsignificant as the network size increases. Secondly, PSO routing is \\nrelatively time-consuming, resulting in some of the demand nodes in the \\nlogistics network not being satisfied within the specified time. The larger \\nthe network size, the more demand nodes that cannot be satisfied in PSO \\nrouting, whereas the other two routing schemes can complete the \\ntransport task within the specified time regardless of the network size. \\nThirdly, there are no overloaded paths under any of the three routing \\nschemes. Lastly, as the network becomes larger, PSO routing has better \\nload balancing of logistics nodes than the other two methods. In addi-\\ntion, by comparing the performance of the network with 2 logistics \\nnodes and 50 demand nodes and the network with 4 logistics nodes and \\n50 demand nodes, the results show that an increase in the number of \\nlogistics nodes leads to a decrease in routing overhead when the number \\nof demand nodes is constant. \\nIn Fig. 9, the routes of containers sent from different logistics nodes \\nare marked with different colors. From the routing based on CPI routing \\nmechanism, the different logistics nodes have relatively limited-service \\ncoverage and non-overlapping service networks, and the routes are \\ndenser the closer they are to the logistics nodes. In contrast, GA routing \\nand PSO routing have a wide range of logistics node services, and the \\nservice networks of different logistics nodes overlap each other, and the \\nroutes are denser towards the centre of GBA. \\nTable 6 shows that the CPI routing mechanism is the most stable \\nalgorithm of the three. The stability of all three algorithms decreases \\nsignificantly when the network size increases significantly. Table 4 \\nThe clustering results.  \\nClustering method The optimal number of clusters Silhouettes coefficient \\nK-means 6  0.555301 \\nK-medoids 5  0.510533 \\nDBSCAN 2  0.562324  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n116.5. Discussion \\nIn this case study, we apply the CPI-LI2 framework to the logistics \\nnetwork of a medical logistics company in the GBA, gradually \\ncompleting the link layer fusion, the transport layer fusion, and the \\ntransport layer operation. The link layer fusion is to integrate the lo-\\ngistics infrastructures in the logistics network to support the integrative \\noperation at the upper layer. According to the distribution of customers, i.e. demand nodes, the logistics networks are divided into 6 autonomous \\nareas, and the logistics infrastructure, i.e. logistics nodes, are also clus-\\ntered into six autonomous areas based on their geographical location. \\nHowever, due to the data limitation, we do not demonstrate the election \\nof core autonomous area, and set the distance weights only considering \\nthe demands in the generation of regional link centers. In practice, many \\nnon-quantitative indicators need to be taken into account. We have \\npresented the principles and methods of elections from a theoretical \\nFig. 7.The clustering visualization on the GBA map.  \\nFig. 6.The results of the elbow method, silhouette coefficient and CH index.  \\nFig. 5.The clustering results of three K-means, K-medoids and DBSCAN.  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n12point of view in the previous sections. After the link layer fusion, paths \\nwith specific types are generated accordingly. Link layer fusion is for \\ninterconnection at the physical level, and transport layer fusion is pri-\\nmarily for interconnection at the cyber level. The information flows in \\ncyberspace to control the real-life logistics process. The transport re-\\nsources in the logistics network are integrated and coordinated. For \\nexample, routing relies on routing tables that reflect real-time road and \\nlink information to guide routing in logistics networks. \\nIn the routing experiment, we used three methods to solve the \\nrouting: the CPI routing mechanism, GA and PSO. In addition to the \\nrouting overhead, we also consider delay and overload penalties in the \\nobjective function. However, delays are still not avoided in the solution \\nof PSO routing as shown in Table 5. This indicates that the PSO solution \\nfails to balance the routing overhead and traveling time. Compared with \\nGA and PSO, the CPI routing mechanism has a short solution time, since \\nCPI routing mechanism does not require iteration and does not seek to \\nobtain an optimal solution. It only relies on rules that are set in advance, \\nwhereas the optimization algorithms need time to optimize the initial \\nsolution. As the size of the network becomes larger, the solution space \\nbecomes larger, and finding the optimal solution becomes more difficult. \\nWith a limited number of iterations and time, the solution is inferior to \\nthat of the CPI routing mechanism. Since the infrastructure-integrated \\nlogistics network is a large-size network and the path status in the \\nnetwork is volatile, a routing mechanism based on routing tables that \\ncan respond to changes in the network in real time is more appropriate. \\nFrom Fig. 9, it can be shown that both GA and PSO routing schemes have \\nmany long routes shuttling nodes in different autonomous areas, and the \\nhigh guiding price of these distance vector paths affects their total \\nrouting overhead. Under CPI routing, the service scope of each logistics \\nnode is independent. This is because the mechanism of CPI routing is \\nthat logistics nodes will prioritize serving the nearest demand node, which avoids overlapping of routes. What ’s more, the difference in \\nrouting overhead between the second and third network sizes illustrates \\nthat the overhead can be reduced by appropriately increasing the \\nnumber of logistics demand nodes to match the demand nodes. The \\nvalue presented in Table 6 shows that the CPI routing mechanism is the \\nmost stable algorithm of the three. When the supply and demand in the \\nlogistics network are relatively balanced, the variation of the solution of \\nthe CPI routing mechanism under repeated experiments is small. The \\nstability of all three algorithms decreases significantly when the network \\nsize increases significantly. The solutions of the GA and PSO algorithms \\nare unstable because they obtain the optimal solution by exchanging \\noperators with uncertainty. \\nThe CPI-LI2 framework provides an open interconnected logistics \\nnetwork based on logistics infrastructure integration. Since land re-\\nsources and social resources are limited, endless construction of new lo-\\ngistics infrastructure is not sustainable. Although the overall trend of \\nlogistics demand is growing, logistics demand is dynamic and imbal -\\nanced. Logistics demand is not always so high and varies among logistics \\ncompanies. For example, demand for e-commerce logistics explodes \\nduring merchandise promotions but usually remains largely at a stable \\nlevel. When there is an oversupply of logistics services, a large amount of \\nlogistics infrastructure and logistics resources go idle. Integration of lo-\\ngistics infrastructure promotes resource sharing and balances the utili-\\nzation of logistics infrastructure by coordinating logistics infrastructure, \\nthereby alleviating the waste of resources. Moreover, infrastructure \\nintegration reduces unnecessary infrastructure construction costs. \\nIn addition, the CPI-LI2 framework enhances the interoperability of \\nlogistics networks. In the CPI logistics network, goods may transit \\nthrough multiple PI nodes, with different carriers relaying transport to \\ndeliver them to their destination. This is enabled based on the integra -\\ntion of links provided by different carriers. The CPI-LI2 framework Table 5 \\nThe performance of the algorithms for different network sizes.  \\nNetwork Size Algorithm Solution Time Overhead Delay Rate Overload Load Balance \\n2–20 GA  3.092  138596.067 / /  3.625 \\nPSO  1.290  226626.177 / /  4.025 \\nRT  0.009  116536.750 / /  3.625 \\n2–50 GA  36.657  413417.466 / /  15.425 \\nPSO  15.093  535588.077 0.002 /  12.800 \\nRT  0.261  195124.045 / /  16.800 \\n4–50 GA  40.750  340111.797 / /  9.819 \\nPSO  16.850  492093.734 0.006 /  7.613 \\nRT  0.393  143109.102 / /  8.713 \\n4–100 GA  302.390  743460.719 / /  28.156 \\nPSO  123.189  975129.287 0.020 /  23.588 \\nRT  6.228  185652.529 / /  28.006  \\nFig. 8.Schematic diagram of the CPI link layer fusion.  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n13evaluates each link and coordinates the available capacities in the lo-\\ngistics network. Since the decision-making is supported by real-time \\ndata, the logistics network can act in response to disturbances and dis-\\nruptions. When a path failure occurs in the CPI logistics network, the \\nrouting solutions for the destination from the current PI node can be \\nalternatively the next optimal path immediately by querying the routing \\ntable. In addition, if a new quote for a path is made, it will be processed \\nsoon, and the corresponding link will be created for routing. Thus, it \\nalways selects the carriers with the lowest charge to provide transport \\nservice. \\nFig. 9.The routes for different network sizes.  \\nTable 6 \\nThe stability of the algorithms for different network sizes.  \\nNetwork Size Routing Overhead \\nGA PSO RT \\n2–20  265624425.6  1998732846.6  128081.6 \\n2–50  382756553.1  4950039632.0  37522.1 \\n4–50  771335554.4  3536649152.9  569.3 \\n4–100  2634480093.5  11474126420.4  580853.1  H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n147.Conclusion \\nCPI is an emerging paradigm for building global interconnected lo-\\ngistics networks. It constructs a new management paradigm by recon -\\nfiguring the logistics network to improve logistics efficiency. CPI builds \\na cyber layer on top of the PI framework. PI containers are configured \\nand scheduled in cyberspace, enabling seamless flows of PI containers in \\nthe logistics network similar to data packets that are transmitted in the \\ncomputer network. The fusion of the logistics networks is a significant \\nstep to initiate this concept. With an integrated logistics infrastructure, \\ndata and information can be shared and used throughout the logistics \\nnetwork for maintaining stable network operation. \\nAs one of the world’s economic centers, the GBA has complete lo-\\ngistics infrastructures, yet the capability of some logistics infrastructures \\nhas not been effectively developed and utilized. The integration of lo-\\ngistics infrastructure is a key move in promoting the realization of \\nsynergistic development of the logistics industry in the GBA. In this \\nstudy, the two-layered CPI-LI2 framework is proposed to enable the lo-\\ngistics infrastructure integration in the GBA. At the link layer, a LEAF \\nmethod is adopted to divide the region into several autonomous areas \\nand to establish connections between them by electing core autonomous \\nareas and regional link centers. Through the link layer fusion, the lo-\\ngistics infrastructures in the network are integrated. The DVMP routing \\nmechanism and LSBP routing mechanism are proposed based on the \\ndistance vector characteristics and the path state characteristics of the \\npath, respectively. The combination of these two routing mechanisms \\nforms the CPI routing mechanism to coordinate the transport resources. \\nTo validate the CPI-LI2 framework, it is applied to a medical logistics \\nnetwork in GBA. Through a case study, we demonstrate the applicability \\nand extensibility of the framework and the advantages of the CPI routing \\nmechanism in the context of dynamic, large-scale networks. \\nThe CPI-LI2 framework improves interoperability of infrastructure \\nthrough logistics infrastructure integration and enables real-time \\nmonitoring, analysis and decision-making in CPI logistics networks \\nthrough flexible operational mechanisms. The CPI-LI2 framework has \\nthe potential to contribute to the development of CPI in two aspects: \\n(1)This paper proposed the LEAF method to integrate logistics in-\\nfrastructures. This method introduces the concept of computer \\nnetworks and uses clustering methods to divide the logistics \\nnetwork into different autonomous areas, similar to the autono -\\nmous systems in computer networks. In this case, failures in the \\nlocal network do not have a huge impact on the whole network, \\nand the division of areas reduces the size of the database and \\nlowers the requirements for infrastructure capacity. Moreover, the \\nLEAF method improves network response efficiency and disaster \\ntolerance through a weighted distance function and core autono -\\nmous area election. The LEAF method is a combination of several \\ngeneral methods. It considers the influence of realistic factors on \\nthe division of physical network areas and adopts a suitable \\nmethod to construct a reliable physical network link layer, which \\nprovides a solution to the problem of large-scale logistics infra-\\nstructure integration that is influenced by multiple factors.  \\n(2) This paper developed a DVMP routing mechanism for distance \\nvector paths and an LSBP routing mechanism for link state paths \\nat the transport layer and adopted the guiding price as the path \\noverhead, forming a CPI routing mechanism that can flexibly \\nselect the optimal next hop. This mechanism adapts to changes in \\ninfrastructure-integrated logistics networks and ensures efficient \\ndecision-making in large-scale networks. In addition, this mech -\\nanism has portability. Since the guiding price is based on carriers’ \\nquotes, which widely exist in logistics networks for different \\ntypes and different scales, the CPI routing mechanism can be \\napplied in other transportation scenarios (e.g., air transportation \\nand shipping) and even be further developed into a multimodal \\ntransport model. Notably, some specific topics are worthy of further exploration. First, \\nthis study uses the Euclidean distance rather than the actual logistical \\ndistance as the distance between two locations. The main reason is that \\ndistance does not have a large impact on the results, and most studies \\nused the Euclidean distance to design routing mechanisms. If the dis-\\ntance between two locations is given or can be calculated, the Euclidean \\ndistance can be replaced by the actual distance. For another reason, the \\nreality is highly complicated, while the experimental approach in this \\npaper is a simulation based on the principle of simplicity; thus, it is \\nunnecessary to use a complex model consisting of actual paths for \\nresearch. Second, the process of designing the CPI-LI2 framework con-\\nsiders mostly physical factors and neglects human factors. When the \\nregional link centers are elected based on a distance weighting function, \\nthe weights are roughly estimated based on experience. The high levels \\nof standardization and intelligence of CPI infrastructure significantly \\nreduce the manpower required in the logistics process. In addition, labor \\ncosts are only a small part of the operating cost in the logistics process. \\nFinally, there is no comparison between the CPI routing mechanism and \\ntraditional navigation. On the one hand, the CPI routing mechanism \\nproposed in this paper is based on a highly shared intelligent logistics \\nnetwork; however, the current logistics infrastructure and logistics in-\\nformation from different logistics stakeholders are not shared at a high \\nlevel, and there is still a gap compared to an ideal CPI logistics scenario, \\nso this paper cannot force the level of infrastructure sharing. On the \\nother hand, the limited scale of data collected in this paper is not suf-\\nficient to support the comparison of CPI routing mechanisms with \\ntraditional navigation, which is also one of the limitations of this paper. \\nThis part of the work will be supplemented if more comprehensive data \\ncan be obtained subsequently. Although the current research on CPI is at \\nthe stage of theoretical accumulation and validation, the experiments in \\nthis paper can reflect the advancement and feasibility of the CPI concept \\nto some extent. \\nThe CPI-LI2 framework developed in this paper is based on the land \\ntransportation scenario in the GBA. However, one of the characteristics \\nof the logistics network in the GBA is multimodal transport. The current \\nrouting mechanism at the transport layer cannot support multimodal \\ntransport scenarios due to the various data formats and standards in \\ndifferent transport modes. In the future, we will focus on the route \\ntransfer and transport mode switch problems under multimodal trans -\\nport scenarios and develop improved routing mechanisms to allow the \\nCPI-LI2 framework to be adapted to the multimodal scenarios, promot -\\ning deeper integration of logistics infrastructure. \\nCRediT authorship contribution statement \\nHang Wu: Data curation, Formal analysis, Methodology, Software, \\nValidation, Writing – original draft. Linhao Huang: Investigation, \\nWriting – original draft, Methodology. Ming Li: Conceptualization, \\nFunding acquisition, Methodology, Resources, Supervision, Writing – \\nreview & editing. George Q. Huang: Funding acquisition, Supervision, \\nWriting – review & editing. \\nDeclaration of competing interest \\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial \\ninterests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence \\nthe work reported in this paper. \\nData availability \\nData will be made available on request. \\nAcknowledgments \\nThis research is supported by the Hong Kong RGC Theme-based \\nResearch Scheme (No. T32-707/22-N), the Science and Technology H. Wu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \\nAdvanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\\n15Projects in Guangzhou (No.202201010329) and Departmental General \\nResearch Fund of PolyU (No. P0045748). \\nReferences \\n[1]M. Fazili, U. Venkatadri, P. Cyrus, M. Tajbakhsh, Physical internet, conventional \\nand hybrid logistic systems: a routing optimisation-based comparison using the \\nEastern Canada road network case study, Int. J. Prod. Res. 55 (9) (2017) \\n2703 –2730, https://doi.org/10.1080/00207543.2017.1285075 . \\n[2]B. Montreuil, R. D. Meller, and E. Ballot, ‘Physical Internet Foundations ’, in Service \\nOrientation in Holonic and Multi Agent Manufacturing and Robotics , T. Borangiu, A. \\nThomas, and D. Trentesaux, Eds., in Studies in Computational Intelligence. , Berlin, \\nHeidelberg: Springer, 2013, pp. 151–166. doi: 10.1007/978-3-642-35852-4_10. \\n[3]M. Rajahonka, A. Bask, S.A. Yawar, M. Tinnil a, The physical internet as enabler of \\nnew business models enhancing greener transports and the circular economy, \\nSustain. Effic. Transp. (2019) 70–94. \\n[4]P. Pagano et al., ‘RTPORT: the 5G-based Model-Driven real Time Module for \\nGeneral Cargo Management ’, p. 17. \\n[5]Y. Sallez, S. Pan, B. Montreuil, T. Berger, E. Ballot, On the activeness of intelligent \\nPhysical Internet containers, Comput. Ind. 81 (2016) 96–104, https://doi.org/ \\n10.1016/j.compind.2015.12.006 . \\n[6]T. Ambra, A. Caris, C. Macharis, Towards freight transport system unification: \\nreviewing and combining the advancements in the physical internet and \\nsynchromodal transport research, Int. J. Prod. Res. 57 (6) (2019) 1606 –1623, \\nhttps://doi.org/10.1080/00207543.2018.1494392 . \\n[7]H. Tran-Dang, D.-S. Kim, The physical internet in the era of digital transformation: \\nperspectives and open issues, IEEE Access 9 (2021) 164613 –164631, https://doi. \\norg/10.1109/ACCESS.2021.3131562 . \\n[8]X.T.R. Kong, K. Kang, R.Y. Zhong, H. Luo, S.X. Xu, Cyber physical system-enabled \\non-demand logistics trading, Int. J. Prod. Econ. 233 (2021) 108005, https://doi. \\norg/10.1016/j.ijpe.2020.108005 . \\n[9]X.T.R. Kong, X. Yang, K.L. Peng, C.Z. Li, Cyber physical system-enabled \\nsynchronization mechanism for pick-and-sort ecommerce order fulfilment, \\nComput. Ind. 118 (2020) 103220, https://doi.org/10.1016/j. \\ncompind.2020.103220 . \\n[10] Z. Zhao, R.Y. Zhong, Y.-H. Kuo, Y. Fu, G.Q. Huang, Cyber-physical spatial temporal \\nanalytics for digital twin-enabled smart contact tracing, Ind. Manag. Data Syst. 121 \\n(5) (2021) 1082 –1106, https://doi.org/10.1108/IMDS-11-2020-0634 . \\n[11] Y. Jiang, X. Liu, K. Kang, Z. Wang, R.Y. Zhong, G.Q. Huang, Blockchain-enabled \\ncyber-physical smart modular integrated construction, Comput. Ind. 133 (2021) \\n103553, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compind.2021.103553 . \\n[12] ‘The physical internet ’, Economist , vol. 379, no. 8482, pp. 3–4, 2006. \\n[13] E. Ballot, B. Montreuil, F. Fontane, (2011) ‘Topology of Logistic Networks and the \\nPotential of a Physical Internet ’, in UDICE-French Research Universities , L. \\nBenyoucef, D. Trentesaux, A. Artiba, and N. Rezg, Eds., pp. 585–594. \\n[14] B. Montreuil, Toward a Physical Internet: meeting the global logistics sustainability \\ngrand challenge, Logist. Res. 3 (2) (2011) 71–87, https://doi.org/10.1007/s12159- \\n011-0045-x . \\n[15] R.Y. Zhong, C. Xu, C. Chen, G.Q. Huang, Big data analytics for physical internet- \\nbased intelligent manufacturing shop floors, Int. J. Prod. Res. 55 (9) (2017) \\n2610 –2621, https://doi.org/10.1080/00207543.2015.1086037 . \\n[16] E.K.H. Leung, C.K.H. Lee, Z. Ouyang, From traditional warehouses to Physical \\nInternet hubs: A digital twin-based inbound synchroniza