  "output": "{\n  \"content\": \"Enterprise Information Systems\\nISSN: 1751-7575 (Print) 1751-7583 (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/teis20\\nCyber-physical internet based intelligent\\noperation platform for oﬀ-site construction\\nsupply chain management\\nLi Yin, Tengda Li, Guojin Li, Svetlana Besklubova, Ray Y. Zhong & Shuxuan\\nZhao\\nTo cite this article: Li Yin, Tengda Li, Guojin Li, Svetlana Besklubova, Ray Y. Zhong & Shuxuan\\nZhao (2024) Cyber-physical internet based intelligent operation platform for oﬀ-site\\nconstruction supply chain management, Enterprise Information Systems, 18:6, 2346743, DOI:\\n10.1080/17517575.2024.2346743\\nTo link to this article:  https://doi.org/10.1080/17517575.2024.2346743\\nPublished online: 28 Apr 2024.\\nSubmit your article to this journal \\nArticle views: 487\\nView related articles \\nView Crossmark data\\nCiting articles: 7 View citing articles \\nFull Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=teis20\\nCyber-physical internet based intelligent operation platform \\nfor off-site construction supply chain management\\nLi Yina, Tengda Lib, Guojin Lic, Svetlana Besklubovaa, Ray Y. Zhonga and Shuxuan Zhaoa\\naDepartment of Industrial and Manufacturing Systems Engineering, The University of Hong Kong, \\nHong Kong, China; bIndustrial Energy Products Department, China Mobile Shanghai Industry Research \\nInstitute, Shanghai, China; cDigital Building Technology Center, Central South Architectural Design Institute \\nCo. Ltd, Wuhan, China\\nABSTRACT\\nOff-site construction (OSC) projects rely on a smooth off-site supply \\nchain. Previous studies improved supply chain management (SCM), \\nwhile scantly mentioning business flow and recent advanced tech -\\nnology. This paper proposes a three-layered cyber-physical internet \\n(CPI)-based platform for practical OSC-SCM. The scalable layer con-\\nnects resources via an industrial driver (ID) for an information chain. \\nThe kernel layer designs protocols for digital twins (DT) and twin \\nnetworks with incentive and information sharing mechanisms. The \\napplication layer offers thirteen services for offsite plant, logistics, \\nand on-site assembly. A case study demonstrates 20% reduced \\nconstruction time and millions of cost savings, proving enhanced \\nefficiency.ARTICLE HISTORY \\nReceived 21 January 2024  \\nAccepted 20 April 2024 \\nKEYWORDS \\nCyber-physical internet; \\ndigital twin technology; \\nsupply chain management; \\noff-site construction; \\nintelligent platform\\n1.Introduction\\nPrefabricated buildings are increasingly applied globally in the construction industry (Li, \\nStephens, and Ryba 2014 ; Rippon 2011 ; Zhang et al. 2021 ). This innovative method \\ninvolves transferring a large amount of on-site work to factories, where building compo -\\nnents such as floors, wall panels, stairs, balconies, and bathrooms are processed, manu -\\nfactured, and subsequently transported to the construction site for assembly. \\nPrefabricated buildings offer a range of benefits, including good quality, enhanced \\nefficiency, environmental sustainability, heightened safety, and increased flexibility \\n(Wang et al. 2023 ). However, off-site construction (OSC) method also presents new \\nchallenges for the construction industry (Yu et al. 2019 ). These challenges include the \\nneed to adapt to a dispersed work space and changes in work processes (Teng et al. 2017 ; \\nWang et al. 2020 ). Moreover, compared to traditional construction methods, prefabricated \\nbuildings require more intricate coordination in both on-site and off-site logistics due to \\nthe specific construction sequence (Tan, Xue, and Ting Cheung 2017 ). In fact, 60–80% of \\nthe decisions related to building materials and services in a prefabricated building project \\nrequire the involvement of suppliers and subcontractors (Lindén and Josephson 2013 ; \\nCONTACT Ray Y. Zhong \\n zhongzry@hku.hk \\n Department of Industrial and Manufacturing Systems Engineering, \\nThe University of Hong Kong, 8-6, Haking Wong Building, Pokfulam Road, Hong Kong, ChinaENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS                 \\n2024, VOL. 18, NO. 6, 771–794 \\nhttps://doi.org/10.1080/17517575.2024.2346743\\n© 2024 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group \\nTaherdoost and Madanchian 2023 ). Therefore, the OSC building supply chain assumes \\na pivotal role in improving construction efficiency and reducing construction costs.\\nPrevious studies on OSC-SCM have primarily addressed supply chain issues in separate \\nstages, resulting in two identified research gaps. In the logistics stage, transportation \\nplanning (Yi, Wang, and Zhang 2020 ), component-vehicle allocation plans (Jang et al.  \\n2022 ), precast concrete slab stacks (G. H. Lee et al. 2022 ), and fleet management (Xu et al.  \\n2019 ) have been examined. Similarly, the construction stage has seen studies on con-\\nstruction site layout planning (Tao et al. 2022 ), tower crane layout (Lien and Cheng 2014 ), \\nand material layout planning (Cheng and Chang 2019 ). In terms of off-site planning, \\nresearch has focused on optimising the prefabrication finishing level (Almashaqbeh and \\nEl-Rayes 2021 ), autonomous production tracking (Arashpour et al. 2015 ), and module \\nconfiguration selection (Moselhi and Salama 2015 ; Zhao et al. 2023 ). While these studies \\nhave contributed to supply chain efficiency at each stage, the coordination of multiple \\nstages heavily relies on emerging system technologies. For example, Irizarry, Karan, and \\nJalaei (2013 ) developed an integrated Building Information System-Geographic \\nInformation System (BIM-GIS) to improve visual monitoring and provide real-time infor -\\nmation on components and resources. Chen et al. (2018 ) establish a physical internet-BIM \\n(PI-BIM) platform that offers real-time component and resource information. The various \\ntechnologies and theories, such as Lean and BIM synergies (Bataglin et al. 2020 ), con-\\nstruction process modelling (Zhou, Wei, and Peng 2021 ), game engine technology for \\nteam collaboration (Ezzeddine and de Soto 2021 ), have been applied to supply chain \\ncoordination. However, despite these contributions, two research gaps have been identi -\\nfied. Firstly, previous studies have primarily focused on material and information flows, \\noverlooking the importance of business flow in facilitating collaboration and data collec -\\ntion. Secondly, the application of CPI technology in OSC-SCM has received limited atten -\\ntion, despite its potential for fostering cooperation among stakeholders across multiple \\nstages.\\nThe CPI demonstrates interconnectedness, standardisation, and interoperability \\namong Meta-bodies (Koubâa and Andersson 2009 ). It aims to facilitate object exchange \\nin a manner akin to sending and receiving real-time chat messages on the internet. CPI \\nrepresents a broader vision of a highly interconnected and intelligent network infrastruc -\\nture that enables seamless integration and collaboration between physical objects and \\ndigital systems. In 2023, the CPI concept expanded from the physical internet (PI) to \\ninclude the Metaverse (Huang 2022 ). Within the realm of OSC supply chains, DTs can play \\nan important role in realising the goals of CPI. DTs are established based on essential \\ncomponents such as people, machines, objects, and orders, forming Meta-bodies known \\nas Meta-twins. These Meta-twins are intricately connected both in physical and digital \\nspaces, realising real-time visibility, transparency (Koubâa and Andersson 2009 ; Wei et al.  \\n2023 ). By leveraging DTs, the CPI approach can be effectively implemented, promoting \\ninterconnectedness and interoperability among Meta-bodies and facilitating efficient \\nobject exchange within the supply chain. The introduction of ‘CPI’ presents a fresh and \\ninnovative approach to OSC-SCM.\\nThe remaining sections of this paper are organised as follows: In Section 2, related \\nresearch has been reviewed. Section 3 presents the design of a three-layer network \\nphysical interconnection platform, specifically tailored to support the operation of OSC \\nlogistics. The platform comprises the scalable access layer, the kernel services layer, and 772\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nthe application layer, each described separately. In Section 4, a case study is shown \\nillustrating the implementation of the CPI-enabled SCM platform across nine applications. \\nThe utilisation of DT technology and various sensors facilitates the realisation of the \\nproposed CPI platform. Finally, the paper concludes with a summary and outlines future \\ndirections for research and development.\\n2.Literature review\\n2.1. Off-site construction supply chain management\\nThe success of projects in OSC is highly dependent on effective OSC-SCM (Wang et al.  \\n2019 ). SCM plays a critical role due to the increased complexity and fragmentation of \\nlogistics in OSC (L. Li 2024 ; Tsang, Youqing Fan, and Lau 2024 ; Wang et al. 2020 ). Firstly, \\nOSC involves multiple levels, including the detailed design, production, manufacturing, \\nstorage, transportation, and installation of prefabricated components (PCs) to the con-\\nstruction site (Hsu, Aurisicchio, and Angeloudis 2020 ; Zhai, Zhong, and Huang 2015 ). Since \\nPCs are customised for each construction project, manufacturers cannot produce PCs in \\nadvance without receiving specific orders. Secondly, due to the large size and space \\nrequirements of PCs, contractors often face challenges maintaining a large inventory as \\na logistics buffer (Hsu, Aurisicchio, and Angeloudis 2020 ). Moreover, various risks affecting \\nlogistics efficiency exist at different stages of OSC (Wang, Hu, and Gong 2018 ). \\nImplementing a well-organised SCM in prefabricated factories contributes to achieving \\noverall operational efficiency in the supply chain (Ahn et al. 2022 ).\\nOSC logistics has various uncertain factors, such as off-site logistics processes, demand- \\nside and supply-side dynamics, planning and control systems, and environment consid -\\nerations (Yang et al. 2021 ). To address these uncertainties, scholars have conducted \\nextensive research. These studies include investigating the mismatch between logistics \\nand information flow in the supply chain of OSC (Bortolini, Torres Formoso, and Viana  \\n2019 ), optimising the configuration of prefabricated logistics (Hsu, Aurisicchio, and \\nAngeloudis 2020 ), reducing costs associated with prefabricated logistics (Yi et al. 2021 ; \\nYi, Wang, and Zhang 2020 ), and ensuring sustainable transportation of PCs from factories \\nto construction sites (Yi, Phipps, and Wang 2020 ). Moreover, the exploration of crucial \\ndecision-making issues pertaining to logistics and construction is conducted (Mohamed \\net al. 2022 ). The current studies in the field primarily concentrate on material and \\ninformation flows, overlooking the critical importance of business flow in the context of \\nOSC-SCM. In addition, several studies have highlighted the significance of engaging multi- \\nparty engagement and collaborative efforts at different stages to improve the overall \\noperational efficiency of OSC logistics (Daclin, Chen, and Vallespir 2016 ; Mohamed et al.  \\n2023 ; Xia et al. 2021 ; Zhang and Yu 2020 ). In conclusion, fostering collaboration among \\nvarious stakeholders is vital for optimising OSC-SCM, considering the associated business \\nflow along with material and information flows.\\n2.2. Cyber-physical system in supply chain management\\nThe construction supply chain operates in an unstructured environment with dynamic \\nprocesses and complex products requiring effective management and coordination. To ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 773\\naddress these challenges, the integration of cyber-physical systems (CPS) and other \\nemerging technologies in construction is significant (Mendhurwar and Mishra 2023 ; \\nWang et al. 2023 ). CPS establishes a connection between BIM models and on-site \\nassembly sensors, enabling simulation and optimisation capabilities (Wang and Zhang  \\n2021 ). Moreover, CPS can be coupled with blockchain technology to achieve additional \\nbenefits. For example, Jiang et al. (2021 ) propose a blockchain-enabled cyber-physical \\nsmart modular integrated construction platform to facilitate cross-enterprise information \\nsharing among multiple stakeholders. A systematic approach to cyber-physical-centred \\ndesign, incorporating blockchain, establishes relationships between users and their activ -\\nities within a collaborative environment, promoting efficient coordination (Xiao , Zhang, \\nand Zhong 2023 ). Overall, these approaches enable real-time information capture and \\nfoster collaboration among stakeholders.\\nPrevious studies have recognised the potential of CPS as a solution for SCM. For \\ninstance, Anumba et al. (2021 ) investigate the utilisation of CPS to address challenges in \\nconstruction project delivery. In particular, challenges of building component placement \\nand tracking, monitoring temporary structures, and ensuring mobile crane safety are \\nsolved. Jiang et al. (2021 ) propose a CPS-based safety management system. This system \\nfacilitates the synchronisation of risk data between virtual and physical construction sites, \\nincorporating modules for scene reconstruction design, data awareness, data commu -\\nnication, and data processing. Despite the proven effectiveness of CPS in addressing SCM \\nchallenges in various domains, its application in the context of OSC has received limited \\nattention.\\n2.3. DT in construction\\nDT technology is a simulation-based planning and optimisation method, aiming to \\nestablish a virtual representation or replica of a physical object, process, or system (Fei \\net al. 2018 ). It offers benefits throughout the entire life cycle of products by enabling real- \\ntime interaction and accurate mapping between virtual models and physical entities (Bao \\net al. 2019 ; Lin et al. 2021 ). In the construction industry, DT has good potential for \\napplication, spanning from the initial design phase to the eventual demolition stage \\n(Jiang et al. 2021 ; Yitmen et al. 2021 ).\\nIn the design and engineering stage, DTs help to combine information models and \\nphysical models of buildings, ensuring iterative optimisation of both models. The iterative \\noptimisation of models reduces the entire design process and minimises the potential for \\nadditional costs during rework (Li, Greenwood, and Kassem 2019 ). At the manufacturing \\nstage, most research on the application of DTs focuses on evaluating the structural \\nintegrity of the object. Esmaeili and Simeone (2023 ) conduct a study on the implementa -\\ntion of DTs in the construction stage of a project to support the decision-making and \\noperational processes of the general contractor. At the construction stage, DTs can also \\nassist in management activities, including resource management, material management, \\nschedule management, quality management, and sequence management. During the \\noperation and maintenance stages, the management and accessibility of building data \\ncan pose difficulties. Although virtual models offer precise representations of the objects, \\nthe operation and maintenance team often lack a direct connection to the actual \\nconstructed project (Anderl et al. 2018 ). During the demolition and restoration phases, 774\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nthe overlooked application of DT technology may lead to the unfortunate loss of valuable \\nknowledge. Li, Greenwood, and Kassem (2019 ) suggest that virtual space enables the \\nmaintenance of minimum cost by leveraging information from the decomposition and \\nrestoration phases, which encompass the entire life cycle.\\nIn summary, DTs provide significant management and decision-making value through -\\nout the life cycle of the construction industry. They are particularly well-suited for use in \\nthe supply chain of PCs, addressing challenges such as cross-stage collaboration (Zhou, \\nWei, and Peng 2021 ), information security (Lee et al. 2021 ), and efficiency improvement \\nLee and Lee 2021 ). DTs enable efficient and scalable collaboration among stakeholders, \\nbridging the virtual-physical gap in OSC supply chains. Therefore, DTs have tremendous \\npotential for enhancing every stage of OSC.\\n3.A platform for CPI based off-site construction supply chain management\\nThe CPI-OSCSCM platform, as shown in Figure 1, is structured into three layers. The core \\ndesign considerations for this platform are user’s needs and experiences. To achieve this, \\ncontinuous user engagement and feedback are fostered through the design, develop -\\nment, testing, and evaluation processes. Thereby, the iterative development facilitates the \\nentire construction project (Ellwein et al. 2021 ).\\nThe framework of CPI-enabled OSC-SCM is built upon the proposed outline of a project \\nsponsored by the Research Grants Council (Huang 2022 ). It contains three layers: the \\nscalable access layer, the kernel services layer, and the application layer. The foundation \\nlayer is the scalable access layer, which establishes connections with various equipment \\nand stakeholders. The intermediate layer is the kernel services layer, encompassing twin \\nprotocols, networking modules, incentive mechanisms, and secure sharing functionalities. \\nFinally, the application layer plays a crucial role in supporting different aspects of the \\noffsite plant, logistics process, and on-site assembly.\\n3.1. The scalable access layer\\nIn the construction supply chain, diverse stakeholders collaborate in various scenarios to \\nform a dynamic supply chain. The seamless integration of these stakeholders into the \\nplatform’s scalable access layer is achieved through a ‘plug and play’ approach (Lee, \\nBagheri, and Kao 2015 ), facilitated by the industrial driver and integrated information \\nchains. An industrial driver is a software-enabled device that enables communication and \\ncontrol between a system and industrial stakeholders, promoting data exchange and the \\nmanagement of industrial processes. Through the adoption of an industrial driver, stake -\\nholders can easily connect and interact, benefiting from perceptibility, configurability, and \\ninteroperability. A driver serves two main functions: identification and organisation. The \\ndriver employs a hierarchical naming rule to generate a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) \\nfor each stakeholder, enabling their recognition and identification. The first-level subdir -\\nectory of the URI is determined by the stakeholder’s properties, while the second-level \\nsubdirectory corresponds to the spatial properties. For example, a URI ‘Off-Site/GZ/OS’, as \\nan identifier for a stakeholder, denotes a supplier involved in OSC production in \\nGuangzhou city named ‘OS’. The URI’s uniqueness is automatically verified by the system’s \\nauthentication service. Additionally, the drivers also record and describe the extent of ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 775\\nservice through analysis, multiple layers of namespaces, firewalls, and sub-authentication \\nservers. The organiser, as part of its functions, manages and connects the stakeholders. \\nThese stakeholders are isolated entities with distinct URIs but are interconnected in the \\nlogistic process. The organisation function enables users to view and facilitate business \\nwithin the system. To organise the stakeholders effectively, a hierarchical tree structure is \\nemployed to sort and arrange them based on various criteria. When the identifier detects \\nthe stakeholder’s driver, the organiser locates and activates the corresponding driver. If \\nthe information of the stakeholders is already loaded, the organiser will call the driver to \\ninitialise it.\\nThe integrated information chains provide a comprehensive specification of data \\nexchange among logistics stakeholders. In OSC logistics systems, the transportation of \\nvarious PCs is a key aspect. This involves organising stakeholders and resources to ensure \\nefficient transportation in digital networks. The process of routing digital networks encom -\\npasses several steps. Firstly, optimisation objections for path planning are determined \\naccording to different scenarios. Next, the analysis focuses on off-site logistics constraints, \\nincluding component assembly, arrival time, and tool constraints. Finally, a routing algo -\\nrithm is designed to optimise the transportation path and delivery sequence. Through these \\nsteps, the integrated information chains streamline logistics operations.\\nThe integrated information chain is designed to describe the information flow struc -\\nture of the working mechanisms in the logistics process. This chain comprises a meta-data \\ngraph structure containing three functional units: nodes, edges, and global information. \\nOff-site plantMaintenance \\nand upkeep\\nOn-site constructionLogisticsThe kernel \\nservices layerIncentive mechanism of digital twin partnership\\nKernel services of digital twin networks\\nTwin protocols of digital-twin networks Information \\nsharing \\nmechanismThe scalable \\naccess layerThe integrated information chains Industrial driverThe \\napplication \\nlayerCyber-physicalinternet platform\\nOptimization Smart scheduling Decision-making…\\nData& control Digital twin \\nnetworkDT unit\\nDT unitDT unitOff-site construction supply chain managementFigure 1. The framework of proposed platform for CPI-OSCSCM.776\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nNodes act as placeholders, populated by the source unit code from the digital resource \\nchain. Nodes are associated with unique encodings linked to the metadata, rather than \\nthe metadata themselves. These nodes represent entities or stakeholders in the physical \\nlogistics chain. The edges are vectors that describe the relationships between nodes \\nbased on the business interaction between two stakeholders. These connections are \\ncharacterised by a one-to-many relationship, connecting related nodes using unique \\nkeys. The collection of nodes and edges contributes to the global information related to \\nphysical units in DT. The graph structure for information flow allows for adjustments and \\nupdates for functional units to accommodate changes in the technological process or \\nrescheduled resource units. The reconstruction of graph structure ensures the information \\nchain is neatly reconfigurable and learnable.\\n3.2. The kernel services layer\\n3.2.1. Twin protocols of DT networks\\nThe protocols of the DT networks define the communication rules for different DT units in \\nthe OSC supply chain. These protocols contain three levels: static, dynamic, and predic -\\ntion. At the static level, protocols define two aspects. Firstly, guidelines for the geometry \\nmodel derived from real-life objects, encompassing vehicles, PCs, and buildings, are \\nestablished. Secondly, the information interfaces between different DT models are \\ndefined. These enable the description of the mating relationship of building components \\nand the interoperability between different DT models. Based on the static level of \\nprotocols, the meta-twin system can incorporate different 3D BIM, charts, and schematics \\nto construct a static world without relying on real-time data. At the dynamic level, \\nprotocols define operational regulations for DT models. These protocols fully utilise the \\ntemporal dimension by integrating past, present, and future factors to accurately evaluate \\nchanges in logistics metrics. By combining known variables with operation laws based on \\nphysics, it becomes possible to predict operational indicators in specific logistics scenar -\\nios. Furthermore, incorporating real-time data as input enhances the simulation precision. \\nThe protocols at the dynamic level ensure operational effectiveness across all twins in the \\nsupply chain. Lastly, the protocols at the prediction level establish the format for invoking \\nartificial intelligence (AI) models to optimise operations. This level considers the utilisation \\nof various AI models tailored to specific logistics scenarios. The establishment of an \\ninterface for invoking different algorithms has been carried out to facilitate the develop -\\nment of advanced predictive twins in OSC-SCM.\\n3.2.2. Kernel services of DT networks\\nThe kernel services of DT networks are designed to serve the public and provide rich data \\nservices and functional development interfaces. These services are categorised into four \\naspects: Firstly, the data services encompass a wide range of services for DT bodies. This \\nincludes unified management and monitoring of services, ensuring efficient handling of \\ndifferent DT entities. Next, the data weaving service allows for flexible combination, \\nediting, and weaving of DT body properties. It enables customisation and integration of \\ncontent and types. Then, the function service provides advanced features such as DOM \\nchange monitoring, underground pipe network collision analysis, location-based big data \\nanalysis, etc. Additionally, the algorithm capability service provides specialised analysis ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 777\\nsuch as collision avoidance, pedestrian flow, traffic flow, and other simulation and \\ndeduction capabilities. These services comply with data security classification and user \\nauthentication provisions, ensuring a unified standard for data and functional services. \\nThe kernel services also provide low-code scenario-building capabilities to support busi -\\nness personnel in participating in scenario innovation and construction. Thus, the DT units \\ncan adapt to multi-industry, multi-scene, multi-topic invocation and management \\ninterfaces.\\n3.2.3. Incentive mechanism of DT partnership\\nThe DT forms a digital asset catalogue to facilitate the digital management of SCM in the \\nconstruction industry. As a multi-disciplinary product, building a DT is cost-intensive. \\nHence, the incentive mechanism of DT partnerships is critical to building a healthy \\necosystem, which contains five aspects as follows:\\n(1) Revenue sharing : One frequently employed incentive is revenue sharing. It entails \\ndistributing the revenue generated from the sale of DT services or products among \\npartners. The revenue sharing can be set based on a predetermined percentage or \\nmodified based on each partner’s contribution.\\n(2) Cost sharing : In some cases, partners agree to share the costs of developing and \\nmaintaining the DT. This may include the expenses of software development, \\nhardware infrastructure, data storage, and maintenance. The cost sharing can be \\nset based on a predetermined percentage or modified based on each partner’s \\ncontribution.\\n(3) Performance-based incentives : Partners’ incentives can be contingent upon the \\nperformance of the DT. This could involve establishing performance benchmarks, \\nsuch as uptime, accuracy, and efficiency. And rewards for partners are applied \\nwhen they successfully meet these benchmarks.\\n(4) Intellectual property sharing : Another arrangement that partners may consider is the \\nsharing of intellectual property related to the DT. This can entail co-owning any \\nassociated patents or copyrights or sharing the right to utilise the technology in \\nspecific circumstances.\\n(5) Reputational and branding benefits : Partners may also reap the benefits of \\nenhanced branding and reputation through a DT alliance. This may include \\nincreased visibility, bolstering credibility within the industry, expanding the client \\nbase, or venturing into new markets.\\nUltimately, the incentives will vary based on the specific goals and objectives of the DT \\npartnerships, as well as their interests and needs.\\n3.2.4. Timely and secure information sharing mechanism of DTs\\nThe information sharing mechanism relies heavily on digital identity management. Digital \\nidentity refers to the process of encoding real-time user identity and attribute information \\ninto machine-readable data using digital methods. This enables the identification and \\nquerying of user identities in cyberspace. It drives the identity of cyber entities, governing \\nactivities ranging from hosting to autonomous management. The evolution of the digital \\nidentity model from ‘centralised’ to ‘federated’ to ‘distributed’ reflects the need for 778\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nimproved interoperability, portability, identity autonomy, security, and privacy. By imple -\\nmenting a distributed screenshot network, digital identities become identifiable and \\nefficiently managed. It eliminates reliance on a centralised platform for identity registra -\\ntion, verification, and authorisation. This permits a safe and reliable transfer of data usage \\nby giving users confirmed control over their identities.\\nTraceable, controllable, and fine-grained authentication is the foundation of digital \\nidentity management. It permits the interactive behaviour of digital identity across all \\nsegments of the network-wide circulation process. This authorisation needs to be verified \\nby the distributed identity mark held by the user. Since the generation of identity markers \\nhas global uniqueness, the user’s behaviour in the digital world has traceability. At the \\nsame time, each user subject can hold multiple identities to facilitate the decision to select \\na specific identity role flexibly in different application platforms and scenarios. Distributed \\ndigital identity technology ensures that identity data remains under the control of end- \\nusers. It facilitates the trusted exchange of verifiable credentials among issuers, holders, \\nand verifiers through authorisation. This approach guarantees the integrity, authority, and \\nprivacy protection of the credentials.\\n3.3. The application layer\\nThe application layer of the CPI-enabled platform is depicted in Figure 2. In the lifecycle of \\nthe OSC supply chain, a large amount of data is generated from operational activities and \\nthe ubiquitous environment. To enhance the robustness and efficiency of SC operations, \\nthe platform incorporates big data technology, enabling enhanced intelligence. In the \\nOSC-SCM, data intelligence finds application in three aspects: logistics in prefabrication \\nplants, prefabricated logistics, and logistics in on-site assembly. The subsequent section \\nprovides specific details about these applications.\\n3.3.1. The applications in offsite plant\\nThe application in the prefabrication plant illustrates how data intelligence promotes \\nlogistics in the manufacturing process. It encompasses design and planning, production \\nand manufacturing, quality control and inspection, and maintenance and upkeep.\\n3.3.1.1. Design and planning. Designers and planners can simulate and optimise the \\nplant’s layout, workflow, and logistics by using DT technology to construct a virtual \\nversion of the actual facility. DT technology assists in locating and resolving possible \\ndesign problems prior to the commencement of construction. Thus, the possibility of \\nmistakes and delays throughout the building process might decrease. In addition, DT \\ntechnology can also be used to plan and optimise the logistics of PCs. By simulating the \\ntransportation and installation of PCs, designers and planners can identify time and \\nresource constraints, ensuring process efficiency.\\n3.3.1.2. Production and manufacturing. DT technology can be used to simulate and \\noptimise the production and manufacturing processes within plants. By creating a virtual \\nreplica of the production line, manufacturers can simulate different scenarios and proac -\\ntively identify potential bottlenecks and inefficiencies. Furthermore, DT technology \\nenables real-time monitoring of machines and equipment performance. Manufacturers ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 779\\nFigure 2. CPI enabled application layer.780\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\ncan collect machine and equipment performance data by utilising sensors and analytics. \\nThis data can be leveraged to optimise the production process and improve overall plant \\nefficiency.\\n3.3.1.3. Quality control and inspection. Prefabrication plants’ quality assurance and \\ninspection procedures can be strengthened with the use of DT technology. By creating \\na digital replica of the PCs, manufacturers can simulate and test the components prior to \\ntheir actual production. This can help with the early detection of potential issues, con-\\ntributing to contentious quality improvements. Additionally, DT technology allows for \\nreal-time monitoring of PC quality, enabling swift detection and resolution of defects \\nthrough advanced sensors and analytics. This proactive approach ensures prompt recti -\\nfication of any shortcomings, safeguarding the project’s overall quality and integrity.\\n3.3.1.4. Maintenance and upkeep. DT technology improves prefabrication plant main -\\ntenance through virtual replicas for real-time machine monitoring. Through a virtual \\nreplica of the plant, manufacturers gain the capability to conduct online monitoring of \\nmachines and equipment performance, enabling the prediction of potential failures. This \\nproactive approach not only minimises downtime but also mitigates the risk of expensive \\nrepairs. In addition, leveraging DT technology allows for the optimisation of maintenance \\nschedules by analysing comprehensive data on machine and equipment performance. \\nManufacturers can proactively conduct schedule maintenance and repairs to minimise \\ndisruptions and minimise operational productivity.\\n3.3.2. The applications in logistics process\\nThe applications in the logistics process provide scenarios in the handling and transporta -\\ntion process, which contain transport route planning, transport status monitoring, load \\nlayout optimisation, transportation cost analysis, and PC traceability.\\n3.3.2.1. Transport route planning. DT technology can be used to improve the trans -\\nport route planning process by providing an accurate and comprehensive view of the \\ntransportation network. Transportation-affected data, such as traffic conditions, weather, \\nand other factors, is collected via sensors for analysis of driving speed and vehicle wear. \\nDT technology can also be used to simulate different scenarios and test different options, \\nwhich can help identify the most cost-effective method.\\n3.3.2.2. Transport status monitoring. By leveraging DT technology, transport status \\nmonitoring can undergo substantial enhancement through virtual replication of the \\nphysical transport system. This enables real-time tracking, predictive analytics, improved \\ncollaboration and communication, and enhanced visualisation and reporting capabilities. \\nSuch insights are instrumental in optimising the transport process, identifying improve -\\nment measures, improving customer satisfaction, and enhancing risk management. \\nFinally, DT technology has great potential for exception prediction in transportation.\\n3.3.2.3. Load layout optimisation. DT technology enhances load layout optimisation in \\ntrucks through the creation of a virtual replica of both the truck and its cargo. This \\nempowers logistics managers to simulate different cargo layouts, optimising loading to ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 781\\nmaximise volume, weight distribution, and stability. This can lead to improved safety in the \\ntransport process, mitigation of cargo damage risk, and a reduction in transportation costs. \\nOverall, DT technology has great potential to improve the detail optimisation process.\\n3.3.2.4. Transportation cost analysis. DT technology can be used to improve transpor -\\ntation cost analysis in construction logistics by creating a virtual replica of the construction \\nsite and the transport network. By implementing this technology, logistics managers can \\nsimulate different transportation scenarios and optimise the transport process to minimise \\ncosts associated with fuel consumption, labour, and vehicle maintenance. This improves the \\nefficiency and effectiveness of the logistics process, reduces costs, and bolsters profitability. \\nTherefore, DT technology ensures smoother operations in logistics.\\n3.3.2.5. PC traceability. DT technology can enhance PC traceability by creating a virtual \\nreplica of the PCs and the entire transport network. Through DT, logistics managers can \\ntrack the real-time movement of PCs, starting from the plant to the city hubs, then \\nreaching the construction sites. Traceability ensures the precise and punctual delivery \\nof PCs to the correct location. Additionally, DT facilitates the optimisation of the trans -\\nportation process, including route identification, loading arrangements, and PC transfer \\ntimes. These capabilities contribute to reducing costs and improving efficiency within the \\nlogistics process as a whole. Consequently, DT enhances traceability, further improving \\ncustomer satisfaction in construction logistics.\\n3.3.3. The applications of on-site assembly\\nThis section provides scenarios on the construction site, which include safety monitoring, \\nprocess management, stakeholders’ collaboration, and optimisation of on-site logistics.\\n3.3.3.1. On-site safety monitoring. DT technology presents significant benefits in opti -\\nmising on-site safety monitoring in construction across three key aspects: hazard identifica -\\ntion, training and education, and predictive maintenance. a) Through simulation and \\nanalysis, a DT can proactively identify potential hazards and risks. Real-time monitoring \\nand simulation of worker location, equipment usage, and environmental factors allow for \\nthe implementation of preventive measures and safety protocols, mitigating the risk of \\naccidents and injuries. b) A DT can create virtual training and education environments for \\nworkers and stakeholders. It improves safety awareness and knowledge, as well as provid -\\ning risk-free opportunities to practice safety protocols. c) A DT can monitor the condition of \\nequipment and machinery on the construction site, identifying potential issues in advance. \\nIt facilitates scheduling maintenance and repairs as necessary. This proactive approach \\nhelps to minimise the risk of equipment failure and accidents attributed to faulty machinery.\\n3.3.3.2. Construction process management. DT technology offers numerous benefits \\nfor optimising the construction process, involving improvements in planning and design, \\nresource allocation, and scheduling and coordination. a) The utilisation of DT enables the \\ncreation of a virtual model of a construction project, allowing stakeholders to simulate \\nand analyse various design options and construction scenarios. This aids in the identifica -\\ntion of potential issues and improving the planning and design processes. b) DT provides \\ntransparent visualisation of resources such as materials, equipment, and labour on the 782\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nconstruction site. This transparency empowers stakeholders to optimise resource alloca -\\ntion, reduce waste, and mitigate inefficiencies. c) The process timeline can be quantised \\nand analysed with the DT, including the task scheduling and the coordination of the team \\nand stakeholders. This analysis assists in identifying potential conflicts and delays, ulti-\\nmately optimising the construction process.\\n3.3.3.3. Construction stakeholders’ collaboration. DT technology facilitates collabora -\\ntion among different construction stakeholders. This encompasses coordination, stream -\\nlined lifecycle management, and seamless cooperation with remote teams. a) DT enables \\nreal-time sharing of information and data related to construction stakeholders. \\nThroughout the collaborative process, stakeholders can increase communication and \\ncoordination by exchanging information, specifications, and requirements. b) DTs can \\nbe utilised at any stage of their lifecycle, including maintenance and monitoring. Through \\nconsistent observation of DTs’ performance and state, stakeholders can anticipate possi -\\nble maintenance requirements in advance, resulting in better maintenance scheduling \\nand financial savings. c) Remote collaboration among geographically dispersed teams is \\nrealised. Stakeholders can access and interact with DTs from anywhere.\\n3.3.3.4. On-site logistics optimisation. DT technology can optimise on-site logistics in \\nconstruction projects in three aspects: simulation and planning, resource optimisation, \\nand safety management. a) A simulation and plan of logistics operations can be con-\\nducted by creating a virtual replica of the construction site and infrastructure. This enables \\nstakeholders to test various scenarios, optimise workflows, and determine the most \\neffective logistics procedures, minimising material handling and transportation costs. b) \\nDTs provide a platform for optimising the allocation and utilisation of resources on-site. \\nThis can be realised through the integration of data pertaining to worker schedules, \\nequipment utilisation, and material availability. Moreover, effectively allocating resources \\nensures efficient material flow and minimises idle time, resulting in decreased delays and \\nincreased productivity. c) In on-site logistics, DTs are essential in recognising and reducing \\nsafety hazards. By integrating safety data, including hazard zones, equipment proximity \\nsensors, and worker activity monitoring, stakeholders can proactively identify potential \\nhazards and implement preventive measures. This enhances worker safety and reduces \\nthe likelihood of accidents or incidents.\\n4.Case study\\nThis section describes a case study of a construction supply chain to illustrate the \\nimplementation of the CPI-based SC management platform framework, aiming to achieve \\ndigitalisation and synchronisation. This case study focuses on a high-rise construction \\nproject located in Hubei Province, China. The project comprises a frame-shear structure \\nand a prefabricated structure. The total construction area is 270,000 square metres and \\nhas a duration of over 600 days. The prefabricated buildings cover an area of approxi -\\nmately 40,000 square metres, with a single building height of 97 metres and a total of 32 \\nfloors. The top three prefabricated structures in this project are laminated plates (45 \\ntypes), prefabricated shear walls (19 types), and prefabricated external filled walls (20 \\ntypes). Table 1 provides selected parameters for some prefabricated components.ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 783\\n4.1. Project challenges\\n4.1.1. Information synchronization among stakeholders\\nThe off-site construction of high-rise buildings presents unique challenges, including the \\nneed for stringent safety measures, optimal space utilisation, and well-planned equip -\\nment arrangements. For effective communication among stakeholders, it is crucial to \\nmaintain consistent and structured project information. A comprehensive understanding \\nof the project’s complexity and potential construction contingencies enables stakeholders \\nwith different roles to closely monitor the construction schedule. Moreover, information \\nsynchronisation helps bridge gaps resulting from frequent personnel changes and evol -\\nving requirements. This prevents the need for costly trial-and-error approaches.\\n4.1.2. Logistics management\\nTransportation and lifting operations pose significant challenges for logistics manage -\\nment. These processes span multiple stages, encompassing movement from factories to \\nlogistics centres and eventually to construction sites. This raises concerns regarding the \\ntimely and accurate delivery of materials. In this project, each PC is customised for the \\nbuilding construction process, necessitating the use of cranes for loading, unloading, and \\ntransportation. Ensuring the identification and traceability of each PC is crucial for main -\\ntaining reliable construction scheduling. In addition, the detailed specifications, such as \\nlifting points and transportation loading schemes, require extensive technological invol -\\nvement and coordination.\\n4.1.3. Challenges in construction of PCs\\nCompared to traditional construction, OSC entails more rigorous requirements, especially \\nconcerning the workers’ skills and adherence to the construction schedule. The installa -\\ntion of PCs demands higher levels of precision and accuracy. Workers are expected to \\npossess a broader range of skills, such as electrical work, welding, cutting, drilling, and \\nassembly, as well as proficiency in operating various tools and equipment. Moreover, PCs \\nare typically manufactured off-site and subsequently rapidly assembled and installed on- \\nsite. Therefore, the workers responsible for PC installation must adapt to an accelerated \\nconstruction pace and strictly adhere to tight schedules. Failure to effectively manage \\nthese challenges can result in rework and compromise the overall construction quality.\\n4.2. System illustration\\n4.2.1. The access layer of construction supply chain\\nIn this case, the platform connects different resources through the IDs. The captured \\nprocess data from all equipment is interconnected to build a comprehensive digital Table 1. Typical PC parameters.\\nNo. Name Size (mm) Weight (t)\\nYYB1L composite floor slab 2220*1420 0.75\\nYYB2L composite floor slab 2220*1420 0.75\\nYYB3L composite floor slab 3510*1670 1.13\\nYLT1 Precast stairs 2980*1225 1.85\\nYTQ1R Prefabricated exterior wall 2000*200 1.7784\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nthread throughout the construction lifecycle, serving as a vital component of the systems. \\nAs shown in Figure 3, the digital resource chain integrates all resource units in the OSC \\nsupply chain, facilitating easy management and reconfiguration when the resource units \\nare modified in response to evolving business requirements. Resource units include panel \\nsaws, beam saws, edge banding machines, forklifts, trucks, excavators, mobile cranes, and \\nmore. In each branch, the corresponding industry driver, attribute data, and real-time \\nstate are connected with the essential resource units in building construction processing.\\n4.2.2. The DT enabled system\\nThe DT-enabled system is developed on an Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) layer, consisting \\nof four parts. The first module incorporates the twin protocols of the digital-twin networks, \\nenabling access, integration, and interoperability of different digital models. The second \\nmodule comprises the kernel service of DT networks, containing functionalities such as data \\nimporting, data governance, and data fusion. This module facilitates effective data utilisa -\\ntion and ensures the DT model is enriched with real-time data. The third module focused on \\nthe incentive mechanism of the DT partnership. Lastly, the fourth module is dedicated to \\ninformation sharing, ensuring the timely and secure exchange of DTs.\\n4.2.3. The application layer of construction supply chain\\nThe system addresses the mentioned challenges with a DT-enabled system in three \\nstages, namely the manufacturing process, transportation process, and assembly process, \\nas shown in Figure 4. Nine application scenarios are displayed with modelling and the \\nsystems’ screenshots.\\nThe solutions in the manufacturing process are mainly reflected in three steps. The first \\nstep involves leveraging DT to disassemble the designed building model parameters into \\nproducible components that meet design conformity. This results in detailed design \\ndrawings for the PC and template. Through a DT-based system, collaborative efforts \\ninvolving design institutes, manufacturers, and construction companies can be made. \\nThe proposed evolution and communication services promote the realisation of avoiding \\npossible construction problems, such as collision and incompatibility. Additionally, the \\nDT-based system enables the reservation of embedded positions on the PC for electrical \\nand air conditioning equipment through linkage modification.\\nThe second part revolves around production progress control based on logistics data \\nand the production schedule. Logistics data refers to the inventory at the production site, \\ntrucks in transit, and the construction site. These data are tracked by the RFID and QR code \\nattached to the PC obtained from the production factory. Moreover, an overall progress \\nKanban can filter the production progress data from the perspective of components, floors, \\nand different buildings. These data facilitate the coordination of multi-stage processes.\\nThe third part concentrates on the optimisation of manufacturing through the applica -\\ntion of DT. The DT of the building model enables simulation and prediction of the \\nfabrication process, allowing for optimisation of process factors in accordance with design \\nrequirements. One example is the improvement of layouts involving steel bars within PCs, \\naddressing strength requirements. Furthermore, when assessing component assembly, \\nfitness measurement is critical to quality control. Leveraging the digital model, the \\nanalysis of size deviation among thousands of parts ensures compatibility and \\ncomposability.ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 785\\nThe logistics process with a DT system involves typical operations: inventory manage -\\nment, loading and unloading schemes, and transportation management. Firstly, inventory \\nmanagement contains the storage strategy for components at the production site, in on- \\nroad trucks, and at the construction site. Utilising the DT models of PCs, the required \\nquantity of components can be accurately determined. This aspect is critical for main -\\ntaining safety and optimising stock levels. Specifically, in on-site management, improve -\\nments can be made to the layout of storage space due to the yard space limitation and \\nirregular component shapes. With the DT models, geometric space optimisation can be \\nconducted to minimise hoisting time and reduce area waste while considering safety \\ndistance and bearing capacity constraints.\\nSecondly, in the loading and unloading process for trucks, the DT systems play \\na significant role in influencing the choice of vehicle types and optimising the loading \\nscheme. When tackling a typical transportation task, the required space can be accurately \\ncalculated through space simulation and analysis using the DT models. DT models enable \\nthe conduct of stress analysis and dimension analysis, which results in the optimisation of \\nvehicle balance, physical dimension, turning radius, carrying capacity, etc.\\nFigure 3. The integrated information chains in case study.786\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nAdditionally, a DT model can be used to ensure process safety in transportation \\nmanagement in two aspects: virtual testing and simulation, and driver assistance systems. \\nIn the service of virtual testing and simulation, DTs allow for the evaluation of various \\ndriving scenarios, including hazardous conditions or emergencies, by conducting virtual \\ntests and simulation. By replicating real-world driving environments, DTs assess how \\nvehicles and their safety systems respond to different situations. For the drivers, DTs \\nassist in road planning by accounting for the long wheelbase and high centre of trucks \\nacross different road widths and slopes. Moreover, DTs can integrate with advanced driver \\nassistance systems to improve driver awareness and response. By combining real-time \\nvehicle data with environmental information, such as traffic conditions, weather, and road \\nhazards, DTs can provide personalised alerts and warnings. In some cases, DTs even \\nassume control of specific vehicle functions to prevent accidents.\\nAt the assembly site, the DT models have been designed to promote efficiency and safety \\nin the hoisting process. This focuses on three aspects: planning, safety monitoring, and \\ncoordination. a) The utilisation of a DT model of the construction site and hoisting equip -\\nment can virtually plan and simulate hoisting operations. This allows for identifying poten -\\ntial issues, testing hoisting sequences, and optimising workflows. b) Virtual detection of \\nerrors and collisions is possible. Real-time data from sensors installed on cranes and hoisting \\nequipment is integrated into the DT. The function of DT encompasses monitoring of load \\nlevels, equipment positions, movement speeds, etc. Any deviations from safe operating \\nparameters can trigger alerts, facilitating the early detection of risky or improperly executed \\nlifts. c) A shared DT environment provides stakeholders with simultaneous access to con-\\nstruction progress and hoisting plans. This fosters collaborative viewing and enables col-\\nlective resolution of issues without requiring on-site presence.\\nSecondly, for the installation quality inspection in the assembly process, DT is \\nemployed, including two aspects: virtual replication and clash detection. a) DTs generates \\nManufacturing process\\nPC manufacturingProgress controlDetailed design drawingsLogistics process\\nInventory management  \\nLoading and unloading\\nTransportationAssembly Process\\nInstallation quality inspectionHoisting process\\nGrouting 1\\n2\\n34\\n5\\n67\\n8\\n9Figure 4. DT enable OSC supply chain implementations.ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 787\\na virtual replica of the physical construction project, including all its components and \\nsystems. The installation process of buildings can be examined and visualised by inspec -\\ntors. This enables proactive remedial action by identifying any problems, conflicts, or \\nmistakes early on. b) DTs utilising BIM produce a comprehensive 3D model of the \\nconstruction project. This virtual model is employed to clash detection, identifying con-\\nflicts among building systems (e.g. plumbing, HVAC, electrical). Early conflict detection \\nenables to ensure accurate installation quality and prevent costly rework.\\nThirdly, during the assembly process, grouting operations are highly necessary. The \\napplication of DT technology has been instrumental in enhancing the grouting process by \\nproviding a more comprehensive view. This is accomplished through sensors that capture \\ncrucial grouting data, such as grout temperature, pressure, and flow rate. Subsequently, \\nscenario prediction and testing for diverse grouting methods are carried out for the \\nimprovement of quality and cost reduction.\\n4.3. Performance improvements and discussion\\nThe performance improvements are analysed to verify the feasibility of the proposed CPI- \\nenabled SCM platform. Table 2 shows several benefits obtained from the implementation \\nof OSC-SCM. Firstly, the CPI-enabled SCM platform enhances production efficiency by \\n13.7%, resulting in a construction period that is 20% shorter than planned. Secondly, the \\nimplementation of CPI ensures 100% visibility and traceability of major project resources, \\nenabling cross-platform management of PC parts in quality control, cost analysis, and \\nschedule simulation. Thirdly, the CPI-OSCSCM saved CNY 2 million (Chinese Yuan, CNY) in \\nlease charges for tower cranes, climbing frames, and aluminium moulds due to the \\nreduction of the construction period. Additionally, material costs, including concrete \\nand rolled steel, have been reduced by CNY 1.5 million.\\nThe utilisation of the proposed platform also contributes to information sharing and \\nenhances technological accessibility in CSC. On the one hand, the platform improves \\ninformation granularity management through hierarchical material standardisation and \\nthe implementation of CPI systems. Specifically, the material data hierarchy is structured \\ninto three layers: parts, components, and buildings. Each layer could be divided into sub- \\ncategories, such as building components, electromechanical components, and decorative \\ncomponents within the components layer. This hierarchical approach enables effective \\ndata comparison and validation among contracts, building schedules, and construction \\nprogress. This further ensures a reduction in project timelines, cost effectiveness, optimal \\nresource allocation, and enhanced work efficiency.\\nOn the other hand, the CPI-OSCSCM framework provides a blueprint for enhanced \\nindustrial technology access. The proposed framework addresses the requirements for \\nconnectivity, interoperability, and real-time data exchange among various components in \\nTable 2. Statistical analysis.\\nItem Benefits\\nImprove construction efficiency 13.7%\\nReduce construction period 20%\\nVisibility and Traceability 100%\\nSave lease charge (CNY) 2 million\\nLessen material cost (CNY) 1.5 million788\\n L. YIN ET AL.\\nthe SCM system. Stakeholders utilise the URL as the identifier, allowing for flexible and \\nseamless access to the framework. This framework not only facilitates streamlined project \\nexecution without significant changes but also mitigates deviations in materials, quan -\\ntities, and costs. The successful implementation of the framework outlines a roadmap for \\nthe integration of intelligent technologies such as AI-driven automation, robotic systems, \\nand AI data analytics in OSC-SCM.\\n5.Conclusion and future work\\nThe paper proposes a CPI platform designed to support the operation of OSC supply \\nchains. The platform comprises three layers: the scalable layer, the kernel service layer, \\nand the application layer. In the scalable layer, various resource units are interconnected \\nthrough IDs, establishing an information chain that facilitates the flow of information. This \\nlayer enables the active participation of different resource units, promoting efficient \\ncommunication and coordination. The kernel service layer focuses on designing protocols \\nfor building DTs and twin networks. DTs represent virtual versions of physical assets or \\nprocesses, while twin networks connect these DTs. By utilising DTs and twin networks, the \\nplatform enhances the visibility, control, and optimisation of SC operations. To create \\na favourable ecosystem for stakeholders, the paper proposes incentive and information- \\nsharing mechanisms on the platform. These mechanisms foster transparency and \\naccountability among the stakeholders, facilitating improved collaboration and efficiency. \\nIn the application layer, the paper introduces nine different scenarios that demonstrate \\nthe practical implementation of the platform. These scenarios encompass various aspects \\nof OSC, including off-site plant operations, logistics processes, and on-site assembly. \\nThrough these scenarios, the paper aims to showcase the versatility and effectiveness \\nof the platform in different construction contexts.\\nThe proposed system can be applied throughout the different stages of OSC-SCM. \\nIt serves the stakeholders involved in OSC projects, including owners, designers, \\nsuppliers, construction teams, and facility managers. In the design stage, the platform \\nsupports collaborative design and virtual modelling, optimising logistics flow and \\nmaterial handling. During the procurement and supply stage, it facilitates supplier \\nmanagement, inventory control, and efficient transportation planning. In the con-\\nstruction stage, the platform enables real-time tracking of materials and equipment, \\nensuring timely delivery and minimising disruptions. By leveraging these applica -\\ntions, the platform promotes seamless coordination and communication among \\nstakeholders in the supply chain.\\nThis paper addresses the need for enhanced OSC-SCM in construction projects. The \\nplatform’s functionality and implementation findings strongly support the value and \\nbenefits of adopting the CPI platform in OSC-SCM. In future research, the application of \\nblockchain technology for stakeholders’ data sharing will be explored to construct \\na smarter construction SCM system platform. Then, the connection between different \\nstakeholders and the guarantee of data security represent a critical area for future inves -\\ntigation. Future research will delve deeper into the mechanisms through which stakeholder \\ncollaboration can enhance security protocols and the role of emerging technologies in \\nfortifying data protection. In addition, efforts will concentrate on expanding the number of \\ncase studies for system testing, allowing for a robust evaluation of the system’s adaptability ENTERPRISE INFORMATION SYSTEMS\\n 789\\nand the collection of extensive user feedback. Such practical applications are anticipated to \\nyield crucial insights into the system’s performance in real-world scenarios, thereby facil -\\nitating improvements and promoting its widespread adoption within the industry.\\nAcknowledgments\\nAcknowledgement is given to RGC GRF (17203620), Public Policy Research Funding (2022. \\nA8.129.22C), Guangdong Special Support Talent Program—Innovation and Entrepreneurship \\nLeading Team under Grant 2019BT02S593, RGC Research Impact Fund (R7036-22) and RGC Theme- \\nbased Research Scheme (T32-707-22-N).\\nDisclosure statement\\nNo potential conflict of interest was reported by the author(s).\\nFunding\\nThe work was supported by the RGC Research Impact Fund RGC Theme-based Research Scheme \\nPublic Policy Research Funding Guangdong Special Support Talent Program—Innovation and \\nEntrepreneurship Leading Team under Grant Research Grants Council, University Grants Committee.\\nReferences\\nAhn, S. J., S. Han, M. Sadiq Altaf, and M. Al-Hussein. 2022 . “Integrating Off-Site and On-Site Panelized \\nConstruction Schedules Using Fleet Dispatching.” Automation in Construction 137:104201.  \\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.autcon.2022.104201  .\\nAlmashaqbeh, M., and K. El-Rayes. 2021 . “Optimizing the Prefabrication Finishing Level in Modular \\nConstruction.” Canadian Journal of Civil Engineering 48 (11): 1534–1540. https://doi.org/10.1139/ \\ncjce-2020-034  .\\nAnderl, R., S. Haag, K. Schützer, and E. Zancul. 2018 . “Digital Twin Technology–An Approach for \\nIndustrie 4.0 Vertical and Horizontal Lifecycle Integration.” IT-Information Technology 60 (3): \\n125–132. https://doi.org/10.1515/itit-2017-0038  .\\nAnumba, C. J., A. Akanmu, X. Yuan, and C. Kan. 2021 . “Cyber-Physical Systems Development for \\nConstruction Applications.” Frontiers of Engineering Management 8 (1): 72–87. https://doi.org/10. \\n1007/s42524-020-0130-4  .\\nArashpour, M., R. Wakefield, N. Blismas, and T. Maqsood. 2015 . “Autonomous Production Tracking \\nfor Augmenting Output in Off-Site Construction.” Automation in Construction 53:13–21. https:// \\ndoi.org/10.1016/j.autcon.2015.03.013  .\\nBao, J., D. Guo, J. Li, and J. Zhang. 2019 . “The Modelling and Operations for the Digital Twin in the \\nContext of Manufacturing.” Enterprise Information Systems 13 (4): 534–556. https://doi.org/10. \\n1080/17517575.2018.1526324  .\\nBataglin, F. S., D. D. Viana, C. T. Formoso, and I. R. Bulhões. 2020 . “Model for Planning and Controlling \\nthe Delivery and Assembly of Engineer-To-Order Prefabricated Building Systems: Exploring \\nSynergies Between Lean and BIM.” Canadian Journal of Civil Engineering 47 (2): 165–1