  "status": "success",
  "output": "{\n  \"content\": \" \\n1 \\n  \\n \\nAbstract—The intrinsic stochasticity of manufacturing is \\none of the main factors that hinder system resilience. Planning \\nand scheduling problems are typical examples plagued by \\nuncertainties such as stochastic processing time, arrivals of \\nnew orders, and breakdowns of workstations. Frequent \\nuncertainties disturb the workflow, and their cascading effects \\ncreate chaos in the whole system. The transparency and \\ntraceability analytics in Cyber-Physical Manufacturing \\nSystems (CPMS) bring new hope to tackle uncertainties. \\nInspired by the core spirit of Out-of-Order (OoO) Execution \\nin CPU, this paper proposes a novel OoO architecture for \\nresilient planning and scheduling in CPMS with real-time data \\nanalytics. Following OoO principles, multi-level instruction \\nqueues are constructed, under which manufacturing \\noperations (instructions) are sequenced and performed by \\nanalyzing real-time dependencies and executability. This \\nstudy contributes a new perspective to enhance decision \\nresilience using real-time data in CPMS. Results validate the \\neffectiveness and resilience of OoO under different levels of \\nuncertainty, showing improvements in the on-time delivery \\nrate and reductions in the average order flow time.  \\n \\nNote to Practitioners—This study addresses the challenge of \\nplanning and scheduling in cyber-physical manufacturing \\nsystems under uncertain environments. Traditional planning \\nand scheduling methods often struggle to adapt to real-world \\nuncertainties, resulting in decreased efficiency and stability. \\nTo this end, an Out-of-Order (OoO) architecture is proposed, \\nwhich dynamically organizes operations based on real-time \\nexecutability. This approach is well suited for manufacturing \\nenvironments that often face disruptions, such as discrete \\nmanufacturing, electronic assembly, or high-mix, low-volume \\nproduction. These settings frequently experience issues such \\nas demand changes, machine breakdowns, and material \\nshortages. By leveraging real-time data and dynamic queuing \\nmechanisms, OoO helps reduce order completion times, \\nimprove on-time delivery rates, and maintain operational \\nresilience under uncertainty. \\nIndex Terms—Planning and scheduling, Resilience, Real-\\ntime data, Cyber-Physical System, Smart Manufacturing. \\n \\n*Research supported by several funding sources, including the \\nNational Natural Science Foundation of China (52405548, 52375498, \\n72231005), the Guangdong Basic and Applied Basic Research Foundation \\n(2023A1515110622), the 2019 Guangdong Special Support Talent \\nProgram – Innovation and Entrepreneurship Leading Team (China) \\n(2019BT02S593), 2018 Guangzhou Leading Innovation Team Program \\n(China) (201909010006). (Corresponding author: Ting Qu).  \\nMingxing Li, Ting Qu, Binyang Liu, Qijie Luo, and Mian Yan are with \\nthe School of Intelligent Systems Science and Engineering, the Institute of \\nPhysical Internet, and the Guangdong International Cooperation Base of \\nScience and Technology for GBA Smart Logistics, Jinan University, \\nZhuhai 519070, China (e-mail: limingxing@jnu.edu.cn; \\nquting@jnu.edu.cn;liuby88@stu2023.jnu.edu.cn; \\nluoqj@stu2024.jnu.edu.cn; yanmian@jnu.edu.cn). \\nZhen He is with the College of Management and Economics, Tianjin \\nUniversity, Tianjin 300072, China (e-mail: zhhe@tju.edu.cn) \\nMing Li and George Q. Huang are with the Department of Industrial \\nand Systems Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung \\nHom, Hong Kong, China (e-mail: ming.li@polyu.edu.hk; \\ngq.huang@polyu.edu.hk). I. INTRODUCTION \\nHE complex and stochastic nature of manufacturing \\nsystems is one of the main factors that hinder system \\nresilience. System complexity originates from its \\nstatic structure and time-dependent dynamic \\nbehavior [1]. The static complexity refers to the system \\nstructure, such as the variety of system components (e.g., \\nhumans, machines, materials), the patterns and strengths of \\nthe interactions among these components, and large product \\nportfolio; while the dynamic aspect is concerned with the \\ndecision-making, operational characteristics, and the \\nuncertainty/stochasticity in system behavior over time [2]. \\nThe complexity of the manufacturing system inevitably \\ncomplicates the production management (planning and \\nscheduling), especially in the modern competitive market \\nwith diversified customer demand (e.g., growing product \\nvariety and mixed volume). Besides, real-life production \\nprocess is subjected to various uncertainties, such as new \\norder arrivals, equipment failures, delayed material supply, \\nand uncertain operational times [3, 4]. These frequent \\nuncertainties and their adverse effect keep disturbing the \\nsystem and inevitably lead to unpredictable deviations \\nbetween the original plan/schedule and actual execution.  \\nConsiderable efforts have been made by industry and \\nacademia to tackle the operational complexity and \\nuncertainty. Leading companies developed various \\ninformation systems such as Advanced Planning and \\nScheduling (APS), Material Requirement Planning (MRP), \\nand Manufacturing Execution System (MES) to allocate \\nmaterials and capacity to fulfill customer demand [5]. The \\nefficacy of these systems is widely appreciated. However, \\nthere are vast differences in scale/size, manufacturing mode, \\nand equipment among different industries, and costs for \\ncustomizing the embedded models/algorithms within the \\nsystems are usually high. Moreover, it is difficult to use \\nthese systems to respond to frequent uncertain events in \\nactual manufacturing progress without advanced real-time \\ndata analytics [6, 7].  \\nThe combinations of different uncertainties have been \\nstudied in recent years. For example, Ghaleb et al. studied \\nseveral common maintenance-related issues (e.g., machine \\ndegradation, random breakdowns, minimal repairs, and \\ncondition-based maintenance) together with demand \\nchange in a flexible job shop scheduling problem [7]; a real-\\ntime integrated production and maintenance planning was \\ndesigned as an effective solution. These research efforts in \\novercoming uncertainty are widely appreciated. They \\nprovide a deeper understanding of the stochastic nature of \\nmanufacturing systems. However, most existing models and \\nalgorithms mainly focus on addressing single or limited Out-of-Order Architecture for Real-Time Data-Driven Resilient \\nPlanning and Scheduling of Cyber-Physical Manufacturing Systems \\nMingxing Li, Member, IEEE, Ting Qu, Senior Member, IEEE, Binyang Liu, Qijie Luo, Mian Yan, \\nMing Li, Senior Member, IEEE, Zhen He, and George Q. Huang, Fellow, IEEE This article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n2 \\n  \\ntypes of uncertainties. In contrast, real-world manufacturing \\nsystem operations often face the compounded effects of \\nmultiple sources of uncertainties. Such effects not only \\nweaken system stability but may also exacerbate the risks of \\nsystem degradation. Moreover, while certain complex \\nalgorithms are capable of optimizing system behavior in \\nspecific scenarios, they are often heavily reliant on large \\namounts of historical data or predefined models, making \\nthem less effective in quickly responding to the dynamics in \\nreal-time environments. Therefore, in practical applications, \\nthere is an urgent need for a management mechanism that is \\nsimple, flexible, and resilient to effectively address the \\ndynamic challenges posed by multi-source uncertainties. \\nThe advent of cutting-edge technologies or concepts, \\nsuch as Industrial Internet-of-Things (IIoT), Digital Twin \\n(DT), and Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS), provides a \\npromising direction forward and paves the way for Industry \\n4.0 manufacturing [8]. Advanced Industry 4.0 technologies \\nendow manufacturing objects with accurate identification, \\nubiquitous sensing, and seamless communication \\ncapabilities [9]. The power of the IIoT devices in CPS \\npromises to capture real-time manufacturing data, building \\nup cyber-physical transparency and spatial-temporal \\ntraceability for the whole factory [10, 11]. Thus, how the \\nmanufacturing operations management is reshaped in such a \\nreal-timely transparent, traceable, and interconnected \\nenvironment; how to solve the initial complex planning and \\nscheduling problem by leveraging the strengths of new \\ntechnologies; how can transparency and traceability be \\nintegrated to support production operations management, \\nare of concern in this study. \\nThis study aims to develop a novel Out-of-Order (OoO) \\narchitecture for resilient planning and scheduling using real-\\ntime data in Cyber-Physical Manufacturing Systems \\n(CPMS). OoO is a widely used dynamic instruction \\nscheduling and execution method in high-performance \\nCPUs. It schedules and distributes computer instructions \\nbased on real-time executability, thus effectively avoiding \\nunnecessary waits and stalls caused by disturbances. This \\npaper borrows the OoO spirit for CPMS operations \\nmanagement, there are three specific objectives: \\n• To develop an OoO architecture for CPMS through \\ndecomposition, digitization, and reconstruction of \\nmulti-level instruction queues and multi-granularity \\nexecution unit. \\n• To formalize OoO mechanisms with instruction \\nallocation and dispatching as the fundamental \\ndecision and operations logic of CPMS with the \\nintegration of real-time data.  \\n• To carry out computational experiments to examine \\nthe effectiveness and resilience of OoO under \\ndifferent levels of uncertainties. \\nThe rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section II \\nreview relevant works. Section III develops the Out-of-\\nOrder architecture for CPMS and Section IV formulates the \\nmechanism of real-time data-driven resilient planning and \\nscheduling. Computational experiments are carried out in \\nSection V to verify the effectiveness of the proposed solution. \\nFinally, Section VI summarizes this study briefly. II. RELATED WORK \\nA. Resilient Production Planning and Scheduling \\nNowadays, demand-driven customized manufacturing \\nparadigms urgently require more flexible production \\nplanning and scheduling strategies to adapt to the evolving \\nmarket environment [12]. To address various disturbance \\nfactors in production processes, numerous studies have \\nemployed optimization models and heuristic algorithms for \\ndynamic planning and scheduling. Specifically, Wang and \\nTang [13] proposed a robust optimization model and an \\nimproved artificial bee colony algorithm to enhance \\nmanufacturing system stability under processing and \\nhandling time uncertainties. Zhao et al. [14] developed a \\nDRL-based reactive scheduling strategy for the flexible job \\nshop scheduling problem with stochastic job arrivals, \\nvalidating its effectiveness in various discrete manufacturing \\nscenarios. Furthermore, Xiao et al. [15] focused on \\nequipment state uncertainties, implementing innovative \\nimprovements in workshop layout, resource utilization, and \\ndata loss prevention to achieve efficient and orderly \\nmanufacturing system operation. Finally, David Heik et al. \\n[16] simulated various advanced manufacturing scenarios \\nand addressed dynamic scheduling problems by evaluating \\nheuristic, metaheuristic, and reinforcement learning (RL) \\nmethods, significantly enhancing system resilience. These \\nstudies have made substantial progress in addressing various \\nuncertainty factors in manufacturing. \\nHowever, industrial applications indicate that these \\nresearch outcomes often fall short of expected performance \\nin real production environments. Existing studies are \\nfrequently confined to specific scenarios and disturbance \\ntypes. Therefore, further research is needed to develop \\nproduction systems capable of adapting to multiple \\ndisturbance environments. \\nB. Data-Driven Manufacturing Systems \\nUnder the framework of Industry 4.0, the increasing \\ncomplexity of customized manufacturing systems \\nnecessitates production systems to possess greater flexibility \\nand traceability for dynamic planning and scheduling \\n[17].Traditional periodic data collection methods struggle to \\ncope with uncertain production environments, making real-\\ntime data application key to optimizing and dynamically \\nadjusting production plans. In this context, Li et al. [18] \\nemployed digital twin technology to construct a multi-\\ndimensional workshop status monitoring system, achieving \\nprecise capture of real-world scenario information. Ji et al. \\n[19] achieved comprehensive real-time data collection for \\nproduction systems based on Industrial Cyber-Physical \\nSystems (ICPS) architecture. These studies collectively aim \\nto enhance the capability to address uncertainties in discrete \\nmanufacturing environments. Beyond traditional digital \\ntools, academia has proposed numerous innovative solutions. \\nNotably, Katarina Stankovovic et al. [20] developed a real-\\ntime framework that enables interventions based on real-\\ntime production parameters, improving production \\nefficiency and product quality. Similarly, Rafael Diaz et al. \\n[21]  designed a simulation and validation platform capable \\nof collecting data from various traditional manufacturing \\nenvironments, providing reliable technical support for the This article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n3 \\n  \\nintelligent transformation of conventional manufacturing \\nsystems. These studies leverage real-time data, significantly \\nenhancing the realism and reliability of production systems \\nacross various manufacturing scenarios. \\nCurrent digital tools and theoretical frameworks provide \\na foundation for data collection and initial application, but \\nthere are still gaps in addressing uncertainty and enhancing \\nsystem flexibility. This indicates a need for further \\ntheoretical exploration and empirical validation to \\neffectively utilize real-time data to improve the transparency \\nand traceability of production systems. \\nC. Planning and Scheduling in CPMS \\nWith the support of IIoT and DT, CPMS are enabled to \\nmake autonomous decisions aimed at enhancing the \\nperformance of the manufacturing process [22]. Various \\nstudies have explored planning and scheduling in CPMS. \\nLin et al. [23] constructed a cyber-physical digital twin \\nplatform based on CPS and DT technology, which can \\ngenerate optimal production schedules based on real-time \\ninformation and has designed an integration scheme with \\nsystems such as ERP, MES, and SCADA. Novák [24] \\nproposed a CPPS-based distributed MES for flexible \\nproduction plans control across industries. Yun et al. [25] \\nproposed a CPS-enabled and knowledge-aided real-time \\ndemand response strategy to address the electricity demand \\nresponse issue in sustainable manufacturing, validated in a \\ncase study. Villalonga et al. [26] presented a DT-based \\ndecision framework for dynamic scheduling, demonstrating \\nits utility in an Industry 4.0 assembly line. Guha et al. [27] \\nproposed a CPS-based closed-loop production model \\nintegrating big data analytics for self-adaptation and real-\\ntime quality control under varying conditions. Khadiri et al. \\n[28] explored intelligent CPPS scheduling using multi-agent \\nmodeling, validated in flexible manufacturing cells. \\nExisting studies based on the CPMS architecture have \\nmade significant progress in dynamic task allocation and \\nresource scheduling. However, challenges remain in \\naddressing the conflicts between real-time requirements and \\nmulti-resource coordination during planning and scheduling. \\nIn particular, there is a lack of efficient scheduling \\nmechanisms that fully exploit the advantages of CPMS to \\nalleviate adverse cascading effects caused by resource \\ncontention, task dependencies, and random disturbances. \\nIII. OUT-OF-ORDER ARCHITECTURE FOR \\nMANUFACTURING OPERATIONS OF CPMS \\nInspired by OoO execution, this section proposes an Out-\\nof-Order architecture for CPMS and develops corresponding \\nmulti-level instruction queuing mechanism for resilient \\noperations using real-time data. \\nA.  The Analogy of OoO in CPU and Manufacturing  \\nOoO execution is a widely used dynamic instruction \\nscheduling and execution method in high-performance \\nCPUs. Its key idea is to achieve adaptive scheduling and \\ndistribution of computational instructions based on real-time \\nexecutability of instructions by checking their required input \\ndata, computing resources, and data/instruction \\ndependencies, thus effectively avoiding unnecessary waits and stalls caused by disturbances such as cache misses \\nduring the computation process. Given the fact that it \\noperates under the guidance of real-time executability rather \\nthan simply following the rigid predetermined order of \\ninstructions in computer programs, it is named “out-of-\\norder”. OoO execution is a breakthrough in the field of \\ncomputer science and greatly improves the computational \\nefficiency and resource utilization of CPUs.  \\nConsidering the CPMS as a high-performance CPU, \\nmanufacturing execution resources, materials, and \\nproduction-logistics operations can be analogized to \\ncomputing execution units, input data, and instructions, as \\nshown in Table I. This study fully leverages the advantages \\nof highly interconnected and real-time perception of \\nmultiple resources in the CPMS, as well as transparent and \\ntraceable operation processes, to reshape the decision-\\nmaking and control mode of production and logistics \\noperations within the system using the OoO execution \\narchitecture, achieving high resilience, adaptability, and \\nintelligent operations of the CPMS. Figure 1 and Figure 2 \\npresent the OoO logic in CPU and in manufacturing systems, \\nrespectively. Table II shows the symbols and descriptions \\nrelated to instruction and queuing structure. \\nTABLE I.  THE ANALOGY BETWEEN MANUFACTURING AND CPUS \\nManufacturing System CPU \\nName Function Name Function \\nOrders/Jobs/ \\nOperations Define operations \\nto be executed Instructions Define \\noperations to be \\nexecuted \\nMaterials/WIP/ \\nProducts Inputs, \\nintermediates and \\noutputs of \\noperations Data Inputs, \\nintermediates \\nand outputs of \\noperations \\nWarehouse Store materials/ \\nproducts Memory Store data \\nRaw Material \\nBuffer Temporarily store \\ninput materials Load Buffer \\n& Registers Temporarily \\nstore input data \\nWork-in-Progress \\nBuffer Temporarily store \\nin-process \\nmaterials Registers Temporarily \\nstore in-process \\ndata \\nOrder Instruction \\nQueue (푂푄) Store instructions Instruction \\nQueue Store instructions \\nJob/Production \\nOperation/Logistic\\ns Operation \\nInstruction Queue \\n(퐽푄/ P푄 L푄) & \\nBuffer Buffer pending \\ninstructions, track \\nmaterial \\navailability, \\ndispatch when \\nready Reservation \\nStations Buffer pending \\ninstructions, \\ntrack operand \\navailability, \\ndispatch when \\nready \\nWorkstations/ \\nMachines/Worker Perform/execute \\noperations Functional \\nUnits Perform/execute \\noperations \\nFinished Product \\nBuffer Temporarily store \\nfinished products Store Buffer Temporarily \\nstore results \\nIn computer OoO execution, instruction processing \\ngenerally consists of four stages: Fetch, Decode, Execute, \\nand Writeback. By analogy, they can be mapped to four key \\nstages in manufacturing process: Confirm, Release, \\nExecute, and Complete. Meanwhile, there are \\nmultidimensional dependencies both within and among \\ninstructions, as shown in the execution graph in Figure 3(a). \\nThese dependencies are mainly manifested in four aspects: \\n(1) Dependencies of internal stages: an instruction must \\nproceed from the first stage to the last stage within the  This article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n4 \\n  \\n \\nFigure 1.  Schematic Diagram of OoO Architecture in CPU  \\nFigure 2.  OoO Architecture in Manufacturing System\\n \\nFigure 3.  An  Example of OoO for Manufacturing Operations\\nTABLE II.  SYMBOL DEFINITIONS FOR INSTRUCTION AND QUEUING \\nSTRUCTURE. \\nSymbol Description \\n푂퐼￿ Order instruction, 표 is the order index \\n푂푄￿  Order instruction queue at 푇  \\n퐽퐼￿ Job instruction, 푖 is the job index \\n퐽퐼￿￿￿￿ The 푛 th product of the 푗 th type in the 푖 th order \\n퐽푄￿,￿  The queue of job instructions at time t \\n푃퐼￿  Production-operation instruction, 푝 is the operation index \\n퐿퐼￿  Logistics-operation instructions \\n푃푄￿  Production-operation instruction queue at time 휏 \\n퐿푄￿ Logistics-operation instruction queue at time 휏 \\ninstruction in order; (2) Dependencies of operational \\nconstraints: some instructions must be completed before an \\ninstruction can start due to operational requirements; (3) \\nDependencies of initial sequence: initial queue of \\ninstructions; and (d) Dependencies of resource contention: the contending relationships of the same resource among \\ninstructions. \\nThe execution graph shown in Figure 3(a) is selected as \\nan example and allocated to three workstations to illustrate \\nOoO execution mechanism in the manufacturing. As \\npresented in Figure 3(b), the multidimensional real-time \\nstates of system are considered, including operators, \\nmachines, materials, and instructions, while introducing \\nuncertainty factors such as machine failures, temporary \\noperator absences, and material supply delays to more \\naccurately reflect real-world manufacturing scenarios. \\nComparative experiments with the in-order instruction \\nexecution mode demonstrated that the operational decisions \\nunder the OoO execution exhibited significant superiority \\nunder uncertainties, resulting in an effective reduction of the \\nproduction cycle by three periods. \\nB.  OoO Architecture for CPMS \\nFigure 4. depicts the OoO architecture for operations in  \\nThis article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n5 \\n  \\nFigure 4.  OoO Architecture for planning and scheduling of CPMS \\nCPMS, consisting of multi-granularity CP execution units \\nand multi-level instruction queues.  \\nThe complex organization and workflow of the CPMS \\nare divided of into finite smaller units with simpler decision \\nand operation models to reduce the overall complexity and \\nlocalize uncertainty as the basis for digitization. The \\ndecomposition proceeds in a top-down and hierarchical way. \\nA general manufacturing system is naturally divided into a \\nnumber of distributed workstations. These workstations \\nmay differ in their configurations, responsibilities, and \\nscales. Each workstation is subdivided into smaller discrete \\nphysical objects, including human operators, \\nvehicles/trolleys, robots, machines/tools, WIPs/materials, \\netc. These objects are characterized by distinct attributes, \\nsuch as the skills of humans, the capacity of vehicles, \\nfunctions of machines, type of materials, etc. These \\nheterogeneous and hierarchical organizations (system, \\nworkstations, objects) are then abstracted as physical twins \\n(PTs). The decomposition granularity should strike a \\nbalance between the overall complexity and the costs for \\ndigitization. That is, the appropriate granularity of PTs \\nought to be small enough to have relatively straightforward \\ndecisions/operations while large enough to keep the \\ndigitization costs (e.g., IIoT devices deployment, software \\ndevelopment) within reasonable bounds.  \\nHeterogeneous and hierarchical physical twins (PTs) are \\nthen transformed into faithful DTs by deploying a set of plug \\n& play, self-configurable, and scalable IIoT devices for \\ncreating highly visible, traceable, and interconnected \\nmanufacturing environments. The digitization starts from \\nthe object level. Key physical objects are equipped with \\nsmart tags (e.g., RFID, iBeacon) that carry essential data or \\nsmart sensors to capture critical status/attributes data. \\nCoordinated with smart devices (e.g., gateways, readers, \\nsmartphones, wearables), data can be real-timely transmitted \\nto the cyber factory. Various communication and \\nnetworking technologies (e.g., 4/5G, Bluetooth, UWB, and ZigBee) serve as data carriers to link the physical and cyber \\nfactories. Cyber and physical spaces are seamlessly \\nsynchronized under smart gateways to support visibility and \\ntraceability analytics. Cyber-Physical Agents (CP-Agents) \\nare applied to deal with heterogeneous network protocols \\nand IIoT devices so that they can be encapsulated and \\norganized in a uniform plug-and-play and self-configurable \\nmanner. With the adoption of IIoT devices and CP-Agents, \\nall the hierarchically discretized, spatially distributed PTs \\nare augmented with the capabilities of identification, \\nubiquitous sensing, and seamless communication. Multi-\\ndimensional data (space, time, and state) from diverse \\nsources (different manufacturing processes and operations) \\nof PTs are utilized to map onto their corresponding DTs via \\nadvanced computing and data analytics; DTs can also, in \\nturn, generate real-time feedback to PTs.  \\nThe combination of PT and its corresponding DT can be \\nconsidered as a Cyber-Physical Execution Unit (CPEU) in \\nOoO architecture, and they operate under the governance of \\nmanufacturing instructions, for instance, 푂퐼, 퐽퐼, 푃퐼 and 퐿퐼. \\nThe multi-level instruction queues of CPEUs are real-\\ntimely updated using visibility and traceability. It \\ndynamically queues and schedules each instruction with \\nvalidation, establishing flexible and resilient decision-\\nmaking and operational logic for CPMS to achieve resilient \\nmanufacturing operations under uncertainties.  \\nIV. OUT-OF-ORDER MECHANISMS FOR REAL-TIME DATA-\\nDRIVEN RESILIENT PLANNING AND SCHEDULING \\nThis subsection formalizes the spirit of OoO as a \\nstraightforward, flexible, and resilient decision/operation \\nlogic of CPEUs to promote their autonomy under \\nuncertainty. The OoO queuing mechanism is divided into \\nthe Synchronized Instruction Allocation (planning stage) \\nand the Real-Time Instruction Dispatching (scheduling \\nstage), as shown in Figure 5. Table III shows the symbols \\nand descriptions related to the formulations. \\nThis article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n6 \\n  \\n \\nFigure 5.  OoO Mechanisms for Real-Time Data-Driven Resilient Planning and Scheduling\\nTABLE III.  SYMBOL DEFINITIONS FOR MODEL FORMULATION  \\nSymbol Description \\nℎ,푘 Index of 푂퐼 \\n퐽퐹￿ Set of job families of the requested products in 푂퐼￿ \\n푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿ Distance of 푂퐼￿ and 푂퐼￿ based on job family similarity \\n푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿ Distance of 푂퐼￿ and 푂퐼￿ based on due date similarity \\n푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿ Distance of 푂퐼￿ and 푂퐼￿ based on setup similarity \\n퐷푖푠푡￿,￿ Overall distance of 푂퐼￿ and 푂퐼￿ \\n푤￿, 푤￿, 푤￿ Weight coefficients of distance  \\n푠￿,￿￿ The setup time for changeover between family 푓 and 푓￿ \\n푑￿ The due time of 푂퐼￿ \\n푠푡푑(푑) The standard deviation \\n푆(퐴,퐵) The inter-cluster similarity between clusters A and B \\n푎￿,푏￿ Centroids of clusters A and B \\n푊푆￿ Workstation, 푠 is the index \\n푃￿,￿￿ Preference index that reflects the urgency of 퐽퐼￿ on 푊푆￿ \\n푃￿,￿￿ Preference index that reflects the production progress of \\nthe order containing 퐽퐼￿ on 푊푆￿ \\n푃￿,￿￿ Preference index that reflects potential setup of 퐽퐼￿ on \\n푊푆￿ \\n푃￿,￿ The overall preference index of 퐽퐼￿ on 푊푆￿ \\n푤￿, 푤￿, 푤￿ Weight coefficients of preference index \\n푃푇￿￿￿￿ Total processing time of queuing work in 퐽푄 of 푂퐼￿ \\n푃푇￿￿￿￿ Total processing time of work in progress of 푂퐼￿ \\n푃푇￿￿￿ Total processing time of finished work of 푂퐼￿ \\n푃푇￿ Total processing time of total work of 푂퐼￿ \\n푛￿￿￿￿ The number of 퐽퐼s belongs to the same job family as 퐽퐼￿ \\nin current 퐽푄 \\n푠￿(￿),￿ The potential setup time if the 퐽퐼￿ is assigned to 푊푆￿ \\nA. Synchronized Instruction Allocation \\nTo make sure 퐽퐼s can be dispatched in an “Out-of-Order” \\nmanner in the scheduling stage, similar 푂퐼s needed to be grouped and assigned to a planning period at the planning \\nstage. The similarity of 푂퐼s can be measure from job \\nfamilies, due time, and so on. In this paper, pairwise \\ndistances are applied (a small distance indicates high \\nsimilarity). Hierarchical clustering is adopted in the \\nplanning stage. Three 푂퐼 attributes, including job families, \\npotential setup, and due time, are considered in distance \\ncalculation.  \\nThe distance regarding job families 푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿ can be \\ncalculated using Jaccard distance.  \\n푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿=1−|￿￿￿∩￿￿￿|\\n|￿￿￿∪￿￿￿|(1)  \\nThe 푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿ is used to evaluate the potential setup time. \\n푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿=∑ 푠￿,￿￿￿,￿￿∈￿￿￿∪￿￿￿(2)  \\nThe due time distance 푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿ is computed using the \\nStandardized Euclidean distance.  \\n푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿=￿￿￿￿￿￿￿\\n￿￿￿(￿)￿￿\\n(3)  \\nFor a set made up of  푁￿￿ 푂퐼s ,there will be ￿￿￿(￿￿￿ – ￿)\\n￿ \\n푂퐼 pairs in the set. The result is a distance matrix 퐷푖푠푡￿,￿, \\nwhere 푛표푟푚() is the Min-Max normalization function used \\nto normalize each type of distance to the interval [0,1]. The \\ndistance between 푂퐼￿ and 푂퐼￿ 퐷푖푠푡￿,￿ is computed as: \\n퐷푖푠푡￿,￿=∑ 푤￿ ￿￿{￿,￿,￿} ∙푛표푟푚￿푑푖푠푡￿,￿￿￿ (4)  \\nThe distance matrix determines the proximity of 퐽퐼s to \\neach other, 푂퐼푠 that are close enough will be linked \\ntogether to generate 푂퐼 pairs. And then, linkages are \\ncreated between the newly formed 푂퐼 pairs to form more \\ngiant 푂퐼 clusters until all pending 푂퐼푠 are connected into a \\nhierarchical 퐽퐼 tree. Let 푎 and 푏 represent the centroids of \\nclusters 퐴 and 퐵, and 푛￿ and 푛￿ denote the 푂퐼푠 number of \\nclusters 퐴 and 퐵, respectively. The similarity between 푂퐼 \\nThis article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n7 \\n  \\ncluster 퐴 and 퐵 is calculated as \\n푆(퐴,퐵)=￿￿￿￿￿￿\\n￿￿￿￿￿￿푎−푏￿￿(5)  \\nThe hierarchical tree can be either naturally partitioned \\ninto distinct, well-separated clusters or divided by a \\nspecified number. Therefore, the decisions can be flexible \\nbased on the real-time situation of the shop floor. Then all \\nthe 퐽퐼s below a cut are allocated to a specific planning \\nperiod. In this phase, the computational complexity is \\n푂(푛￿￿). 푛￿ represents the current number of orders. This \\ncomplexity is acceptable since the production planning \\nperiod is relatively long in real-world factories. \\nB. Real-Time Instruction Dispatching \\nAs the 푂퐼푠 allocated to a single planning period are \\nsimilar, the exact processing sequence of these 푂퐼푠 / 퐽퐼s is \\ninsignificant. Therefore, the OoO spirit can be applied to \\nachieve real-time 퐽퐼 sequencing. At the scheduling stage, \\nall 퐽퐼s are organized, dispatched, and executed in an order \\nthat considers the real-time situations in the factory (e.g., \\nworkloads, production progress, workstation status, \\noperational constraints, real-time availability of required \\noperators, materials, and various equipment) to enhance the \\noverall efficiency when the initial plan/schedule is \\ndisturbed by uncertainty. That is, CPEUs look ahead in the \\ninstruction queues and find the ready 퐽퐼s. When a \\ndisturbance like material delay or machine breakdown \\noccurs, the CPEU can decide to handle other ready 퐽퐼s or \\nuse other available machines rather than wait. This study \\nproposes a multi-dimensional preference index to evaluate \\nthe real-time preference for a station to process the 퐽퐼. \\n푃￿,￿￿ considers the urgency of the 퐽퐼;  \\n푃￿,￿￿=푑￿−푝￿ (6)  \\n푃￿,￿￿ considers the production progress of the 푂퐼 that \\ncontains the 퐽퐼 to reduce flow time and potential inventory. \\n푃￿,￿￿=푒(￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿￿\\n￿￿￿)(7)  \\n푃￿,￿￿ aims to improve the production efficiency by \\nreducing setup work.  \\n푃￿,￿￿=￿1푛￿￿￿￿⁄ ,푖푓 푠푒푡푢푝 푖푠 푛표푡 푟푒푢푖푟푒푑\\n푠￿(￿),￿, 푖푓 푠푒푡푢푝 푖푠 푟푒푞푢푖푟푒푑(8)  \\nThe overall preference index is calculated as: \\n푃￿,￿=∑ 푤￿ ￿￿{￿,￿,￿} ∙푛표푟푚￿푃￿,￿￿￿ (9)  \\nFor all available stations and 퐽퐼s, a preference matrix \\n푷풔,풋 can be obtained. To find the most suitable station- 퐽퐼 \\nassignment is to find the 푷풔,풋 that have small diagonal \\nentries. Hungarian algorithm can be applied for this purpose. \\nIn this phase, the complexity in preference calculation is \\n푂(푛￿∙푛￿), and the Hungarian algorithm complexity is also \\n푂(푛￿∙푛￿), where 푛￿ and 푛￿ denote the number of pending \\n퐽퐼s and available stations. Considering real-time instruction \\nvalidation process, the value of 푛￿ and 푛￿ is usually small, \\nthus the computational burden should be acceptable for \\nreal-time scheduling in real-world factories.  V. COMPUTATIONAL EXPERIMENTS \\nThis section conducts computational experiments to \\nexamine the effectiveness and resilience of the proposed \\nsolution under the influence of various uncertain events.  \\nA. Experimental Setting \\nThe dataset used in the experiments is randomly \\ngenerated based on TABLE IV, with reference to parameter \\nconfiguration practices from related literature. The \\ninvestigated manufacturing system consists of a number of \\nworkstations. one station can only process one job/product \\nat a time, and setup is required for changeover between \\ndifferent job families. All experiments are carried out using \\ncustom code in MATLAB R2022a on a personal computer. \\nTABLE IV.  DATASET OF EXPERIMENTS \\nData Values \\nNumber of workstations 푁￿ 10 \\nNumber of customer orders 푁￿ 100 \\nNumber of jobs per customer order 푁￿ 5 \\nNumber of job families 푁￿ 10 \\nStandard processing time of jobs 푝 푈[80,120] \\nStandard setup time for changeover 푠￿￿ 푈[10,30] \\nDue time of customer orders 푑 푈(1\\n4퐷,5\\n4퐷) \\n퐷=(∑∑ 푝￿ ￿∈￿ ￿\\n푁￿) \\nTo verify the performance resilience of OoO under \\nuncertainty. Typical uncertain events (uncertain processing \\ntime, uncertain arrivals of new order, and uncertain \\nbreakdowns of workstation) and three levels of uncertainty \\n(see TABLE V) are introduced in the experiments. \\nProbability distributions of uncertainty are set to be similar \\nto previous papers. The disturbance factor for processing \\ntimes and the ratio of new order arrivals follow commonly \\nused settings in studies on scheduling under uncertainty \\n[29]. The number of orders is configured according to \\ntypical experimental scales observed in flexible job-shop \\nscheduling [30]. Those for machine breakdown and repair \\ntimes—are set in accordance with the previous simulation \\nstudies [9, 31]. Details are given as follows. \\n1) Uncertain processing time is modelled using \\ntriangular fuzzy number 푝￿. \\n푝￿=￿(1−훼)∙푝,푝,￿1+3\\n2훼￿∙푝￿          (10) \\n2) Uncertain arrivals of new order are simulated using \\nPoisson process with 휆. The total number of new \\norders is set as 휌∙푁￿. \\n3) Uncertain breakdowns of workstation are modelled by \\nexponential distributions. Mean time between two \\nconsecutive breakdowns is 푀푇퐵퐵, and the mean time \\nto repair is 푀푇푇푅. \\nTABLE V.  PARAMETERS OF UNCERTAINTY \\nUncertainty level 휶 흀 흆 푴푻푩푩 푴푻푻푹 \\nLow 0.25 60 25% 1000 25 \\nMedium 0.5 30 50% 500 50 \\nHigh 0.99 15 75% 250 100 \\n This article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n8 \\n  \\nTwo metrics, including On-Time Delivery Rate (OTDR) \\nof customer orders and Average Order Flow Time (AOFT) \\nare used to evaluate the performance of the proposed \\nsolution. OTDR is adopted to evaluate punctuality \\nperformance; higher on-time delivery rate usually indicates \\ngreater customer satisfaction. AOFT considers the WIPs \\nand product inventory levels. It is defined as the time \\ndifferences between the earliest release time and the latest \\ncompletion time of jobs within one customer order. \\nExperiments containing a deterministic environment and \\nthree levels of uncertainty are designed and compared with \\nthree flexible scheduling strategies. 1) First Come First \\nServe (FCFS): 푃￿￿￿￿=푡￿, Orders/jobs are released and \\nsequenced in ascending order of their arrival time; 2) \\nModified Earliest Due Date (MEDD): 푃￿￿￿￿=\\n푚푎푥(푝,푑−휏), Orders/jobs are released and sequenced in \\nascending order of their modified due time; 3) Shortest Setup Time (SST): 푃￿￿￿=푠￿,￿￿, Jobs are sequenced in \\nascending order of their standard setup time with the \\nprevious job processed at the station. The weights in \\nEquation (4) and (9) are set as follows: 푤￿=0.25, 푤￿=\\n0.25, 푤￿=0.5, 푤￿=0.25, 푤￿=0.5, 푤￿=0.25. \\nB. Results and Implications \\nTo reduce the influence of random errors on the results, \\nall experiments were repeated 100 times. Figures 6-7 \\ndirectly show all 100 results of each method under different \\nlevels of uncertainty. It can be clearly found that the curves \\nof OoO and the other methods basically do not overlap, \\nwhich indicates that its performance advantage is \\nstatistically significant. And it can be seen that OoO shows \\na strong advantage in both AOFT and OTDR metrics, and \\nits curves not only have a smaller overall fluctuation range, \\nbut also maintain a better stability in uncertainty \\nenvironments. \\n \\nFigure 6.  OTDR Results in Stochastic Environments \\n \\nFigure 7.  AOFT Results in Stochastic Environments \\nThis article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n9 \\n  \\nTABLE VI.  PERFORMANCE OF DIFFERENT METHODS \\na. The bolded data are the best values for different methods in the same environment. \\nTo present a more comprehensive investigation, this \\nstudy introduces four metrics to evaluate resilience: \\nStandard deviation, Coefficient of variation, Mean \\ndeviation proportion, and Maximum deviation proportion \\nof OTDR and AOFT. As shown in Table VI, OoO achieves \\nthe best performance in mean values and mean deviation \\nproportion among all environments. Although OoO does \\nnot always exhibit the lowest standard deviation or \\ncoefficient of variation, its results are very close to the best \\nvalues. From the perspective of deviation proportions, OoO \\ndemonstrates the smallest performance deterioration. For \\ninstance, under high uncertainty, the mean deviation \\nproportion of AOFT for OoO is 27.01%, significantly lower \\nthan FCFS (37.22%), MEDD (49.44%), and SST (35.06%). \\nThese findings indicate that OoO strikes a balance between \\nsuperior mean performance and reduced variability, \\nshowcasing its robustness and resilience in uncertain \\nenvironments. \\n \\nFigure 8.  OTDR of Different Methods in Different Uncertainty Levels The trend is more visually demonstrated through Figures \\n8-9. On the OTDR curve, OoO shows the smallest decline, \\nmaintaining a relatively high on-time delivery rate even \\nunder high uncertainty. In contrast, FCFS and MEDD \\nexhibit noticeable drops even in environments with lower \\nuncertainty. For AOFT, OoO starts with the lowest value \\nand demonstrates the smallest increase as uncertainty grows, \\nreflecting its smoother workflow and greater stability. SST, \\non the other hand, shows the steeper increase and the \\nhighest values, indicating poor performance in complex \\nenvironments. These findings highlight the consistent \\nresilience of OoO across various conditions.  \\nIn summary, the experimental results show that OoO is \\nable to demonstrate efficacy and high resilience under all \\nlevels of uncertainty, and can effectively reduce order flow \\ntime, reduce WIP, finished goods inventory and improve \\non-time delivery. \\n \\n \\nFigure 9.  AOFT of Different Methods in Different Uncertainty Levels \\nEnvironment Methods OTDR AOFT \\nMean \\nvalues Standard \\ndeviation Coefficient \\nof variation Mean \\ndeviation \\nproportion Maximum \\ndeviation \\nproportion Mean \\nvalues Standard \\ndeviation Coefficient \\nof variation Mean \\ndeviation \\nproportion Maximum \\ndeviation \\nproportion \\nDeterministic OoO 100.00 N/A N/A N/A  N/A 588.08 N/A N/A N/A  N/A \\nFCFS 100.00 N/A N/A N/A  N/A 618.99 N/A N/A N/A  N/A \\nMEDD 100.00 N/A N/A N/A  N/A 609.95 N/A N/A N/A  N/A \\nSST 100.00 N/A N/A N/A  N/A 792.92 N/A N/A N/A  N/A \\nLow uncertainty \\n(LU) OoO 96.44 3.16 0.0327 3.56% 15% 668.78 11.45 0.0171 13.72% 18.51% \\nFCFS 70.62 4.06 0.0575 29.38% 38% 727.59 19.25 0.0265 17.55% 42.10% \\nMEDD 70.27 4.07 0.0580 29.73% 38% 704.01 10.46 0.0149 15.42% 19.88% \\nSST 77.36 6.05 0.0783 22.64% 43% 992.44 46.31 0.0467 25.16% 36.19% \\nMedium \\nuncertainty \\n(MU) OoO 88.8 4.88 0.0550 11.20% 28% 687.68 10.91 0.0159 16.94% 21.67% \\nFCFS 66.56 3.11 0.0467 33.44% 38% 751.39 35.25 0.0469 21.39% 49.98% \\nMEDD 66.98 2.85 0.0426 33.02% 38% 716.40 8.86 0.0124 17.45% 20.61% \\nSST 73.78 6.03 0.0817 26.22% 39% 1000.72 47.53 0.0475 26.21% 38.99% \\nHigh uncertainty \\n(HU) OoO 66.51 3.67 0.0552 33.49% 40% 746.90 13.00 0.0174 27.01% 32.40% \\nFCFS 59.44 2.02 0.0339 40.56% 46% 849.40 52.88 0.0623 37.22% 58.25% \\nMEDD 58.98 2.16 0.0367 41.02% 47% 911.52 135.92 0.1491 49.44% 77.05% \\nSST 58.01 6.55 0.1130 41.99% 63% 1070.94 52.10 0.0486 35.06% 53.91% This article has been accepted for publication in IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and Engineering. This is the author's version which has not been fully edited and \\ncontent may change prior to final publication. Citation information: DOI 10.1109/TASE.2025.3563084\\n© 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved, including rights for text and data mining and training of artificial intelligence and similar technologies. Personal use is permitted,\\nbut republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission. See https://www.ieee.org/publications/rights/index.html for more information.Authorized licensed use limited to: Hong Kong Polytechnic University. Downloaded on July 15,2025 at 18:20:55 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply. \\n \\n10 \\n  \\nVI. CONCLUSION \\nThis study proposes an Out-of-Order (OoO) architecture \\nfor Cyber-Physical Manufacturing Systems (CPMS), \\nleveraging real-time data analytics to enhance the resilience \\nof planning and scheduling under uncertainty. By drawing \\ninspiration from OoO execution in CPU systems, we \\nconstruct a multi-level instruction queuing mechanism that \\nenables dynamic scheduling based on task executability. \\nThe contributions of this work are twofold. First, it \\nintroduces a novel decision-making framework for CPMS \\nthat bridges the structural disconnect between planning and \\nreal-time execution, enabling decentralized and execution-\\naware control. Second, the proposed approach provides a \\nreal-time alternative to traditional data-driven scheduling \\nmethods. Unlike conventional models that rely heavily on \\nhistorical data or pre-trained policies and often address only \\nlimited types of uncertainty, OoO mechanism dynamically \\nadapts to multiple disturbances without offline learning or \\nstatic assumptions. This enhances its applicability in real-\\nworld disturbance-prone environments. \\nNonetheless, two limitations should be acknowledged. \\nFirst, the effectiveness of the OoO mechanism relies on the \\navailability and timeliness of high-quality real-time data. \\nDue to the limited deployment of Industry 4.0 infrastructure \\nand the lack of systematic recording of disturbance-related \\ndata, real-world industrial datasets with multi-dimensional \\nuncertainty remain scarce. As such, this study adopts a \\ncontrolled simulation environment designed to emulate \\ntypical uncertainty patterns based on realistic assumptions. \\nSecond, the implementation of the OoO architecture \\nrequires a certain level of digital infrastructure—such as \\nIIoT and digital twins—which may pose challenges for \\nresource-constrained factories. \\nImplementation of OoO architecture in real factory \\nscenarios, fusion of big data with real-time data in OoO \\ndecision process, and extension of OoO to include \\nintralogistics could be fruitful areas for further work. \\nREFERENCES \\n[1] M. Li, M. Li, D. Guo, T. Qu, and G. Q. Huang, \\\"Real-Time Data-\\nDriven Out-of-Order Synchronization for Production and \\nIntralogistics in Multiresource-Constrained Assembly Systems,\\\" \\nIEEE Transactions on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics: Systems, pp. \\n1-13, 2023. \\n[2] B. Alkan, D. A. Vera, M. Ahmad, B. Ahmad, and R. Harrison, \\n\\\"Complexity in manufacturing systems and its measures: a \\nliterature review,\\\" European Journal of Industrial Engineering, vol. \\n12, no. 1, pp. 116-150, 2018. \\n[3] D. Lovato, R. Guillaume, C. Thierry, and O. Battaia, \\\"Managing \\ndisruptions in aircraft assembly lines with staircase criteria,\\\" \\nInternational Journal of Production Research, pp. 1-17, Jan 1, 2022. \\n[4] M. Li, D. Guo, M. Li, T. Qu, and G. Q. Huang, \\\"Operation twins: \\nproduction-intralogistics synchronisation in Industry 4.0,\\\" \\nInternational Journal of Production Research, pp. 1-19, 2022. \\n[5] F. M. Kasie, G. Bright, and A. Walker, “Decision support systems \\nin manufacturing: a survey and future trends,” Journal of Modelling \\nin Management, vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 432-454, 2017. \\n[6] X. Jing, Q. Pan, L. Gao, and L. Wang, “An Effective Iterated \\nGreedy Algorithm for a Robust Distributed Permutation Flowshop \\nProblem With Carryover Sequence-Dependent Setup Time,” IEEE \\nTransactions on Systems Man Cybernetics-Systems, Dec 10, 2021. \\n[7] M. Ghaleb, S. Taghipour, and H. Zolfagharinia, “Real-time \\nintegrated production-scheduling and maintenance-planning in a \\nflexible job shop with machine deterioration and condition-based \\nmaintenance,” Journal of Manufacturing Systems, vol. 61, pp. 423-\\n449, Oct, 2021. [8] Y. Zhang, K. Ding, J. Hui, S. Liu, W. Guo, and L. Wang, \\\"Skeleton-\\nRGB integrated highly similar human action prediction in human–\\nrobot collaborative assembly,\\\" Robotics and Computer-Integrated \\nManufacturing, vol. 86, pp. 102659, 2024/04/01/, 2024. \\n[9] M. Li, M. Li, H. Ding, S. Ling, and G. Q. Huang, \\\"Graduation-\\ninspired synchronization for industry 4.0 planning, scheduling, and \\nexecution,\\\" Journal of Manufacturing Systems, vol. 64, pp. 94-106, \\n2022/07/01/, 2022. \\n[10] E. Mahmoodi, M. Fathi, M. Tavana, M. Ghobakhloo, and A. H. C. \\nNg, \\\"Data-driven simulation-based decision support system for \\nresource allocation in industry 4.0 and smart manufacturing,\\\" \\nJournal of Manufacturing Systems, vol. 72, pp. 287-307, \\n2024/02/01/, 2024. \\n[11] M. Zhang, F. Tao, and A. Y. C. Nee, \\\"Digital Twin Enhanced \\nDynamic Job-Shop Scheduling,\\\" Journal of Manufacturing Systems, \\nvol. 58, pp. 146-156, 2021/01/01/, 2021. \\n[12] Huang J, Huang S, Moghaddam S K, et al. \\\"Deep reinforcement \\nlearning-based dynamic reconfiguration planning for digital twin-\\ndriven smart manufacturing systems with reconfigurable machine \\ntools,\\\" IEEE Transactions on Industrial Informatics, vol. 20, no. 11, \\npp. 13135-13146, 2024. \\n[13] L. Wang, Z. D. Luo, H. T. Tang, S. S. Guo, and X. X. Li, \\\"A Novel \\nModel for Dynamic Manufacturing Service Collaboration on \\nIndustrial Internet,\\\" IEEE Transactions on Industrial Informatics, \\nvol. 19, no. 12, pp. 11788-11799, Dec, 2023. \\n[14] L. L. Zhao, J. X. Fan, C. J. Zhang, W. M. Shen, and J. Zhuang, \\\"A \\nDRL-Based Reactive Scheduling Policy for Flexible Job Shops \\nWith Random Job Arrivals,\\\" IEEE Transactions on Automation \\nScience and Engineering, pp. 12, 2023 2023. \\n[15] H. Xiao, W. S. Hu, H. Zhou, and G. P. Liu, \\\"Prediction-Based \\nPower Consumption Monitoring of Industrial Equipment Using \\nInterpretable Data-Driven Models,\\\" IEEE Transactions on \\nAutomation Science and Engineering, vol. 21, no. 2, pp. 1312-1322, \\nApr, 2024. \\n[16] D. Heik, F. Bahrpeyma, and D. Reichelt, \\\"Study on the application \\nof single-agent and multi-agent reinforcement learning to dynamic \\nscheduling in manufacturing environments with growing \\ncomplexity: Case study on the synthesis of an industrial IoT Test \\nBed,\\\" Journal of Manufacturing Systems, vol. 77, pp. 525-557, \\n2024. \\n[17] C. Zhang, J. Li, G. Zhou, Q. Huang, M. Zhang, Y. Zhi, and Z. Wei, \\n\\\"A multi-level modelling and fidelity evaluation method of digital \\ntwins for creating smart production equipment in Industry 4.0,\\\" \\nInternational Journal of Production Research, vol. 62, no. 10, pp. \\n3671-3689, 2024. \\n[18] X. Li, B. He, Z. P. Wang, Y. M. Zhou, G. Li, and X. Li, \\\"Toward \\nCognitive Digital Twin System of Human-Robot Collaboration \\nManipulation,\\\" IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and \\nEngineering, pp. 14, 2024 Sep, 2024. \\n[19] Z. D. Ji, C. L. Chen, J. P. He, S. Y. Zhu, and X. P. Guan, \\\"Learning-\\nBased Edge Sensing and Control Co-Design for Industrial Cyber-\\nPhysical System,\\\" IEEE Transactions on Automation Science and \\nEngineering, vol. 20, no. 1, pp. 59-73, Jan, 2023. \\n[20] K. Stanković, D. Jelić, N. Tomašević, and A. Krstić, \\n\\\"Manufacturing process optimization for real-time quality control \\nin multi-regime conditions: Tire tread production use case,\\\" Journal \\nof Manufacturing Systems, vol. 76, pp. 293-313, 2024. \\n[21] R. Diaz, and A. Ardalan, \\\"Innovating in data-driven production \\nenvironments: simulation analysis of Net-CONWIP priority rule,\\\" \\nIndustrial Management & Data Systems, vol. 123, no. 5, pp. 1569-\\n1598, 2023. \\n[22] R. Romero-Silva, and G. Hernández-López, \\\"Shop-floor scheduling \\nas a competitive advantage: A study on the relevance of cyber-\\nphysical systems in different manufacturing contexts,\\\" \\nInternational journal of production economics, vol. 224, pp. \\n107555, 2020. \\n[23] W. Lin, \\\"An integrated digital twin simulation and scheduling \\nsystem under cyber-physical digital twin environment.\\\" IEEE \\nInternational Conference on Industrial Engineering and \\nEngineering Management (IEEM), pp. 0231-0235, 2022. \\n[24] J. Vyskočil, P. Douda, P. Novák, and B. Wally. \\\" A Digital Twin-\\nBased Distributed Manufacturing Execution System for Industry \\n4.0 with AI-Powered On-The-Fly Replanning Capabilities,\\\"  \\nSustainability, vol. 15, no. 7, pp. 6251, 2023.  \\n[25] L. X. Yun, S. Y. Ma, L. Li, and Y. Liu, \\\"CPS-enabled and \\nknowledge-aided demand response strategy for sustainable \\nmanufacturing,\\\" Advanced Engineering Informatics, vol. 52, pp. \\