  "status": "success",
  "output": "{\n  \"content\": \"Computers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\nAvailable online 13 June 2024\\n0360-8352/© 2024 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.Routing protocols for B2B e-commerce logistics in cyber-physical \\ninternet (CPI) \\nXinye Qua, Ming Lib,c,d,*, Zhiyuan Ouyangb,c, Chung-lam Nga, George Q. Huangb,c \\naDepartment of Industrial and Manufacturing Systems Engineering, The University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China \\nbDepartment of Industrial and Systems Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China \\ncResearch Institute for Advanced Manufacturing, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China \\ndResearch Centre for Digital Transformation of Tourism, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China   \\nARTICLE INFO  \\nKeywords: \\nCyber-physical internet \\nPhysical internet \\nLogistics \\nRouting ABSTRACT  \\nCurrently, the boom in B2B e-commerce logistics and the fragmentation of logistics networks have put \\ntremendous pressure on the efficient and economical routing of packages. The idea of the cyber-physical internet \\n(CPI) in logistics is to establish a new paradigm for sending and receiving goods, similar to how sending and \\nreceiving instant messages over the internet might be a solution. This work focuses on the routing process in B2B \\ne-commerce logistics driven by the CPI. A framework of B2B e-commerce logistics in CPI networks is proposed to \\nintegrate logistics networks with components such as CPI routers, routing tables, and autonomous logistics \\nsystems (ALSs). Routing protocols within and among ALSs are proposed for making routing decisions based on \\ninformation in routing tables for efficient and economical routing. Finally, two B2B e-commerce logistics sce-\\nnarios are utilized to simulate the routing protocols in CPI networks to prove their feasibility, and their sensi-\\ntivity is analysed. This work may support the improvement of B2B e-commerce logistics networks and intelligent \\nlogistics in Industry 4.0.   \\n1.Introduction \\nRouting is a crucial aspect of business-to-business (B2B) e-commerce \\nlogistics because it directly impacts the cost and time of transportation \\nand reflects the level of network connectivity in B2B e-commerce lo-\\ngistics (Prajapati et al., 2021 ). As B2B e-commerce logistics has become \\nan increasingly important activity (Hertz & Alfredsson, 2003 ) and ac-\\ncounts for more than 90 % of total e-commerce logistics (Leinbach, \\n2007 ), it places significant pressure on the routing process. The pros-\\nperity of B2B e-commerce logistics has created challenges in terms of the \\ncapacity and efficiency of transportation routes, and customers \\nincreasingly demand timely routing. At the same time, the connections \\nof logistics networks are complex and fragmented (Schoder et al., 2016 ), \\nwith transport routes or networks being owned mainly by various or-\\nganizations (Heskett, 1976 ), making it difficult to organize the entire \\nrouting process efficiently and economically. Even with the concepts of \\nsupply chains and vertical coordination, due to the rise of traditional \\nthird-party logistics (3PL) and freight forwarders, this issue has persisted \\n(Carbone & Stone, 2005 ). Consequently, to address this situation, the \\nnew approach of the physical internet (PI) has revolutionized the efficiency and economics of routing (Kaup et al., n.d.). \\nThe concept of the physical internet (PI) was introduced by Mon-\\ntreuil (2011) as a solution to the economic and social challenges asso-\\nciated with the movement, handling, and supply of physical objects \\nwithin global logistics networks. The routing of containers is addressed \\nin the design and operation of PI logistics networks, which constitute \\none of the two main focuses of current research on the PI; the other main \\nfocus is the design of PI infrastructure (Fazili et al., 2017 ). In the PI, \\nstandardized, modular containers are proposed to be routed efficiently, \\nas information is routed on the digital internet (Montreuil et al., 2012 ). \\nMost research on the PI has focused on proposing concepts and strategies \\nfor integrating transportation routes into a network structure in the \\nphysical world by sharing logistics facilities and resources (Matusiewicz, \\n2020 ). The routing problem in the PI still relies heavily on optimization \\nalgorithms in vehicle routing problems (VRPs) based on traditional lo-\\ngistics (Sarraj et al., 2012 ). To avoid the NP-hard characteristic of VRPs \\n(Raza et al., 2022 ), several routing protocols have been proposed, \\nfocusing mainly on routing between different logistics service providers \\n(LSPs) (Gontara et al., 2018 ) and the freight transportation service \\nprocurement problem (Lafkihi et al., 2019; Briand et al., 2022 ). \\n*Corresponding author at: Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Hong Kong, China. \\nE-mail address: ming.li@polyu.edu.hk (M. Li).  \\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering \\nu{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2mkto!\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.cie.2024.110293 \\nReceived 22 November 2023; Received in revised form 29 May 2024; Accepted 8 June 2024   \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n2However, existing physical internet (PI) networks have not achieved \\ninformation synchronization, hindering their ability to integrate route \\nand carrier information for independent decision-making. Therefore, \\nfuture research on the PI should prioritize the strategic role of \\ncommunication and information technologies (Maslari ˘c et al., 2016 ). \\nSince the PI does not demonstrate the characteristics of the internet, \\nthe concept of the cyber-physical internet (CPI) is proposed in this work \\nto realize the collaboration of information and correspondence between \\nthe virtual world and the physical world logistics networks in the PI. The \\nCPI is a new paradigm for sending and receiving goods in logistics \\nnetworks, similar to sending and receiving instant messages over the \\ninternet with an additional cyber layer in the PI. This aligns with the \\nIndustry 4.0 assumption that products and storage facilities in the form \\nof cyber-physical systems (CPSs) (Maslari ˘c et al., 2016 ), including those \\nin information systems, should uniquely communicate (Song et al., \\n2023 ), generate, and control themselves (Zhao et al., 2023 ) in global \\nnetworks. This work aims to construct a CPI network framework and \\napply its internet and logistics properties to the routing process of B2B e- \\ncommerce logistics to make efficient and economical routing decisions \\nfor physical packages. Therefore, this paper aims to answer the \\nfollowing two research questions:  \\n≡How can the fragmented and extensive networks of B2B e-commerce \\nlogistics be integrated with the idea of the CPI?  \\n≡How can the concept of datagram routing in digital networks be \\napplied to the scenario of physical package routing in CPI networks \\nto make the routing process economical and efficient? \\nIn response to the above research questions, a framework of CPI \\nnetworks in B2B e-commerce logistics is proposed to integrate frag-\\nmented logistics networks. In terms of network structure, due to the \\nlarge scope and complexity of logistics networks, the concept of the \\nautonomous logistics system (ALS) is introduced into CPI networks. \\nMoreover, critical components of CPI networks related to routing are \\nalso designed. Logistics nodes in B2B e-commerce networks are inte-\\ngrated into CPI routers in CPI networks. CPI routers have internet con-\\nnections in the cyber layer and physical connections (transport \\nchannels) among themselves, which help them transfer information in \\ndigital networks and physical packages in logistics networks. To make \\nrouting decisions, we design a routing table in the CPI router, including \\nrouting protocols and metrics. Since the CPI network consists of multiple \\nALSs, routing protocols within and among the ALSs are proposed sepa-\\nrately so that efficient and economical routing decisions can be made. \\nFinally, a numerical study is conducted to simulate and validate the \\nproposed CPI network framework and routing protocols. With the above \\nresearch questions and work, the academic and engineering contribu -\\ntions of this research are as follows:  \\n≡This work introduces components such as the cyber layer as well as \\nthe CPI router and routing table into B2B e-commerce logistics net-\\nworks on the basis of the PI at an early stage and establishes the \\nframework of CPI networks and their basic operation process in B2B \\ne-commerce logistics routing. This provides an important reference \\nand guidance for the construction of CPI networks for specific sce-\\nnarios and provides new paradigms and strategies for solving the \\nproblems of B2B e-commerce logistics.  \\n≡This work proposes two routing protocols to solve the problem of \\nrouting physical packages within and among the ALSs in CPI net-\\nworks, enabling the use of CPI routers and routing tables to make \\nrouting decisions and achieve efficient and economical routing in \\nnetworks of different sizes. This promotes the technological inno-\\nvation and development of the logistics industry and provides \\nguidance and a case study for the development of logistics in the \\ncontext of Industry 4.0 CPSs. \\nThis article is structured as follows. Section 2 analyses the current research on the routing problem in B2B e-commerce logistics and on the \\nPI and CPI. Section 3 introduces the framework of CPI networks in B2B \\ne-commerce logistics and the concepts of the CPI router and routing \\ntable in CPI networks. Section 4 describes the routing problem in the CPI \\nwith a mathematical model, and routing protocols and algorithms \\nwithin and among ALSs are proposed. Section 5 presents a numerical \\nstudy of two B2B e-commerce logistics scenarios based on the frame -\\nwork and protocols of CPI routing proposed in this work, followed by an \\nanalysis of the results. Section 6 presents the conclusions and directions \\nfor future research. \\n2.Literature review \\n2.1. Routing problem in B2B e-commerce logistics \\nRouting is a well-known approach in several areas, such as computer \\nnetworks, logistics, and transportation (Sarraj et al., 2014b ). In current \\nB2B e-commerce logistics, the routing problem refers to finding the \\noptimal transport route from a source to a destination, which corre -\\nsponds to finding transport routes from factories to wholesalers or re-\\ntailers (Prajapati et al., 2021 ). This problem involves several factors, \\nsuch as delivery locations, delivery time windows, vehicle capacities, \\nand constraints such as route conditions and customer preferences. The \\nmain objective in solving the routing problem is to minimize trans -\\nportation costs while meeting customer demands and ensuring timely \\ndeliveries. This involves finding the most efficient routes that optimize \\ndistance, delivery time, fuel consumption, and vehicle utilization (Vidal \\net al., 2020 ). The current strategy of logistics service providers (LSPs) for \\nsolving this problem is mainly to use optimization algorithms (Karels \\net al., 2024 ), which can be crudely divided into three categories: exact, \\nheuristic, and learning-based algorithms (Li et al., 2022 ). Zhang et al. \\n(2021) solved the forwards and reverse vehicle routing problem for B2C \\ne-commerce logistics using an exact optimization method. Prajapati \\net al. (2021) identified the best route in terms of environmental impact, \\neconomy, speed and overall transportation cost using a branch and \\nbound algorithm. Zhang et al. (2023) solved the multiwarehouse \\ncontaminated pathway problem with a time window using an improved \\nadaptive extensive neighbourhood search, tabu search, and oriented ant \\ncolony algorithm to explore reducing carbon emissions and integrating \\nsustainability into e-commerce logistics. Prajapati et al. (2019) captured \\nthe significant constraints related to availability, demand, maintenance, \\nand time horizons by developing an integer nonlinear programming \\nmathematical model using metaheuristics, i.e., GA, to obtain the optimal \\nrouting of orders from origin to destination in B2B e-commerce. Many \\nscholars have also considered the impact of 3PL in B2B e-commerce \\nlogistics. (Ou et al., 2022 ) proposed a coordinate representation particle \\nswarm optimization for the open vehicle routing problem with time \\nwindows, considering 3PL by improving mixed integer linear pro-\\ngramming as a solution. \\n2.2. Physical internet and cyber-physical internet \\nThe idea of the physical internet (PI) first appeared in an article in \\nThe Economist in 2006 (Markillie & Paul, 2006) before the concept of \\nthe cyber-physical internet (CPI) was proposed. Montreuil (2011) \\nintroduced the concept of the physical internet (PI) after analysing \\nthirteen aspects of global unsustainability. Later, Montreuil et al. (2013) \\nidentified the PI as an open global logistics system based on physical, \\ndigital, and operational interconnection through encapsulation, in-\\nterfaces, and protocols, with a working principle and basis similar to \\nthose of the digital internet. The PI disrupts traditional business models \\nand creates a new environment of collaborative and cooperative logis-\\ntics that spans organizations, industries, and interorganizational net-\\nworks (Montreuil, 2011 ), which essentially changes the way physical \\nobjects are handled, moved, stored, fulfilled, supplied, and used (Raja-\\nhonka et al., 2019 ) to achieve global logistics efficiency and X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n3sustainability (Crainic & Montreuil, 2016 ). Compared to fragmented \\nlogistics networks, Sarraj et al. (2014b) hypothesized that inter-\\nconnected logistics networks are much better because such networks in \\nthe PI involve the sharing of resources such as containers, load transfers, \\ntransfer hubs, and logistics providers. In contrast, fragmented logistics \\nnetworks involve minimal sharing of information or infrastructure. The \\nphysical internet can be regarded as a logistics service network based on \\ninterconnection or hyperconnection (Oger et al., 2018; Ballot et al., \\n2014 ); hence, an essential assumption of the PI is that the effective \\ninteroperability of physical and information flow between networks can \\nbe obtained through modularity and standardization (Montreuil et al., \\n2015 ). This relies on the physical elements that make up the PI: PI nodes, \\nPI movers, and PI containers. The description of the PI by Montreuil \\net al. (2013) , which includes generic interconnections, encapsulation, \\nstandard intelligent interfaces, standard coordination protocols, logis-\\ntics network enablers, and open logistics systems, further explains these \\nphysical elements. \\nThe PI framework provides a new perspective for solving the routing \\nproblem in logistics networks and has attracted the attention of many \\nscholars. Recent research has focused mainly on constructing PI logistics \\nnetworks (Sun et al., 2018; Crainic & Montreuil, 2016 ), applying the PI \\nto different routing scenarios (Gontara et al., 2018 ), and adding new \\nconstraints to existing routing models to reflect the advantages of the PI \\n(Fazili et al., 2017 ). The objectives of most research are to reduce the \\ncost of transportation and improve the efficiency of the routing process. \\nA topological model of PI logistics networks was constructed according \\nto the topology of computer networks (Sarraj et al., 2014b ), which was \\nused to help build an organizational model between factories, ware-\\nhouses, and retailers. From the perspective of a logistics network, Ballot \\net al. (2012) propose that the routing solutions for the PI should be \\ndecentralized because the flow assignment problems are large-scale with \\nmultiple decision-makers. Additionally, some researchers have associ -\\nated the idea of the PI with classical optimization problems and algo-\\nrithms in vehicle routing. Sarraj et al. (2012) proposed a routing \\nprotocol in the PI that relies primarily on the Dijkstra algorithm to find \\nthe shortest route between each pair of nodes, including parameters \\nsuch as the time and cost spent in the routing process (Sarraj et al., \\n2012 ). Yao (2017) applied the PI to a one-stop delivery scenario and \\nestablished a scheduling optimization model based on the ant algorithm. Mohamed et al. (2017) explored the operational urban transportation \\nproblem of PI containers under interconnected city logistics (ICL) con-\\nsiderations. Dong and Franklin (2021) related the PI model to the classic \\nshortest route problem and the dynamic traffic flow assignment problem \\nto construct a network model using graph theory. Peng et al. (2020) \\noptimized the delivery network route by reducing the container trans -\\nportation distance, aiming to minimize economic, environmental, and \\nsocial objectives. In addition to the original constraints on the PI- \\ncontainer routing process, Fazili et al. (2017) also limited the \\nmaximum return time of truck drivers. \\nWhile the physical internet references the digital internet model in \\nseveral aspects, its routing mechanisms do not substantially resemble \\nthose of the internet. It still relies only on the idea and scenarios of \\nvehicle routing problems instead of focusing on developing new para-\\ndigms for packet routing or PI-container routing in PI networks (Gontara \\net al., 2018 ). To improve this situation, Gontara et al. (2018) focused on \\ntruck-transported PI containers and established the PI-BGP algorithm for \\nfinding the optimal route according to the internet ’s border gateway \\nprotocol (BGP). Current research on routing in the PI is limited to lo-\\ngistics networks in the physical world. \\n2.3. Summary \\nAfter reviewing the traditional approaches to the routing problem in \\nB2B e-commerce logistics and the approaches in the PI, we found that \\nthe following limitations still exist. \\nRouting is an essential issue in B2B e-commerce logistics and has \\nattracted the interest of many researchers, who have proposed solutions \\nin terms of methods and operations. At the methodological level, \\ntraditional exact and heuristic algorithms employed in routing optimi -\\nzation for B2B e-commerce logistics have inherent limitations in terms of \\nflexibility and scalability. The search process of these algorithms may be \\ntime-consuming, and the optimality of their results is only sometimes \\nguaranteed. Moreover, learning-based algorithms require more data \\navailability and generality. Additionally, at the operational level, third- \\nparty logistics (3PL) and horizontal collaboration are common strategies \\nin current logistics. Coordination and communication can be compli -\\ncated due to the collaboration between multiple suppliers and partners; \\nthus, sharing and collaboration processes between different partners \\nFig. 1.B2B e-commerce logistics in the CPI.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n4may need to be more efficient, resulting in compromised accuracy and \\ntimeliness of routing. \\nResearchers have also made several attempts at routing in the PI, and \\nthese studies are very informative and inspire us to think further. \\nAlthough the PI references the digital internet model, the routing \\napproach in the PI still needs to be revised with the core idea of routing \\non the internet. It still relies heavily on the ideas and scenarios of vehicle \\nrouting problems without developing new paradigms for packet routing \\nor PI container routing in PI networks. \\n3.CPI networks \\nThis section focuses on the framework of CPI networks, with some of \\nits essential components in B2B e-commerce logistics, as the concept of \\nCPI networks is the basis for routing in the CPI. \\n3.1. From computer networks to CPI networks \\nThe data routing process in computer networks can be summarized \\nin the following steps. First, the source node generates the datagram and \\nselects the next-hop router based on the destination address. Next, the \\ndatagram is forwarded between routers, and each router determines the \\nnext hop and sends the datagram according to its routing table. This \\nprocess is repeated until the datagram reaches the destination node. \\nFinally, the destination node receives the datagram and processes it \\naccordingly. Through these steps, the datagram can be successfully \\ntransmitted from the origin to the destination, completing the process of \\ndatagram routing. The above process relies on critical components such \\nas routers, routing tables, datagrams, and routing protocols. Routers \\nselect the next hop based on the routing table. Datagrams are forwarded \\nbetween routers, and routing information is exchanged and updated \\nthrough routing protocols for efficient and accurate data transmission. \\nNumerous data transmissions are handled in computer networks \\nevery moment, and generally, these data are transmitted with few delays \\nor errors. Additionally, the methods of resource allocation (Song et al., \\n2023; Zhang et al., 2023; Zhao et al., 2024 ) and node control (Song \\net al., 2023 ) in computer networks and real-life large-scale networks \\nensure network efficiency and stability. Consequently, the routing \\napproach suggests that in today ’s fast-growing B2B e-commerce logis-\\ntics, it might be feasible to use similar ideas to build a logistics network \\nfor routing logistics packages. Thus, based on computer networks and \\nthe PI, we designed the framework of CPI networks and present it in this \\nwork in the context of B2B e-commerce logistics, as shown in Fig. 1. Based on the PI, the CPI has a cyber layer that connects the components \\nof the physical internet, such as logistics nodes and PI containers, to \\nrouters and datagrams in cyberspace, which constitute the network \\ncomponents of the CPI, such as CPI routers and standardized physical \\npackages. In CPI networks, the origins, logistics nodes, and destinations \\nof B2B e-commerce logistics correspond to routers in computer net-\\nworks. In a CPI network, the origin, logistics node, and destination are \\ndesigned as CPI routers, which function similarly to routers in computer \\nnetworks. In B2B e-commerce logistics, the routing process involves the \\nCPI router of a factory receiving order information, obtaining the link \\nconnection information of the CPI networks to the CPI router of a \\nwholesaler at the destination through the cyber layer, and selecting the \\ntransport route of the physical packages based on the routing tables in \\nthe CPI routers. \\n3.2. The CPI network framework \\nCPI networks are constructed based on the digital internet model and \\ncomprise three fundamental components: flows, nodes, and arcs. In the \\ndigital internet, the three components are datagrams, routers, and \\ninternet connections. In the PI, all three components are physical entities \\nof the logistics process. The flows are PI containers (Montreuil et al., \\n2015 ); the nodes are warehouses, plants, distribution centres, or ports; \\nand the arcs are freight with transportation means and lines. According \\nto the concept of the CPI, in this work, a cyber layer is added to the PI to \\nachieve information collaboration. The CPI’s primary purpose is to \\ntransport goods in the logistics network; hence, a significant object in \\nthe transport flow is a physical package. Nevertheless, information \\nconnections between nodes are also needed to determine logistics route \\nselection before transporting physical packages, so the flow also in-\\ncludes datagrams. The logistics nodes in the CPI are the same as those in \\nthe PI and represent the transit stations, distribution centres, and ports. \\nHowever, to make the nodes in the network identify each other ’s in-\\nformation and determine the transportation routes, it is necessary to set \\nup CPI routers at each logistics node to exchange information so that the \\nCPI routers route and forward not only physical goods but also data \\nflows. The arcs in the CPI need to transmit datagrams as well as physical \\npackages. Hence, the arcs include internet connections for communi -\\ncating information, transport channels, and transport services for \\ntransporting physical packages. \\nComputer networks aim to transmit datagrams from sources to re-\\nceivers through multiple routers of various intermediate networks. \\nTherefore, due to the complexity of the computer networks, the \\nFig. 2.Routing tables for a computer network and the CPI.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n5intermediate networks are composed of complex connections of all \\nrouters and combinations of autonomous systems (ASs) (Tanenbaum, \\n2003). Segmenting networks into multiple ASs enables organizations or \\nbusinesses to independently manage and control their networks without \\ninterfering with or affecting other networks. Each AS develops its \\nrouting policy to forward datagrams from one network to another \\noptimally. The ASs communicate through routing protocols to exchange \\nrouting information and control network traffic, which helps the ASs \\nconnect. Similarly, B2B e-commerce logistics networks aim to enable the \\nefficient flow of goods and information between e-commerce businesses. \\nDue to the large scale and complexity of B2B e-commerce logistics \\nnetworks, we divide CPI networks into autonomous logistics systems \\n(ALSs). In the same ALS, all CPI routers share the same routing protocol, \\nand all CPI routers in an ALS are managed by a single logistics service \\nprovider, which provides more freedom and flexibility for routing \\nwithin an ALS. On the other hand, the routing protocols of different ALSs \\nmay differ, and the route information exchanged among ALSs is \\nrestricted. \\n3.3. CPI router and routing table \\nA router is a primary component of computer networks that connects \\nnetworks and is responsible for routing and forwarding datagrams. \\nDatagrams are usually forwarded through a network from one router to \\nanother until they reach their destination node. Similarly, CPI routers \\nare located at the nodes in a B2B e-commerce logistics network, such as \\nnodes representing transshipment centres, factories, and warehouses, as \\nshown in Fig. 1. They can not only deliver order and route information \\nthrough the cyber layer of the logistics network by routing and for-\\nwarding datagrams like traditional routers but also have logistics func-\\ntions for routing and forwarding the physical packages of logistics \\norders. \\nIn computer networks, routing and forwarding datagrams by routers \\nrequire updating route information and selecting the next hop through \\nrouting protocols, which rely heavily on routing tables, which are \\nessential data tables in routers. Routing tables contain topological in-\\nformation about neighbouring routers and networks and store infor-\\nmation about routes to specific network addresses and the routing \\nmetrics of the routes. While there are various routing protocols in a \\nrouting table, there are also different routing protocols within and \\namong ASs. Fig. 2(a) shows an example of a routing table for a computer \\nnetwork. \\nIn this work, we aim to route physical packages from sources to \\ndestinations through CPI networks; hence, the routing table in the CPI \\nrouter also plays a vital role in the routing process. Routers need to \\ndetermine the transportation routes of physical packages based on the \\ndatabase and routing protocols in the routing table. Therefore, based on \\nthe routing tables of computer networks, routing tables in CPI routers \\ninvolve logistic characteristics. Since the design of a complete routing \\ntable is outside the scope of this work, only the contents of the routing \\ntable related to this work are listed here, which mainly include desti-\\nnation, next hop, protocol, metric, and transportation information, as \\nshown in Fig. 2(b). In the routing table, destination refers to the IP \\naddress of the destination of a logistics order, and the next hop is the IP \\naddress of the next CPI router in the route to the destination. Protocol refers to the rules and basis for generating a transportation route for an \\norder. Transportation in the routing table refers to the transportation \\nmode along the route. \\nAmong the components of a routing table, routing protocols and \\nmetrics are the critical elements that are involved in routing decisions. \\nRouting protocols in computer networks facilitate the selection of the \\noptimal routes to ensure that datagrams can reach their destinations as \\nquickly as possible (Stavrou & Pitsillides, 2010). Metrics are essential for \\nevaluating the connectivity quality in the route selection process. \\nNevertheless, the use of metrics is directly related to the type of routing \\nprotocol applied in the networks. In most general terms, a metric is a set \\nof attributes of a route, consisting of any possible values used by the \\nrouting protocol to select the best route. When routing protocols are \\nused, they must guarantee route stability, and more importantly, the \\nselected routes should provide the optimal efficiency parameters (e.g., \\nhop counts, link quality parameters, and interference) within the ranges \\nof the selected attributes; thus, all these efficiency parameters can be \\nused as metrics in the routing table (Owczarek & Zwierzykowski, 2013). \\nSimilarly, in CPI networks, suitable metrics need to be selected to pro-\\nvide the optimal parameters of efficiency for the routing process in B2B \\ne-commerce logistics. On-time delivery of goods is essential for both \\nfactories and wholesalers, and choosing the shortest distance and fastest \\nmode of transportation could shorten the delivery time, satisfy buyers’ \\nneeds, and increase customer satisfaction. The transportation cost is an \\nimportant consideration in B2B e-commerce logistics. Choosing low-cost \\ntransportation options could reduce logistics costs and increase profit -\\nability. At the same time, wholesalers can also benefit from lower \\ntransportation costs, resulting in more competitive pricing. For B2B e- \\ncommerce, the time required for the transportation and delivery of \\ngoods is on the order of hours or days, whereas the time required for \\ninformation transmission in the routing process and the computation \\ntime for the routing decision are on the order of milliseconds or seconds \\n(Mao et al., 2017); hence, the information transmission time and \\ncomputation time are not the focus of this work (Sarraj et al., 2014a). \\nTherefore, important parameters in routing in B2B e-commerce logistics \\ninclude the distance and time for package transportation and the \\ntransportation cost, among others (Zhang et al., 2017), which are used \\nfor routing decisions as metrics in the routing table in this work. \\n4.CPI routing protocols \\nBased on the framework and components of the CPI networks pro-\\nposed in Section 3, this section focuses on modelling the routing prob-\\nlems and proposing routing protocols for B2B e-commerce logistics in \\nCPI networks. The protocols here are defined as rules in the CPI for \\nselecting a transport route in a network within a particular area based on \\nthe routing information in the routing table of the entire topology map \\nand for deciding which route the physical packages should follow, \\nthereby helping the physical packages reach their destinations. \\n4.1. Problem description and modelling \\nIn B2B e-commerce logistics, the routing process aims to ensure the \\nefficient and timely delivery of physical packages. This involves estab -\\nlishing CPI connections between factories at the origin and wholesalers \\nat the destination and then choosing transport routes through routing \\ntables of CPI routers on these network nodes. This process relies on the \\ninformation in the routing tables, such as the IP addresses of the origin \\nand destination; metrics such as distance between CPI routers, trans -\\nportation cost, and transportation time; and, most importantly, the \\nrouting protocols between CPI routers. Since routing protocols are the \\nbasis for route selection decisions, we need to propose routing protocols \\nin the routing table for selecting efficient and economical transportation \\nroutes in CPI networks and to choose appropriate algorithms for the \\nprotocols. \\nBefore proposing routing protocols, we first develop a mathematical Table 1 \\nNotations used in the model.  \\no Starting point \\nh, i, j Node indices \\nI Set of node i \\nCij Transportation cost on arc (i,j) \\nCi Transhipping cost at i \\nTi Transhipping time at i \\nxij Binary variable that equals 1 when there is an arc between i and j \\nyij Binary variable that equals 1 when there is transshipping at i  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n6model for this problem. Although the transmission of information is also \\nincluded in the CPI, since its cost and time are negligible relative to those \\nfor transporting physical packages (Sarraj et al., 2014a ), our objective \\nfunction includes the time and cost at the physical level as the metrics in \\nthe routing table. Therefore, the optimization objective of this paper \\nmainly considers the following two factors:  \\n≡Costs: transit cost and transportation cost;  \\n≡Time: transit time and transportation time. \\nAccording to the optimization objective and the and notations in \\nTable 1, there are two objective functions, (1) and (2): \\nminZ1min̂\\niCj∃ACijxij̂\\ni∃TrCiyi (1)  \\nminZ 2min̂\\niCj∃ATijxij̂\\ni∃TrTiyi (2)  \\n̂\\nh∃Ixhi\\u0000̂\\nj∃Ixhjfx|\\n〈\\n⎜1Cid\\n0C⊥i∃Tr\\n\\u00001Cio(3)  \\nxij≼1⊥iCj∃A (4)  \\nyi≼1⊥i∃Tr (5)  \\n|\\n〈\\n⎜yi̂\\nj∃Ixij⊥i∃Tr\\n̂\\nh∃Ixhiyi⊥i∃Tr(6)  \\nxij〉\\n0\\n1⊥iCj∃A (7)  yi〉\\n0\\n1⊥i∃Tr (8)  \\nyi0⊥i∃⊔oCd⊓ (9) \\nEqs. (1) and (2) are the objective functions to be optimized in the \\ntransportation process. Objective function (1) minimizes the cost, which \\nconsists of the transshipment and transportation costs. Objective func-\\ntion (2) minimizes the time, which consists of the transhipment time and \\nthe transportation time. \\nConstraint (3) balances the flow constraints at each point. A value of \\n0 means that the point is an intermediate node and the number of \\ninflowed physical packages is equal to the outflow, while a value of 1 \\nmeans that there are only inflows and no outflows and that it is the \\ndestination of the transportation. A value of \\u00001 indicates that there are \\nonly outflows and no inflows and that it is the starting point of the \\ntransportation. Constraint (4) ensures that no transportation process \\ninvolves splitting goods in the physical package. Constraint (5) ensures \\nthat each physical package is transited at most once at a node. Constraint \\n(6) indicates that if a physical package is transited at a node, both the \\nnode and the two arcs connected to it must be in the final transportation \\nroute of the physical package. Constraints (7) and (8) denote the vari-\\nable domain constraints. Constraint (9) ensures no transhipment of the \\norigin or destination. \\n4.2. ALS routing protocol \\nIn computer networks, routing protocols for ASs are categorized into \\ninterior and exterior AS routing protocols. Interior routing protocols are \\nmainly used to select the optimal routes within an AS and to forward \\ndata. In contrast, exterior AS routing protocols are mainly used to \\ntransfer and choose routing information among different ASs. The \\ncategorization of routing protocols into interior and exterior routing \\nprotocols helps simplify and optimize the routing process of the network \\nFig. 3.The interior routing protocols between the internet and CPI.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n7and improve the manageability, flexibility, and scalability of the \\nnetwork while also satisfying different levels of routing requirements \\n(Tanenbaum, 2003 ). Similarly, as discussed in Section 3, CPI networks \\nare partitioned into multiple autonomous logistics systems (ALSs), \\nwhere different logistics service providers may manage different ALSs. \\nPhysical packages that need to be transported from the origin to the \\ndestination will pass through several ALSs, and this process involves \\nrouting physical packages within and among different ALSs. Therefore, \\nthis paper proposes a routing protocol within an ALS and among ALSs \\nfor routing physical packages in CPI networks. \\nThe routing protocol within an ALS is used to choose the transport \\nroute of physical packages within the ALS, which is realized mainly by \\nthe CPI router completing the following steps, as shown in Fig. 3. This \\nprocess is not static, and the status of routes within the ALS is updated at \\nregular intervals. This figure also compares the internal routing protocol \\nprocesses on the internet.  \\n(1) First, the CPI router at the origin is responsible for discovering its \\nneighbouring nodes, obtaining the IP addresses of its neighbours, \\nand establishing connections with them to ensure that there are \\navailable transport channels.  \\n(2) After the connections have been established, the CPI routers \\nwithin the ALS obtain information about parameters such as the \\ntransportation cost and time, distance, and CO2 emissions be-\\ntween the origin and the destination of the other routers. The CPI \\nrouter at the origin selects one or a combination of these pa-\\nrameters as the metric in the routing tables of all CPI routers in \\nthis ALS.  \\n(3) The CPI routers in this ALS construct packets containing all the \\npreviously known route information, including the IP address and \\nmetric.  \\n(4) The CPI routers in this ALS send these packets to the surrounding \\nCPI routers and receive packets from others. The topological re-\\nlationships among the CPI routers are obtained to construct a \\ncomplete logistics network diagram with route information.  \\n(5) The CPI router at the origin selects the transport route according \\nto the routing table. After that, the physical package follows the \\nroute chosen by the CPI router at the origin, while the role of the \\nrouters on the route is mainly to forward the package according \\nto the corresponding outbound route in the routing table. \\nThe existing routing protocols in the PI mostly rely on the Dijkstra \\nalgorithm to find the optimal route (Gontara et al., 2018 ). This study \\nuses the A* algorithm in the interior routing protocol in the CPI to obtain \\nthe transport routes. The A* algorithm is a graph traversal and route \\nsearch algorithm used in many computer science fields due to its \\ncompleteness, optimality, and efficiency (Sun et al., 2018 ). It can be \\nregarded as an extension of Dijkstra ’s algorithm and achieves better \\nperformance by using heuristics to guide its search (Mouh, 2023 ). 4.3. Routing protocol among ALSs \\nAfter proposing a routing protocol within each ALS, we still need to \\ndetermine how physical packages should be routed among ALSs. Rout-\\ning between different autonomous systems requires the border gateway \\nprotocol (BGP) of computer networks. Since the internet comprises \\nmany autonomous systems, each with its routing policy, the BGP helps \\nexchange limited routing information and select the optimal routes \\namong different ASs, which can adapt to the changing network topology \\nand protect the routing information of the autonomous systems. \\nSimilarly, due to the complexity and large scale of CPI logistics \\nnetworks, sharing routing information for routing decisions throughout \\nwhole CPI networks is impossible. Therefore, we also need to establish a \\nrouting protocol for ALSs. This approach is combined with the routing \\nprotocols within the ALSs to finalize the routing from the origin to the \\ndestination. Like computer networks, ALSs in the CPI have well-defined \\nboundaries, in this case, the borders of cities or countries, depending on \\nthe complexity of the logistics networks. At the data level, an ALS col-\\nlects part of the logistics information from its interior logistics network \\nand propagates it to other ALSs. It also receives logistics information \\nfrom other ALSs and propagates it to border routers. At the physical \\nlevel, there are physical boundaries among different ALSs, and physical \\npackages pass through a border router located at a customs or city \\nborder before they can access another ALS. \\nRouting between different ALSs relies on routers at the ALS border, \\nrepresented in the transport network by logistic nodes located at the \\nborder of the ALS, to transmit information and physical packages. \\nRouting between different ALSs requires the following steps, as shown in \\nFig. 4.  \\n(1) The border router in ALS 1 forwards the order information of \\nphysical packages to neighbouring ALSs, including the quantity, \\nvolume, time upper bound, and destination. Other ALSs between \\nthe origin and the destination also start exchanging route infor-\\nmation with each other.  \\n(2) The border router of ALS n receives the order information. It \\nreturns information about the reachability of the transport route \\nbetween ALSs, indicating whether the physical packages can \\nreach the destination via ALS n.  \\n(3) If the physical package can reach the destination via ALS n, a \\nneighbour relationship is established between ALS1 and ALS n, \\nand the border router of ALS n returns the total metric expected to \\nbe required to pass through ALS n. Similarly, the neighbour re-\\nlationships between ALS n and other ALSs are established in this \\nway.  \\n(4) ALS 1 obtains the optimal ALS_PATH to the destination based on \\nthe metric value returned by each neighbour, and finally, the \\nborder router in ALS 1 selects the ALS on the optimal ALS_PATH as \\nthe next hop of the physical package. \\nFig. 4.Exterior routing process.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n8The reachability mentioned in step 2 is defined as the ability of a \\ncertain number of physical packages to pass through the channel be-\\ntween two ALSs and then reach their destination. The ALS_PATH here \\nconsists of all the ALSs contained in an exterior route. In exterior rout-\\ning, finding the shortest route between ALSs is still based on the A* al-\\ngorithm mentioned in Section 4.2, and the calculation of the metric for \\nrouting includes the sum of the metrics within and among ALSs. \\n5.Numerical study \\nAfter presenting the structure of the CPI networks and the routing \\nprotocols within and among ALSs for B2B e-commerce logistics, this \\nsection focuses on validating these networks through a numerical study. \\nTwo B2B e-commerce logistics scenarios are simulated, and a sensitivity \\nanalysis is conducted from three perspectives based on the simulation \\nresults. \\nThe research object of this work is B2B e-commerce logistics, char-\\nacterized by a large and stable transportation volume. Therefore, in the \\nnumerical study, there are no scattered small items, and the trans -\\nportation objects are all based on containers. The standardization of \\ncontainers as PI containers is an essential theory in the PI. Similarly, in \\nthe CPI, we define various standard sizes of physical packages to stan-\\ndardize the transportation objects. According to the background of B2B \\ne-commerce logistics, the physical packages in this work are containers \\nwith dimensions of 1203 cm*235 cm*239 cm. \\n5.1. Scenario description \\nTo effectively verify the CPI routing protocols in B2B e-commerce \\nlogistics, we consider two scenarios in the numerical study. \\nScenario 1: Small-range routing involving three factories and two \\nwholesalers, as shown in Fig. 5. The three factories are in Shenzhen and \\nDongguan, Guangdong Province, and the two wholesalers are in Tuen \\nMun and Yuen Long, Hong Kong. There are a few CPI routers in two ALSs \\nbetween the factories and wholesalers, which are distributed among the \\nlocations of transportation hubs. The two ALSs geographically border \\neach other, and the border between the two ALSs is the border between \\nHong Kong and mainland China. According to the administrative re-\\ngions, the border routers of these two ALSs are located at the ends of \\nthree ports: Shenzhen Bay Port, Futian Port, and Luohu Port. According \\nto the routing protocols and algorithm in Section 4, the CPI router at the \\norigin and the border routers first find several possible routes within the \\ntwo ALSs. Then, the border routers exchange information between the \\ntwo ALSs through the border routers. According to the ALS routing protocol, the distances and costs of six optimal routes are obtained from \\nthe three factories in ALS_Shenzhen to the two wholesalers in \\nALS_HongKong. \\nScenario 2: Large-range routing involving four factories in Wuhan, \\nHefei, Hangzhou, and Wenzhou and two wholesalers in Tuen Mun and \\nYuen Long, Hong Kong. There are a few CPI routers between the fac-\\ntories, and the wholesalers are distributed between large cities as hubs \\nfor transshipment. Due to the long distances between the factories and \\nwholesalers, these CPI routers and their starting and ending points are \\ndivided into six ALSs in this scenario, as shown in Fig. 5. To reach the \\nwholesalers from the factories, physical packages need to pass through \\nat least three ALSs, which also rely on the exterior protocol for routing. \\nIn the specific process of CPI routing, each physical package departs \\nfrom the factory, passes through CPI routers in the ALSs on the transport \\nroute according to the routing protocol based on the information in the \\nrouting table, and finally arrives at the wholesalers at the end. This \\nsection focuses only on truck-transported physical packages and makes \\nthe following assumptions:  \\n≡Fleet management is not involved.  \\n≡The consolidation and distribution of physical packages are not \\nconsidered.  \\n≡Only road transportation is considered at this stage. Multimodal \\ntransportation is not utilized. \\nFig. 5.GIS maps of the small-range and large-range scenarios.  \\nTable 2 \\nSimulation model and execution parameters.   \\nParameter Value \\nSmall range Truck speed 50–60 km/h \\nTranshipment time 10–30 min \\nNumber of transport channels 35 \\nNumber of border routers 6 \\nNumber of CPI routers 20 \\nNumber of ALSs 2 \\nNumber of trucks 10 \\nLarge range Truck speed 50–60 km/h \\nTranshipment time 10–30 min \\nNumber of transport channels 58 \\nNumber of border routers 13 \\nNumber of CPI routers 37 \\nNumber of ALSs 6  \\nNumber of trucks 25  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n95.2. Parameter settings \\nThe research object of this work is B2B e-commerce logistics, which \\nis characterized by a large and stable transportation volume. Therefore, \\nin the numerical study, there are no scattered small items, and the \\ntransportation objects are all based on containers. The standardization \\nof containers as PI containers is an essential concept in the PI. Similarly, \\nin the CPI, we define various standard sizes of physical packages to \\nstandardize the transportation objects. \\nBased on the above two ranges of routing scenarios, we use two \\ndifferent sets of environmental parameters in our experiments to simu-\\nlate B2B e-commerce logistics routing in CPI networks, as shown in \\nTable 2. The transport channels involved are obtained from the Baidu \\nMaps API. The cost and time metrics are set in the routing tables for \\nlarge-range routing and small-range routing. The number of transport \\nchannels, the number of border routers, the number of CPI routers, and \\nthe number of ALSs are intended to represent the size and complexity of \\nthe CPI networks and are used to evaluate the performance of the \\nrouting protocols within and between ALSs in the CPI network under \\ndifferent conditions. \\n5.3. Results \\nIn the small range, the time and cost in the routing table are used as \\nthe metrics for routing, and the transport routes are obtained. The first \\nhalf of Table 3 results from interior routing in ALS_Shenzhen, which \\nselects the transport routes from three factories, A, B, and C, to the CPI \\nborders K, L, and M in ALS_Shenzhen. The second half of Table 3 results \\nfrom interior routing in ALS_HongKong, which selects the transport \\nroutes from three CPI-borders K’, L’, and M’ to the wholesalers Q and R \\nin ALS_HongKong. Notably, the optimal interior routes are the same \\nregardless of whether time or cost is used as the metric in the routing \\ntable. The information on this part of the route contains the specific \\nroutes and CPI routers passed within the ALS, which is only used for \\ninterior routing and shared only among the CPI routers within the same \\nALS. After finding the optimal path in each ALS, each ALS routes ac-\\ncording to the exterior protocol based on the information from the \\nrouting table between its starting point and the border router, as well as \\nthe metric information for the optimal path in the ALS. In this process, \\nthe metrics for each ALS might be different, but in the exterior routing of \\nscenario 1, each ALS adopts a uniform metric type. Table 4 shows the \\noptimal paths from the three factories to the two wholesalers in the \\nwhole network obtained by using cost as the metric for the two ALSs. \\nSimilarly, Table 3 also shows the optimal paths with time as the metric. \\nIn the process of exterior routing, only the information on the starting \\npoint, the ending point, and the border routers is exchanged between \\nALSs, so the final outputs are the total distance from the factory to the \\nwholesaler and the total time and cost spent during the transportation \\nand transit process. \\nIn the case of the large range, the time and cost in the routing table \\nare used as metrics for routing, and no matter which factor is used as the \\nmetric, the results are still the same, as shown in Table 5. Two factories \\nlocated in the same ALS, factory 1 and factory 2, also have the same \\noptimal ALS path, while the optimal ALS paths of factories located in \\ndifferent ALSs may differ. This also reflects that the metric type has less \\nof an impact on the overall route selection due to the long distance in the \\nlarge range. Changes in the origin within the same ALS also have less of \\nan impact on overall route selection. \\n5.4. Sensitivity analysis \\nAfter examining the results in Section 5.3, we analyse the sensitivity \\nof the CPI routing protocols proposed in this work, based mainly on \\nsmall-range scenarios. \\nIn the first set of sensitivity analyses, the number of border CPI \\nrouters is set to 1, 2, and 3, and time and cost are used as evaluation \\nparameters. Fig. 6(a) and (b) show the results obtained with time as the \\nmetric in the routing table, and the total time shows a decreasing trend \\nas the number of border routers increases. This implies that increasing \\nthe number of border CPI routers can effectively reduce the time for \\ntransportation between different ALSs and, hence, the overall time, \\nwhich is crucial for CPI network performance. Fig. 6(c) and (d) show the \\nresults obtained with cost as the metric in the routing table. In this case, \\nthe cost decreases or stays the same as the number of border routers \\nincreases, while the time shows an increasing and decreasing trend. \\nOverall, increasing the number of border CPI routers positively impacts \\nthe optimization objective of routing (the metrics in the routing table) \\nand has a greater impact on time. It reduces the overall duration of the Table 3 \\nOptimal routes for ALS_Shenzhen and ALS_HongKong.  \\nALS_Shenzhen Origin Border Distance \\n(km) Time (h) Cost \\n(HKD) \\nA K 99 2.24 1317 \\nL 97 2.41 1306 \\nM 122 2.92 1511 \\nB K 85 2.26 1210 \\nL 75 1.94 1125 \\nM 100 2.45 1330 \\nC K 106 2.13 1368 \\nL 113 2.61 1434 \\nM 138 3.13 1639 \\nALS_HongKong Destination Border Distance \\n(km)  Time (h) Cost \\n(HKD) \\nQ K’ 43  1.73 1099 \\nL’ 33  1.31 959 \\nM’ 23  1.19 829 \\nR K’ 53  2.16 1239 \\nL’ 43  1.73 1099 \\nM’ 27  1.23 881  \\nTable 4 \\nOptimal routes in the small range (metric: *).  \\nTransport route Distance (km) Time (h) Cost* (HKD) Transport route Distance (km) Time* (h) Cost (HKD) \\nA-L-L’-Q 130  3.73 2265 A-L-L’-Q 130  3.73 2265 \\nA-M–M’-R 149  4.16 2392 A-L-L’-R 140  4.15 2405 \\nB-L-L’-Q 108  3.25 2084 B-L-L’-Q 108  3.25 2084 \\nB-M-M’-R 127  3.68 2211 B-L-L’-R 118  3.68 2224 \\nC-L-L’-Q 146  3.92 2393 C-K-K’-Q 149  3.86 2467 \\nC-M-M’-R 165  4.36 2520 C-K-K’-R 159  4.29 2607  Table 5 \\nOptimal routes in the large range.  \\nOD ALS_Path Distance (km) Time (h) Cost (HKD) \\n1-6 ALS1-ALS4-ALS5-ALS6 2144  34.08 22,906 \\n1-5 ALS1-ALS4-ALS5-ALS6 1898  31.00 19,708 \\n2-6 ALS1-ALS4-ALS5-ALS6 1969  31.89 21,506 \\n2-5 ALS1-ALS4-ALS5-ALS6 1723  28.81 18,308 \\n3-6 ALS2-ALS3-ALS5-ALS6 1549  31.12 19,788 \\n3-5 ALS2-ALS3-ALS5-ALS6 1364  22.45 14,386 \\n4-6 ALS2-ALS3-ALS5-ALS6 1610  25.52 17,584 \\n4-5 ALS2-ALS3-ALS5-ALS6 1795  27.83 19,064  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n10routing process and, in some cases, reduces the total cost. \\nIn the second set, the connectivity of the CPI network is reduced by \\ndecreasing the number of CPI routers inside the ALS in the small-range \\nscenario. The CPI router F in ALS_Shenzhen is removed first. Then, we \\nremove CPI router F from ALS_Shenzhen and CPI router P from \\nALS_HongKong at the same time. The results are shown in Fig. 7. For \\nmost routes, the reduction in the number of CPI routers increases the \\ntotal transportation time and cost, and the greater the reduction in the \\nnumber of CPI routers is, the greater the increases in time and cost. \\nFewer CPI routers mean fewer transit options, resulting in increased \\ntime and cost. Orders are affected by this change to different degrees, \\ndepending on the distances between nodes and the route choice. \\nThe two experiments above are based on one principle: the metrics in \\nthe routing table of all ALSs are the same, namely, all are cost or time. \\nThe information provided by the routing tables of different ALSs may \\ndiffer; hence, the third set of sensitivity analyses sets the metrics in the \\nrouting tables of two ALSs to different parameters according to the other \\ncharacteristics of the ALSs. In the small range, the metric for ALS_Shenzhen is set to time, that for ALS_HongKong is set to cost, and \\nthen the metrics for the two ALSs are exchanged. The results of the \\ncomparison between the two cases are shown in Fig. 8. In most of the \\nroutes when the metric is mixed, the cost and time are greater than in the \\nscenarios with a single metric, but there are also routes where the cost or \\ntime is reduced in the mixed metric. \\nThe sensitivity analyses described above are based on the principle \\nthat the metrics in the routing tables of all ALSs are the same, namely, \\neither cost or time. Therefore, a third set of sensitivity analyses are \\nconducted to further explore the impact of different metrics on the re-\\nsults. In this set, the routing table metric of ALS_Shenzhen is set to time, \\nand the routing table metric of ALS_HongKong is set to cost based on the \\nother characteristics of the ALSs; then, the metric settings of the two \\nALSs are exchanged. By comparing the results of these two scenarios, it \\nis found that in most of the routing scenarios with mixed metrics, both \\nthe cost and time are greater than those in the single metric scenario. \\nHowever, the cost or time is lower than that of the original result in a few \\nmixed-metric routings. This experiment provides insight into the impact \\nFig. 6.Comparison of the time and cost for different numbers of border routers.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n11of different metrics on ALS performance and helps us understand the \\ntradeoff between cost and time in routing. In practice, choosing the \\ncorrect metric settings based on specific situations and priorities is \\ncrucial for ALS performance optimization. This will help improve the \\nefficiency and effectiveness of CPI networks and achieve better logistics \\noperations. \\n5.5. Implementation insights \\nThis numerical study aims to investigate the feasibility of utilizing \\nthe framework and routing protocols of CPI networks in B2B e-com -\\nmerce logistics. To achieve this goal, we applied interior and exterior \\nrouting protocols in Section 4. We conducted a series of experiments in a \\nsimulated CPI network environment based on the network framework in \\nSection 3. In the experiments, both small-range and large-range sce-\\nnarios were simulated, both of which included CPI routers, ALSs, routing \\ntables, and other essential components of CPI networks. Through the \\nnumerical study, several important conclusions and insights were ob-\\ntained. First, we found that the choice of routing table metric is signif -\\nicant. Cost and time are used as the primary metrics for routing tables. In most cases, routing schemes with mixed metrics increase cost and time. \\nThis suggests that choosing metrics based on specific requirements and \\npriorities is crucial for optimizing the performance of CPI networks. \\nSecond, in terms of network connectivity, increasing the number of \\nborder routers can effectively reduce the logistics transportation time, as \\nborder routers provide more route options, thereby reducing the overall \\ntransportation time. Therefore, the number of border routers should be \\nappropriately increased in practical applications to improve the effi-\\nciency and performance of CPI networks based on factors such as lo-\\ngistics demand and the diversity of route choices. Finally, reducing the \\nnumber of CPI routers within an ALS may increase the time and cost of \\nlogistics transportation. Consequently, it is necessary to ensure the \\nconnectivity of CPI networks to better choose logistics routes and \\nimprove overall performance. \\nBased on the analysis above, we conclude that the results of our \\nexperiment contribute to engineering and academics in the following \\nways: In terms of engineering, the experiment simulated CPI networks of \\ndifferent sizes and complexities by setting up different numbers of \\ntransport channels, border routers, CPI routers and ALSs. The results \\nprovide an important reference and guidance for scenario-specific CPI \\nFig. 7.Comparison of time and cost for different numbers of CPI routers.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n12network construction. In the experiments, the communication between \\nCPI routers in the cyber layer and the decision-making based on the \\nrouting table according to the protocols were almost real-time, enabling \\nthe logistics network to operate efficiently. The experimental results \\nshow that when routing within an ALS, different routing protocols can \\nmake independent decisions and select the optimal internal path ac-\\ncording to their own needs and conditions. In terms of engineering, by \\nstudying the performance and results of routing protocols under \\ndifferent CPI network sizes and complexities, we gained insight into the \\ninfluence of CPI network elements on routing performance, thus \\nproviding theoretical foundations and methods for the optimization and \\nimprovement of CPI networks. The strategy of information exchange \\nand decision-making in CPI networks through routing tables in the cyber \\nlayer can support the design and implementation of logistics systems in \\nthe context of Industry 4.0. Future research into independent routing \\ndecision methods and strategies within and among ALSs could enable \\nfurther optimization of the framing and routing performance of CPI \\nnetworks. \\n6.Conclusions and future works \\nThe growing prosperity of B2B e-commerce has led to a significant \\nincrease in the number of packages delivered, thus putting more sig-\\nnificant pressure on the logistics network. This situation increases the \\ndemand for efficient and economical package routing, which is difficult \\nto satisfy with the current traditional fragmented logistics networks and \\nrouting approaches. Therefore, there is an urgent need to adopt a new \\nframework to integrate logistics networks and design routing methods \\nfor B2B e-commerce logistics. In this work, we propose a framework for \\nCPI networks based on computer networks that reintegrates some \\ncomponents of B2B e-commerce logistics networks, and we propose \\nrouting protocols within this framework to realize efficient and \\neconomical routing of physical packages. First, the overall framework of \\nB2B e-commerce logistics in CPI networks and its main operational \\nprocesses at the cyber and physical levels are proposed. Due to the large \\nscale of B2B e-commerce logistics, we propose the concept of autono -\\nmous logistics systems (ALSs) in networks. Second, the key components \\nof the routing process, logistics nodes, and routing information are in-\\ntegrated into two crucial components in the CPI networks, i.e., the CPI \\nrouter and the routing table. The optimization objectives in the routing \\nprocess are integrated as metrics in the routing table. Routing protocols within and among ALSs are proposed for making routing decisions based \\non information in the routing table for efficient and economical routing. \\nFinally, the CPI network framework and routing protocols are simulated \\nthrough two B2B e-commerce logistics scenarios, where cost and time \\nare selected as metrics in the routing table to determine the transport \\nroutes. The sensitivity analysis revealed that increasing the number of \\nborder routers and the network connectivity could effectively improve \\nthe efficiency and performance of the CPI networks. The division of ALSs \\nin the networks enables CPI routers to make timely routing decisions \\neven when some routing information is missing by using a mixed metric \\nto ensure the standard routing of physical packages. \\nThis work has significant implications for both academic and engi-\\nneering aspects of B2B e-commerce logistics. Academically, it provides a \\nCPI-driven solution to the routing problem of B2B e-commerce logistics. \\nBy using routing protocols within and among ALSs, routing can be \\nachieved, thus improving the efficiency and performance of logistics \\nsystems. These results provide necessary empirical support for the \\ntheoretical study of logistics networks and offer new ideas and directions \\nfor future research. In engineering, this research provides important \\nguidance for the design, planning, and implementation of B2B e-com -\\nmerce logistics networks in real life. More efficient and economical \\nrouting is possible by selecting appropriate metrics in routing tables, \\noptimizing routing protocols within and among ALSs, ensuring infor-\\nmation exchange, and rationally allocating computational resources. \\nThis study provides support for the continuous improvement of B2B e- \\ncommerce logistics networks. These findings may improve the efficiency \\nand performance of B2B e-commerce logistics networks and promote the \\nsustainable development of the logistics industry, which is of great \\npractical significance for realizing smart logistics and modernized lo-\\ngistics systems in Industry 4.0. \\nAs the concept of CPI is still in the development stage, this paper \\nproposes a generalized framework model, which still has some limita -\\ntions at the theoretical and methodological levels, mainly as follows. \\nFirst, this work considers only cost and time as metrics in the routing \\ntable and does not set constraints on logistics nodes or routes, such as \\ncapacity constraints. Second, the B2B e-commerce logistics scenarios in \\nthis work are point-to-point, and the CPI router at the intermediate \\nnodes is only responsible for providing the route information and for-\\nwarding the routing decisions. There is no consolidation or distribution \\nof the physical packages. Third, the numerical study conducted in this \\nresearch was limited in scale; hence, its results cannot prove the \\nFig. 8.Comparison of time and cost when the metric is mixed.  X. Qu et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \\nComputers & Industrial Engineering 193 (2024) 110293\\n13computing times of the proposed routing protocols in some large scale \\nscenario. \\nHowever, with the continuous development of CPI theory and \\ntechnology, the problem of CPI routing has many directions for further \\nresearch. Our future research will change the point-to-point scenarios \\ninto scenarios that include consolidation and distribution so that the CPI \\nrouters at the intermediate nodes not only forward the routing infor-\\nmation but also participate in the routing decision for the next hop. \\nMoreover, we will consider the routing dynamics, introduce the time \\nfactor to the routing protocols, and update the routing table periodically \\nin future works. Additionally, since ALSs represent different logistics \\nservice providers (LSPs), routing among ALSs may involve competition. \\nTherefore, further research will consider the pricing, time windows, and \\ncapacities of different ALSs and introduce auctions into the routing \\nprotocol among ALSs. \\nCRediT authorship contribution statement \\nXinye Qu: Writing – original draft, Validation, Software, Method -\\nology, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. Ming Li: Writing – review & \\nediting, Supervision, Project administration, Methodology, Funding \\nacquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. Zhiyuan Ouyang: \\nMethodology, Formal analysis. Chung-lam Ng: Software, Formal anal-\\nysis. George Q. Huang: Writing – review & editing, Supervision, Re-\\nsources, Project administration, Funding acquisition. \\nDeclaration of competing interest \\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial \\ninterests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence \\nthe work reported in this paper. \\nData availability \\nData will be made available on request. \\nAcknowledgements \\nThis research is supported by the Hong Kong RGC Theme-based \\nResearch Scheme (No. T32-707/22-N) the Departmental General \\nResearch Fund of PolyU, Hong Kong (No. P0045748) and the Hong Kong \\nRGC Research Impact Fund (No. R7036-22). \\nReferences \\nAit Ben Mouh, L., Ouhda, M., El Mourabit, Y., & Baslam, M. (2023). A Hybrid Approach \\nof Dijkstra ’s Algorithm and A* Search, with an Optional Adaptive Threshold \\nHeuristic. In El Ayachi, R., Fakir, M., & Baslam, M. (Eds.), Business Intelligence (Vol. \\n484, pp. 117–133). Springer Nature Switzerland. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3- \\n031-37872-0_9 . \\nBallot, E., Gobet, O., & Montreuil, B. (2012). Physical internet enabled open hub network \\ndesign for distributed networked operations. Service orientation in holonic and multi- \\nagent manufacturing control , 279–292. \\nBallot, E., Montreuil, B., & Meller, R. D. (2014). The physical internet . La Documentation \\nFrançaise .  \\nBriand, M., Franklin, R., & Lafkihi, M. (2022). A dynamic routing protocol with payments \\nfor the Physical Internet: A simulation with learning agents. Transportation Research \\nPart E: Logistics and Transportation Review, 166, Article 102905. https://doi.org/ \\n10.1016/j.tre.2022.102905 \\nCarbone, V., & Stone, M. A. (2005). Growth and relational strategies used by the \\nEuropean logistics service providers: Rationale and outcomes. Transportation \\nResearch Part E: Logistics and Transportation Review, 41(6), 495–510. \\nCrainic, T. G., & Montreuil, B. (2016). 8 Physical Internet Enabled Hyperconnected City \\nLogistics. Transportation Research Procedia, 12, 383–398. \\nDong, C., & Franklin, R. (2021). From the digital Internet to the physical Internet: A \\nconceptual framework with a stylized network model. Journal of Business Logistics, 42 \\n(1), 108–119. \\nFazili, M., Venkatadri, U., Cyrus, P., & Tajbakhsh, M. (2017). Physical Interne